WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.673
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:02.673 --> 00:00:04.950
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:05.943
for everyday life.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:10.410
I'm Andrew Huberman,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.170
and I'm a professor of
neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.970
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.610
Today my guest is Dr. Peter Attia.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.950
Dr. Attia is a physician who's focused on

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.080
nutritional, supplementation-based,

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.410
behavioral, prescription
drug, and other interventions

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.960
that promote health span and lifespan.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.260
His expertise spans
from exercise physiology

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.730
to sleep physiology,

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.730
emotional and mental
health and pharmacology.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.980
Today we talk about all
those areas of health,

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.480
starting with the very basics,

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.930
such as how to evaluate
one's own health status

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.840
and how to define one's health trajectory.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.330
We also talk about the
various sorts of interventions

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.690
that one can take in order
to optimize vitality,

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:55.110
while also extending
longevity, that is lifespan.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:58.080
Dr. Attia's uniquely qualified to focus on

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.290
the complete depth and breadth
of topics that we cover.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.540
And indeed, these are the
same topics that he works with

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.210
his patients on in his clinic every day.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.980
Dr. Attia earned his Bachelor of Science

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.410
in mechanical engineering
and applied mathematics,

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:14.100
and his MD from Stanford
University School of Medicine.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.590
He then went on to train
at Johns Hopkins Hospital

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:17.520
in general surgery,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.620
one of the premier hospitals in the world,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.900
where he was the recipient of
several prestigious awards,

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.700
including Resident of the Year.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.620
He's been an author on
comprehensive reviews

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:26.880
of general surgery.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.670
He spent two years at the
National Institute of Health

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.590
as a surgical oncology fellow,

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.510
at the National Cancer Institute,

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:34.590
where his work focused on

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.780
immune-based therapies for melanoma.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.100
In the fields of science and medicine,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.140
it is well understood that
we are much the product

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.810
of our mentors and the
mentoring we receive.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.850
Dr. Attia has trained
with some of the best

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.400
and most innovative
lipidologists, endocrinologists,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.550
gynecologists, sleep physiologists
and longevity scientists

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.560
in the United States and Canada.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.630
So the expertise that funnels through him

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.133
and that he shares with us today

00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:58.770
is really harnessed from
the best of the best,

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.110
and his extensive training and expertise.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.280
By the end of today's episode,

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.060
you will have answers to
important basic questions such as

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.170
should you have blood work?

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.090
How often should you do blood work?

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.980
What specific things
should you be looking for

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.650
on that blood work, that
are either counterintuitive

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.360
or not often discussed,

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.600
and yet that immediately
and in the long-term

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.970
influence your lifespan and health span?

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.490
We talk about hormone
health and hormone therapies

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.170
for both men and women.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.830
We talk about drug therapies
that can influence the mind

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.000
as well as the body.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.160
And of course, we talk about
supplementation, nutrition,

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.880
exercise, and predictors of
lifespan and health span.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.085
It is an episode rich with information.

00:02:38.085 --> 00:02:41.280
For some of you, you may want
to get out a pen and paper

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.630
in order to take notes.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.840
For others of you that learn
better simply by listening,

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.730
I just want to remind you
that we have timestamped

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:48.563
all this information

00:02:48.563 --> 00:02:50.550
so that you can go back
to the specific topics

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.350
most of interest to you.

