00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:29.670
And now for my discussion
with Dr. Peter Attia.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.260
Peter, thanks for joining me today.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:32.659
- Thanks for having me, man.

00:07:32.659 --> 00:07:34.830
- I've been looking forward
to this for a very long time.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:35.663
- [Peter] As have I.

00:07:35.663 --> 00:07:37.290
- I'm a huge fan of your podcast.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.150
I know that you went to Stanford

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.380
and worked with a number of people

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:41.610
that are colleagues of mine.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:45.570
So for me, this is already a
thrill, just to be doing this.

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.520
- Yeah, well, it's likewise.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:48.810
- I have a ton of questions,

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:50.193
but I want to start off with something

00:07:50.193 --> 00:07:51.750
that I wonder a lot about,

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:55.710
and that I know many other
people wonder about, which is

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:59.970
how to assess their current
health and their trajectory

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.743
in terms of health and wellbeing,

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.580
specifically as it relates to blood work.

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:05.910
So what are your thoughts on blood work?

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:08.910
Is it necessary for the typical person?

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.290
So this is somebody who's not dealing with

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:12.750
some acute syndrome or illness.

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:14.850
And at what age would you suggest

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:16.080
people start getting blood work?

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.540
How frequently should they get blood work?

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.630
How often do you get blood
work done, et cetera?

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:22.632
- Yeah, there's a lot there.

00:08:22.632 --> 00:08:24.960
I mean, the way I talk
about this with patients is,

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:27.420
first, taking everything
back to the objective.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:31.920
So what's the thing
we're trying to optimize?

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:34.650
So if a person says, look,

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:38.040
I'm trying to break 10
hours for an Ironman,

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:39.840
I don't know that blood
work is going to be

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.867
a game-changing aspect of their trajectory

00:08:42.867 --> 00:08:44.130
and their training.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.780
You know, they're going
to benefit much more

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.810
from sort of functional
analyses of performance.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.390
So I'm assuming, based on the question,

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.850
that you're really coming
at this through the lens of

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:55.590
living longer and living better

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:58.044
through the lifespan health span

00:08:58.044 --> 00:08:59.217
lens?
- Mostly, yeah.

00:08:59.217 --> 00:09:02.640
I think most people have
some sense of their vitality

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.130
or lack of vitality, but
I think everyone wonders

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:06.330
whether or not they could feel better,

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:08.460
and whether or not blood work
will give them a window into

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:11.070
how they might go about feeling better.

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:12.960
- Yeah, I think it does to some extent,

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.330
but I also think that it
has a lot of blind spots.

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:19.320
So I kind of break things down into

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.480
the two vectors that make up longevity,

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:23.610
which are lifespan and health span.

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.670
So lifespan is the easiest of
those vectors to understand,

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.260
because it's pretty binary.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:29.490
You're alive or you're not alive.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:30.600
You're respiring or you're not.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:33.120
You make ATP or you don't, end of story.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:37.140
So what gets in the way of lifespan

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:40.980
is essentially the four
horsemen of disease, right?

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.500
So atherosclerotic disease, cancer,

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.140
neurodegenerative disease,
and metabolic disease,

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:49.170
which directly isn't the
cause of many deaths,

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:52.050
but basically creates the foundation

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:53.600
to all of those other diseases.

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:57.300
So, you know, if you're a non-smoker,

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:01.080
what I just rattled off is
about 80% of your death.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:05.100
So how does blood work help address those?

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:05.933
It varies.

00:10:05.933 --> 00:10:07.650
So on the atherosclerotic standpoint,

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:09.660
it's a very good predictor of risk,

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:11.640
if you know what to look for.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.410
So primarily ApoB would be

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:17.250
the single most important
lipoprotein that we care about.

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:19.470
I can explain what that means in a second.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:22.110
And then also, other
markers of inflammation,

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:25.620
endothelial health, and metabolic health.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:27.090
When it comes to cancer, you know,

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:29.520
blood testing in the sense of biomarkers

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:30.600
is not particularly helpful,

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:35.220
outside of knowing that the
second leading environmental

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:36.930
or modifiable cause of cancer

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:38.700
is metabolic ill health, after smoking.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.850
So we don't actually
know a lot about cancer

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:43.890
in the sense of what causes it.

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:46.500
It's really stochastic,
and it's a lot of bad luck.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.560
So we know that smoking
drives it, and we know that,

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:52.613
even though epidemiologically
we say obesity drives it,

00:10:52.613 --> 00:10:54.900
what it really means is
metabolic poor health.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:56.580
It's probably the hyperinsulinemia

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.740
that comes with obesity that drives it.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:00.840
So biomarkers help with that,

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.880
but there's still an enormous
blind spot to cancer.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:04.680
We could talk about liquid biopsies aside,

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:06.570
'cause those aren't
really biomarker studies,

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.370
but put that away.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.680
On the neurodegenerative side, you know,

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:12.150
I don't think we have a lot of insight

00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:14.640
that comes to understanding
Parkinson's disease,

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:15.840
but when it comes to dementia,

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.160
particularly Alzheimer's disease,

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:18.750
which is the most
prevalent form of dementia,

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.940
I think the biomarkers
can be quite helpful.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:23.430
They overlap a lot with the
atherosclerotic diseases.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:25.500
So the same things that

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:26.550
drive the risk of heart disease

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:28.653
are driving the risk of dementia.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.020
And then there's some novel stuff as well,

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.370
if you include genetic testing,

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:33.960
which you can get out of a blood test.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.780
We get a whole suite of
genes, not just APOE,

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.150
but far more nuanced stuff than that,

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:40.680
that can also play a role.

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:43.130
So you can stratify risk in that sense.

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:45.420
So in aggregate, I would say, you know,

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:46.930
blood testing of biomarkers

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:51.750
provides pretty good
insight into lifespan.

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:53.220
When you get into health
span, you have kind of

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:56.760
the cognitive, physical,
emotional domains.

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:59.430
I think here the biomarkers
are far less helpful,

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:03.210
and here we kind of rely
more on functional testing.

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:06.300
So when it comes to sort of
the cognitive piece, you know,

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.280
you can do cognitive testing.

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:09.922
In terms of long-term risk,

00:12:09.922 --> 00:12:11.010
a lot of the things that imply

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:13.473
good cognitive health as you age,

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:16.260
are in line with the same things

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:19.560
that you would do to reduce
the risk of dementia.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:22.770
So all the biomarkers that
you would look to improve

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:24.150
through dementia risk reduction,

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:26.700
you would be improving
through cognitive health.

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:27.690
On the physical side,

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:29.400
I mean, outside of looking
at hormone levels and things

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:31.020
which we look at extensively,

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:34.140
and understanding how those might aid in

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:38.670
or prevent some of the metrics
that matter, it really is,

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:40.140
this is a biomarker aside thing.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:42.660
I mean, I'd be much more
interested in a person's DEXA,

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:45.660
CPET testing, VO2 max testing, you know,

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.720
zone two lactate testing, fat oxidation.

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:51.060
Those are what I would
consider more functional tests

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.612
that give me far more insight into that.

00:12:53.612 --> 00:12:54.570
And then of course the emotional piece,

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:56.910
which depending on who you are,

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.350
might be the single most important piece,

00:12:58.350 --> 00:12:59.940
without which none of this
other stuff matters, right?

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:01.950
If you're a totally miserable human being,

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:03.810
your relationships suck,

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:05.460
I don't think any of
this other stuff matters.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:07.800
And certainly there's
nothing that I'm looking at

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:10.800
in biomarkers that's giving
me great insight into that.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.480
- Do you ask about emotional state,

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.640
or do you try and assess
emotional state indirectly

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.040
when you do an intake
with one of your patients?

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:19.410
- Probably not so much in the intake,

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:21.660
because I think it takes a
while to form a relationship

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:24.420
with a patient before
that starts to become

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:26.670
something that they're
necessarily going to want to

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:27.503
talk with you about.

00:13:27.503 --> 00:13:30.750
But I definitely think of
it as an important part

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:31.770
of what we do.

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:33.810
And I think without it,

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:35.580
none of this other stuff really matters.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:38.220
Again, the irony of thinking about

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.920
how many years I spent
sort of in pursuit of

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.620
fully optimizing every
detail of everything,

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:46.590
without any attention being
paid to that dimension,

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:48.030
is not lost on me.

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:50.670
And look, there are some patients who,

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:51.780
that's just not something that,

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:53.250
that's something that's compartmentalized.

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:54.150
Maybe they're, you know,

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:55.680
they're doing well in that
department, or maybe they aren't,

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:58.830
but they just aren't willing
to engage on that yet.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:00.810
- In terms of frequency of blood testing,

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:03.240
if somebody feels pretty good

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:06.360
and is taking a number of
steps, exercise, nutrition,

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:10.200
et cetera, to try and extend lifespan

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:12.003
and improve health span,

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:16.740
is once a year frequent enough,

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:20.040
and should a 20-year-old
start getting blood work done

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:21.750
just to get a window into what's going on?

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:22.830
Assuming that they can afford it

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:24.240
or their insurance can cover it.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:26.250
- Yeah, I mean, look, I
certainly think everybody

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:28.590
should be screened early in
life because if you look at,

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:32.040
like, what's the single most
prevalent genetic driver

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:34.530
of atherosclerosis, is Lp[a].

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:36.090
So unfortunately most
physicians don't know

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:37.080
what Lp[a] is,

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:40.020
and yet somewhere between
eight and 12% of the population

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:43.200
has a high enough, and
depending on who you, you know,

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:44.640
I had a recent guest on my podcast

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:46.770
who suggested it could be as high as 20%,

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:48.120
have a high enough Lp[a],

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:51.090
that it is contributing
to atherosclerosis.

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:52.920
So to not want to know that,

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:54.300
when it's genetically determined, right,

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:56.700
this is something that,
you're born with this

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:59.250
and you only need to really check it once,

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.500
why we wouldn't want to
know that in a 20-year-old,

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.980
when it can contribute to a lot
of the early atherosclerosis

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:06.213
we see in people.

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:10.410
It's leaving money on
the table, in my opinion.

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:11.970
The frequency with which you need to test

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:16.140
really comes down to the
state of interventions.

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:18.090
I don't think it makes
sense to just do blood tests

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:19.200
for the sake of doing blood tests.

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:20.400
There has to be kind of a reason.

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:22.350
Is something changing?

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:24.180
You know, a blood test
is, for the most part,

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:26.307
a static intervention.

00:15:26.307 --> 00:15:28.830
It's a look at a window in time.

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:31.890
And there's benefit in having

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:34.440
a few of those over the course of a year,

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:36.180
if you're unsure about a level.

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:38.790
So if something comes back and
it doesn't look great, yeah,

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:40.200
it might make sense just to recheck it

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:41.790
without reacting to it.

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:43.320
But typically, you know, in patients,

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:45.570
we might check blood two
to four times a year,

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:49.260
but we're also probably doing
things in there to now check,

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:52.170
like hey, you know, we gave this drug,

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:53.770
did it have the desired outcome?

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:56.640
You put on three pounds of muscle

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:57.930
and lost three pounds of fat,

00:15:57.930 --> 00:15:59.530
did it have the desired outcome?

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:05.160
- Speaking of tracking weight and fat,

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:08.010
lean mass percentages, is that
something that you recommend

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:09.480
your patients do pretty often?

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:11.010
I know people that step
on the scale every day,

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:13.063
I know people like myself that frankly,

00:16:13.063 --> 00:16:14.730
I might step on the
scale three times a year.

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:16.290
I don't really care.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:17.640
I pay attention to other things

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:19.620
that are far more subjective.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:21.150
Maybe I'm making a huge mistake.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:22.920
What are your thoughts about

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:27.237
quantitative measurements of weight, BMI,

00:16:27.237 --> 00:16:29.340
for the typical person?

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.770
- I think they're pretty crude.

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:36.063
I think a DEXA, I'd rather
take a DEXA annually,

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:40.410
and then maybe follow weight
a little bit more closely

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:41.430
to get a sense of it.

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:43.950
And so with a DEXA you're getting,

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:45.480
at least the way we look at the data,

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:47.580
four pieces of information.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:49.560
Now, most people when they do a DEXA,

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:51.200
should I explain what that is, I'm?

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:53.250
- Yeah, I think some people
might not know what DEXA is.

00:16:53.250 --> 00:16:55.710
In fact, I confess, I
have a crude understanding

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:56.543
of what it is.

00:16:57.690 --> 00:16:58.800
Tell me where I'm wrong,

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:02.070
and hopefully where I'm
at least partially right.

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:02.903
My understanding is

00:17:02.903 --> 00:17:04.320
that there are a number of
different ways to measure

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:07.110
lean mass to non-lean mass ratio,

00:17:07.110 --> 00:17:09.570
and there's one where
they put you underwater.

00:17:09.570 --> 00:17:11.280
There's one where they
put you into some sort of

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:12.930
non-underwater chamber.

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:14.400
There's calipering.

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:17.760
And then there's the looking
in the mirror and pinching

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:19.383
and changing the lighting.

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:22.050
- You know, it's funny,

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:23.460
if you've done it enough, you can,

00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:26.730
I can sort of tell my
body fat by my abs, right.

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:28.440
So I can sort of tell by, you know,

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:30.720
how good the six pack or
how bad the six pack is,

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:31.797
what the leanness is.

00:17:31.797 --> 00:17:34.350
And that's actually not
a terrible way to do it.

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:37.020
A bodybuilder, for example,
which I've never been,

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:41.460
can tell you the difference
between being 6%, 7%, 8%, 10%,

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:46.460
just based on the degree of
visibility within the abs.

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:50.700
But basically, a DEXA scan is an x-ray.

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:53.730
So it's the same principle as
just getting a chest x-ray,

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:56.580
where ionizing radiation
is passed through the body.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:58.080
And there's a plate behind the body

00:17:58.080 --> 00:17:59.820
that collects what comes through.

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:02.730
And the denser the medium

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:05.430
that the electrons are
trying to go through,

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:06.870
the less of them that are collected.

00:18:06.870 --> 00:18:08.040
So when you look at an x-ray,

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:09.750
as everybody's probably seen an x-ray,

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:11.790
that which is white is most dense.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:14.280
So if you had, you know, a
piece of metal in your pocket,

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:16.740
it would show up as a bright white thing.

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:19.560
That's why ribs and
bones show up as white,

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.800
and the things that are the
least dense, like the lungs,

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:25.320
where it's just air, are the blackest.

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:27.600
And everything is a
shade of gray in between.

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:30.540
So a DEXA is just doing that effectively,

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:31.800
but it's a moving x-ray.

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:34.860
So you lay down on a bed and
it takes maybe 10 minutes,

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:37.350
and this little, very low power x-ray

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:39.180
kind of goes over your body.

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:43.440
And the plate beneath it
is collecting information

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:45.930
that is basically allowing
it to differentiate

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:50.930
between three things, bone
mineral content, fat, other.

00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:55.440
And the other is quantified
as lean body mass.

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:58.590
So that's organs,
muscles, everything else.

00:18:58.590 --> 00:19:01.650
So when most people do a DEXA,
they get the report back,

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:02.610
and the reports are horrible.

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:04.863
I've yet to see one company
that can do this in a way

00:19:04.863 --> 00:19:06.780
that isn't abjectly horrible.

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:08.280
We've created our own templates,

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:09.990
so we have our own dashboard
for how we do this,

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:12.390
'cause we've just given up
on trying to use theirs.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:13.650
But the first thing most people look at is

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:14.970
what's my body fat.

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:16.710
And this is the gold standard

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:18.450
outside of like MRI or something,

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:20.040
that's only used for research purposes.

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:23.040
So a DEXA is going to
produce a far better estimate

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:27.510
of body fat, than calipers
or buoyancy testing,

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:28.770
or things like that,

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:31.170
provided the machinery is well-calibrated,

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:33.020
and the operator knows how to use it.

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:37.830
I've heard some people argue
that in the hands of like

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:40.620
the guy who's been doing
calipers his whole life,

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:42.480
it could probably be
comparable with calipers.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:45.660
But nevertheless, for
an off-the-shelf tech,

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:46.713
DEXA is amazing.

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:50.610
Of the four things that
get spit out of the DEXA,

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:53.267
we think that the body fat
is the least interesting.

00:19:53.267 --> 00:19:56.460
And so I would rank that
as fourth on the list of

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:58.050
what's germane to your health.

00:19:58.050 --> 00:19:59.940
The other three things
that you get spit out

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:03.990
are bone mineral density, visceral fat,

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:06.810
and then the metrics that
allow you to compute,

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:09.630
like to basically compute what's called

00:20:09.630 --> 00:20:12.930
appendicular lean mass index
and fat-free mass index.

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:16.110
And so those three metrics are
significantly more important

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:17.460
than body fat.

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:19.470
And the reason is as follows, right?

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:22.890
So, bone mineral density
basically speaks to your risk

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:26.310
of osteoporosis and osteopenia.

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:28.470
And that doesn't sound very sexy

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:30.210
to people our age, you know.

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:31.800
50-year-old guys listening to this,

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:32.760
it's like, yeah, big deal.

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:35.850
But for a 50-year-old woman,
this is a huge deal, right?

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:37.680
A woman who's just about
to go through menopause,

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:39.330
or has just gone through menopause,

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:41.730
is at an enormous risk for osteopenia,

00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:43.920
and then ultimately osteoporosis,

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:47.190
because estrogen is the
single most important hormone

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:50.100
in regulating bone mineral density.

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:51.990
And we can come back and talk
about why that's the case,

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:53.674
but it's very interesting

00:20:53.674 --> 00:20:57.150
how the biomechanics of bones work,

00:20:57.150 --> 00:21:00.300
and why estrogen
specifically is so important.

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:02.720
And this is a huge cause
of morbidity, right?

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:05.130
So, you know, if you're over the age of 65

00:21:05.130 --> 00:21:07.470
and you fall and break your hip,

00:21:07.470 --> 00:21:12.470
your one year morbidity
is about 30 to 40%.

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:14.760
Which again, just to put that in English,

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:17.130
if you're 65 or older, you
fall and break your hip,

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:20.400
there's a 30 to 40% chance
you're dead in a year.

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:21.233
- [Andrew] Wow.

00:21:21.233 --> 00:21:22.066
- Bones matter.

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:27.930
So we want to really get a
sense of where you stack up

00:21:27.930 --> 00:21:30.150
for your age, for your sex.

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:33.360
And if you're anywhere off the pace,

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:36.210
we have to ramp up our strategy
and be super aggressive

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:39.480
about how to increase
that, or at a minimum,

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:41.400
prevent any further decay.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:43.020
- And are there age-related charts

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:44.040
for these sorts of things?

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:47.160
- Yeah, this all gets spit out
into what's called a Z-score.

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:49.470
So when you're looking at your BMD,

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:50.580
it's going to give you a Z-score.

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:54.210
So a Z-score of zero means,
and you understand this,

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:55.770
but it's like, a Z-score referring to

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:57.720
a probability distribution
in a standard mode.

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:00.480
So Z-score of zero means
you're at the 50th percentile

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:01.470
for your age and sex.

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:04.200
A Z-score of plus one, you're
one standard deviation above,

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:06.300
minus one, below, et cetera.

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:08.820
There's also a T-score, which
is doing the same thing,

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:11.397
but comparing you to a young person.

00:22:11.397 --> 00:22:13.350
And so the T-score is technically used

00:22:13.350 --> 00:22:15.810
to make the diagnosis of
osteopenia or osteoporosis.

00:22:15.810 --> 00:22:18.180
We tend to look more at the
Z-score, and basically say,

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:21.510
look, if your Z-score
right now is minus one,

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:24.150
in four years I want
your Z-score to be zero.

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:27.450
Not necessarily because you've
increased that entire way,

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:29.160
but maybe you've increased slightly

00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:31.980
while it's expected that
you would've declined.

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:32.813
- I see.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:36.060
What are some things
that we can do to improve

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:38.910
bone mineral density at any age?

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:41.430
- So it turns out there's
a real critical window

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:43.110
in which we are malleable.

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:45.570
So depending on the age at
which someone's listening to us

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:50.400
discuss this, you know,
if you're under 20, 25,

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:52.800
you are still in that time of your life

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:55.560
when you are able to reach your potential.

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:58.620
So it turns out that strength training

00:22:58.620 --> 00:23:02.280
is probably the single
best thing you can do.

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:04.520
And this was a surprise to
me, 'cause we, you know,

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:07.380
we did an AMA on this
topic a little while ago,

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:09.450
and that's when I got really deep on this

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:10.740
with our analysts.

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:13.110
My assumption was
running must be the best,

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:15.097
like some sort of impact must
be the best thing you can do.

00:23:15.097 --> 00:23:16.950
You know, I assumed
running would be better

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:18.660
than swimming and cycling.

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:20.130
But it turned out that power lifting

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:21.870
was probably the best thing you could do.

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:25.200
And I think once you
understand how bones work,

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:27.480
it became more clear, which is, you know,

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:30.450
power lifting is really
putting more of a sheer force

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:33.120
from the muscle via the
tendon onto the bone,

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.040
and that's what the
bones are really sensing.

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:36.630
They're sensing that sheer force

00:23:36.630 --> 00:23:39.750
that's being applied through
the bone, in a compressive way,

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:41.430
depending on the bone of course.

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:44.310
And that's what's basically
activating the osteoblasts,

00:23:44.310 --> 00:23:49.310
which are the cells that are
allowing bone to be built.

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:54.873
So this turns out to be probably
more important for females,

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:59.310
because how high you can get

00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:02.640
during that period of development,
say till you're 20 or 25,

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:05.670
basically sets your trajectory
for the rest of your life.

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:08.520
So where we get into real
trouble is with patients who,

00:24:08.520 --> 00:24:12.240
for example, used large
amounts of inhaled steroids

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:13.170
during that period of their life,

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:15.360
'cause let's say they
had really bad asthma.

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:18.240
Or patients who needed large
amounts of corticosteroids

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:20.280
for some other immune-related condition.

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:22.170
So during their critical
window of development,

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:25.680
they were taking a drug that
was impairing this process.

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:27.330
So, you know, we have
some patients like that

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:30.270
in our practice and that's
just an enormous liability

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:31.890
that we're working
really hard to overcome,

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:35.943
with nutrition, with hormones,
with drugs, with training.

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:41.040
And, you know, it's just
something you have to be aware of.

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:45.660
- I wasn't aware that inhalants for asthma

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:48.530
and things of that sort can
impair bone mineral density.

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:50.100
- If they're steroid-based.

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:51.810
Some of them of course
are just beta-agonists,

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:52.643
and they're fine.

00:24:52.643 --> 00:24:54.810
- So anything corticosteroid like?

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:55.740
- Yep.
- Interesting.

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:57.420
And then I always get asked this question,

00:24:57.420 --> 00:25:00.180
and I always reflexively want to say no,

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:02.820
but I don't really know the
answer so I don't reply.

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:05.370
What about topical corticosteroid?

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:06.780
You know, people will put cortisone cream.

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:08.580
To me, it seems almost inconceivable

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:10.650
that it would have a systemic
effect, but then again,

00:25:10.650 --> 00:25:11.856
what do I know?

00:25:11.856 --> 00:25:14.370
- It's all dose and time-related.

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:16.320
So, you know, if you're
talking about like I've got

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:17.970
a little rash under my skin,

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:20.373
I'm going to put corticosteroids
on, probably not.

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:23.640
But certainly, with enough of it put on,

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:26.910
I mean it is absorbed,
so it could be an issue.

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:29.100
But that's not typically
what we're concerned with.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:31.590
I mean, we're mostly
concerned with people that are

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:33.450
taking even modest amounts of prednisone

00:25:33.450 --> 00:25:35.103
for months, years at a time.

00:25:36.090 --> 00:25:39.870
Or, like I said, kids that
are using steroid inhalers

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:42.540
for years and years and years.

00:25:42.540 --> 00:25:43.530
Again, I'm not suggesting

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:45.630
that if your kid's on a steroid
inhaler they shouldn't be.

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:47.670
You have to solve the
most important problem,

00:25:47.670 --> 00:25:49.313
and if asthma is the most
important problem, so be it.

00:25:49.313 --> 00:25:52.230
I think you just want to
turn that into, okay, well,

00:25:52.230 --> 00:25:54.780
how much more imperative is it

00:25:54.780 --> 00:25:57.510
that our kid is doing
things that are putting

00:25:57.510 --> 00:26:01.110
a high amount of stress on
their bones, via their muscles,

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:04.980
to make sure that they're in
that maximal capacity to build.

00:26:04.980 --> 00:26:08.220
- Do you think that somebody
in their 30s or 40s or 50s

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:10.500
could still benefit from
strength training in terms of

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:12.818
bone mineral density and longevity,

00:26:12.818 --> 00:26:15.750
as it relates to bone mineral density,

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:18.330
given that there's this
key window earlier,

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:19.644
they might have missed that one.

00:26:19.644 --> 00:26:21.090
- Oh yeah, no, no, this is
essential for the rest of life

00:26:21.090 --> 00:26:23.550
because you're now trying
to prevent the fall off.

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:26.010
So basically the way it
works is you're sort of,

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.860
from birth to say 20 you're in growth.

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:31.230
From 20 to 50, you plateau.

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:34.770
At 50, men start to decline,
but it's really small.

00:26:34.770 --> 00:26:37.147
Women start to decline
and it's precipitous.

00:26:37.147 --> 00:26:38.760
- And it's related to the drop in estrogen

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:40.860
associated with menopause or premenopause?

00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:41.693
- [Peter] Correct.

00:26:41.693 --> 00:26:46.380
- And can we get into
any of the broad contours

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:47.910
of what that strength training looks like?

00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:49.770
We had Dr. Andy Galpin on the show,

00:26:49.770 --> 00:26:51.360
he talked a lot about
ways to build strength

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:53.640
versus hypertrophy versus
endurance, et cetera.

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:56.540
I think there's pretty good
agreement across the fields of

00:26:57.570 --> 00:27:00.690
physiotherapy, et cetera,
of physiology and medicine,

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:01.523
in terms of how to do that.

00:27:01.523 --> 00:27:04.650
But my understanding is
fairly low repetition ranges,

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:07.710
so this is anywhere from
one to six repetitions.

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:12.000
Typically not aiming
for a pump hypertrophy,

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:12.833
that sort of thing.

00:27:12.833 --> 00:27:14.670
But heavy loads that are hard to move,

00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:18.060
80% of one repetition maximum or more,

00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:19.653
done with long rest periods,

00:27:21.870 --> 00:27:24.780
two to three times a week type
thing, is that about right?

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:26.790
- Yeah, if you look at
the literature on this,

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:28.110
it's going to tell you,

00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:31.470
it's going to differentiate
power lifting from weightlifting.

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:32.400
In other words, yeah,

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:36.540
you do need to be kind of moving
against a very heavy load.

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:37.860
Now again, that can look very different

00:27:37.860 --> 00:27:39.870
depending on your level of experience.

00:27:39.870 --> 00:27:42.300
Like I really like deadlifting.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:45.330
Now, I mean, I can count the
number of days left in my life

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:48.330
when I'm going to want to
do sets over 400 pounds.

00:27:48.330 --> 00:27:51.612
But, you know, I'll pick and
choose the days that I do.

00:27:51.612 --> 00:27:54.030
But, you know, I grew
up doing those things,

00:27:54.030 --> 00:27:55.590
I'm comfortable with those movements.

00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:57.630
If I had a 60-year-old woman

00:27:57.630 --> 00:27:59.310
who's never lifted weights in her life,

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:00.933
who we now have to get lifting,

00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:04.830
I mean, we could get her
to deadlift, but I think,

00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:07.830
I wouldn't make perfect the enemy of good.

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:09.810
I'd be happy to put her
on a leg press machine

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:11.253
and just get her doing that.

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:15.150
You know, it's not as pure
a movement as a deadlift,

00:28:15.150 --> 00:28:16.020
but who cares, right?

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:18.600
We can still put her at
a heavy load, for her,

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:20.070
and do so safely.

00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:22.710
So, now that said, I mean,

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:24.840
there was a study that
was done in Australia,

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:27.630
and I'm, you know, hopefully
we can find a link to it.

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:30.180
There's a video on YouTube that
actually kind of has the PI

00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:31.530
sort of walking through the results.

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:32.370
I could send it to you.

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:33.738
- [Andrew] Okay, yeah we'll

00:28:33.738 --> 00:28:34.571
track it down.
- And it's just amazing.

00:28:34.571 --> 00:28:36.060
They took a group of older women.

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:37.650
They looked like they
were in their 60s or 70s,

00:28:37.650 --> 00:28:39.330
who had never lifted
weights in their life,

00:28:39.330 --> 00:28:41.040
who had osteopenia,

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:43.110
and some probably
already had osteoporosis,

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:44.400
and they basically just put them on

00:28:44.400 --> 00:28:45.690
a strength training protocol.

00:28:45.690 --> 00:28:47.730
And it is remarkable to watch these women.

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:48.960
They're doing good mornings.

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:49.830
They're doing dead lifts.

00:28:49.830 --> 00:28:51.930
They're picking heavy
things up off the ground.

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:53.703
I think one woman was picking up,

00:28:54.630 --> 00:28:58.740
God, I want to say she was like
picking like 50, 60 kilos up

00:28:58.740 --> 00:28:59.850
off the ground.

00:28:59.850 --> 00:29:02.010
I mean, just staggering sums
of weight for these women

00:29:02.010 --> 00:29:03.600
who have never done anything.

00:29:03.600 --> 00:29:07.320
And their bone health is
improving at this age.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:11.550
So the goal, frankly,
is to just, you know,

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:13.180
never get to the point where

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:16.230
you have to do this for the first time.

00:29:16.230 --> 00:29:18.990
Strength training is such an
essential part of our existence

00:29:18.990 --> 00:29:22.440
that it's never too late to start,

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:23.740
but you should never stop.

00:29:24.859 --> 00:29:25.692
- I love that advice.

00:29:25.692 --> 00:29:27.510
Is it a systemic effect or a local effect?

00:29:27.510 --> 00:29:31.050
So, for instance, let's say that, well,

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:32.823
my mother's in her late 70s.

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:35.820
She actually used to be really
strong when we were kids.

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:37.800
She could move this fish
tank that was in my room

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:40.150
long before I could move
it, and I was always,

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:41.670
she's really strong.

00:29:41.670 --> 00:29:44.970
Over the years, I wouldn't
call her frail by any means,

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:46.830
but I certainly think she could benefit

00:29:46.830 --> 00:29:48.990
from some strength training.

00:29:48.990 --> 00:29:51.240
Let's say she were to start
doing some leg presses

00:29:51.240 --> 00:29:52.800
or start even with air squats

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:55.080
and maybe work up to some pushups,

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:56.100
are the effects all local,

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:58.380
meaning if she were to just train her legs

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:00.270
or just do pushups,

00:30:00.270 --> 00:30:03.408
would it only be the
loads applied to the limbs

00:30:03.408 --> 00:30:04.620
and muscles and tissues

00:30:04.620 --> 00:30:05.550
that were involved?
- I think that's where

00:30:05.550 --> 00:30:06.742
the bulk of it is, yeah.

00:30:06.742 --> 00:30:07.575
- Okay.
- Yeah.

00:30:07.575 --> 00:30:08.408
- [Andrew] So you need to train

00:30:08.408 --> 00:30:09.241
the whole body, essentially.

00:30:09.241 --> 00:30:10.290
- Yeah, now keep in mind,

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:12.990
the diagnosis of
osteopenia and osteoporosis

00:30:12.990 --> 00:30:16.230
is based on only three
locations, the left hip,

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:18.240
the right hip, and the lumbar spine.

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:22.020
So, you know, that's just the convention

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:24.300
by which we make the diagnosis.

00:30:24.300 --> 00:30:25.920
And I think part of that has to do with

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:28.020
that's where the majority
of the insults occur.

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:30.990
Now not all of the insults,
I've seen people that have,

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:33.210
you know, because of
horrible bone density,

00:30:33.210 --> 00:30:36.810
they're fracturing
ankles and tibia, fibula,

00:30:36.810 --> 00:30:39.990
like they're having low tib
fib fractures just walking.

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:41.250
So clearly bone density

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:43.500
outside of those regions does matter.

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:47.026
But much of it is really
focused on, and, by the way,

00:30:47.026 --> 00:30:48.270
you know, you fall, you break a wrist,

00:30:48.270 --> 00:30:51.060
so this is a systemic issue.

00:30:51.060 --> 00:30:54.210
But the majority of the
response is a local response,

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:56.970
'cause it really comes
down to putting a load

00:30:56.970 --> 00:31:00.120
directly on that bone, and
then having that bone, in kind,

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:03.120
respond by laying down more bone.

