00:31:58.290 --> 00:31:59.970
They're giving away five free travel packs

00:31:59.970 --> 00:32:03.870
plus a year's supply of vitamin
D3 K2, with every order.

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:06.060
And of course, vitamin D3 K2 are vital

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:08.160
for all sorts of things
like hormone health

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:09.000
and metabolic health,

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:11.760
and K2 for cardiovascular
health and calcium regulation.

00:32:11.760 --> 00:32:14.970
Again, you can go to
athleticgreens.com/huberman

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:16.680
to claim that special offer.

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:18.840
You mentioned falling and
the problems with falling

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:21.990
and breaking things, and
mortality related to that.

00:32:21.990 --> 00:32:24.150
I wonder whether or not there are also

00:32:24.150 --> 00:32:26.970
health-related effects
of just having weak bones

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:29.520
that are not just about
falling and breaking a bone,

00:32:29.520 --> 00:32:31.920
and dying a year later.

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:33.750
That's obviously very severe.

00:32:33.750 --> 00:32:35.337
Because I think when
people hear about that,

00:32:35.337 --> 00:32:37.380
some people might think, well,
I'll just be more careful.

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:38.340
I'll just move more slowly.

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:40.920
I'll sit in a wheelchair if I need to,

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:42.140
even though I might be able to walk,

00:32:42.140 --> 00:32:43.470
if it keeps me from falling.

00:32:43.470 --> 00:32:45.603
Some people I think adopt that mentality.

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:49.110
What are some of the benefits of having

00:32:49.110 --> 00:32:51.840
high bone mineral density
for men and women,

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:55.860
that are perhaps independent
of risk of injury?

00:32:55.860 --> 00:32:57.180
- Well, I think it's actually the inverse

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:58.260
of what you just said, right?

00:32:58.260 --> 00:33:03.260
It's sort of like, you have to
sort of be able to articulate

00:33:03.690 --> 00:33:05.730
what it is you want in
your marginal decade.

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:07.410
So we use this thing in our practice

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:08.790
called the marginal decade.

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:10.860
Marginal decade is the
last decade of your life.

00:33:10.860 --> 00:33:12.810
So everyone will have a marginal decade.

00:33:12.810 --> 00:33:13.980
That's the only thing I can tell you

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:16.804
with absolute certainty, right?

00:33:16.804 --> 00:33:17.637
- I believe you.

00:33:17.637 --> 00:33:18.570
- There's no immortality.

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:19.770
There's no hidden elixir

00:33:19.770 --> 00:33:22.200
that's going to help us live
to be, you know, whatever.

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:25.473
I mean, we're all going to be in
our last decade at some point.

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:28.260
And outside of people who die suddenly

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:29.433
or through an accident,

00:33:30.600 --> 00:33:33.120
most of us know when we're
in that marginal decade.

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:34.530
You might not know the day you enter it,

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:37.860
but most people, you
know, who are old enough,

00:33:37.860 --> 00:33:39.810
if you tell them, are you in
the last decade of your life,

00:33:39.810 --> 00:33:41.970
they probably have a sense that they are.

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:44.940
So I think the exercise
that we like to go through

00:33:44.940 --> 00:33:49.650
with our patients very
early on is have them,

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:51.690
in exquisite detail,

00:33:51.690 --> 00:33:53.340
more detail than they've ever considered,

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:56.133
so we have to prompt them
with like 50 questions,

00:33:57.030 --> 00:34:00.600
lay out what their marginal
decade should look like.

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:02.700
- Wow, that's a serious exercise.

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:04.230
- It's a very serious exercise, right?

00:34:04.230 --> 00:34:06.870
Like what, tell me everything
that is going to happen

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:07.710
in your marginal decade.

00:34:07.710 --> 00:34:08.760
I don't know when it's
going to be, Andrew,

00:34:08.760 --> 00:34:12.450
it could be 87 to 97 if
we're doing well, right?

00:34:12.450 --> 00:34:14.880
It might be 79 to 89, I don't know.

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:19.880
But, you know, it would really
be a very nuanced exploration

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:21.660
of that topic.

00:34:21.660 --> 00:34:23.700
And I think until you do that,

00:34:23.700 --> 00:34:26.700
all of this other stuff is just abstract

00:34:26.700 --> 00:34:28.173
and kind of nonsense.

00:34:29.010 --> 00:34:30.750
Until a person can tell you

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:34.770
what it is that they want to
be doing in that last decade,

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:37.950
you can't design a
program to get them there.

00:34:37.950 --> 00:34:39.100
I mean, think about it.

00:34:40.140 --> 00:34:41.790
Someone wants to do an Ironman,

00:34:41.790 --> 00:34:44.880
we take it for granted that
we know what the objective is.

00:34:44.880 --> 00:34:46.530
I have to be able to swim 2 1/2 miles.

00:34:46.530 --> 00:34:48.510
I have to be able to get
out, take my wetsuit off,

00:34:48.510 --> 00:34:51.750
hop on my bike, ride 112
miles, get off my bike,

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:53.520
take the bike shoes off,
put the run shoes on,

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:54.570
run 26.2 miles.

00:34:54.570 --> 00:34:56.370
Like we get it, we know
what the objective is.

00:34:56.370 --> 00:34:58.980
And only by knowing that can you train.

00:34:58.980 --> 00:35:01.470
Can you imagine if I said to you,

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:02.670
Andrew, I'm going to have you do

00:35:02.670 --> 00:35:05.580
an athletic event in a
year, start training.

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:08.460
I'm not going to tell you
what it is, just do it.

00:35:08.460 --> 00:35:10.440
Could be playing basketball.

00:35:10.440 --> 00:35:12.870
You know, it could be
swimming to Catalina Island.

00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:15.180
It could be running 100 miles.

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:16.290
You wouldn't be able to do it.

00:35:16.290 --> 00:35:17.730
So similarly, if we don't know

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:20.490
what our marginal decade is meant to be,

00:35:20.490 --> 00:35:22.230
there's no way to train for it.

00:35:22.230 --> 00:35:23.580
- Do you think this is a good exercise

00:35:23.580 --> 00:35:26.040
for anyone and everyone
to do on their own,

00:35:26.040 --> 00:35:26.940
regardless of age here?

00:35:26.940 --> 00:35:27.840
I'm hearing this
- Absolutely.

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:28.673
- and I'm thinking,

00:35:28.673 --> 00:35:31.410
I need to think about when
my last decade might be

00:35:31.410 --> 00:35:33.150
and what I want that to look like.

00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:34.980
- Absolutely, I mean, when I
say we do it with our patients,

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:36.660
that's only because that's
the population I work with.

00:35:36.660 --> 00:35:37.920
But there's simply no reason

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:40.140
everybody shouldn't be
going through this exercise.

00:35:40.140 --> 00:35:43.080
- And then you sort of
back script from there,

00:35:43.080 --> 00:35:44.543
figure out what people should be doing,

00:35:44.543 --> 00:35:45.600
given their current health status.

00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:47.310
- Exactly right, we call it back casting.

00:35:47.310 --> 00:35:50.610
So the first step we do is,
once we've really delineated

00:35:50.610 --> 00:35:53.850
what the objective function
looks like, we then say, okay,

00:35:53.850 --> 00:35:58.850
how do you break down that into
metrics that we can measure?

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:02.640
So, you know, you described
doing a whole bunch of things.

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:05.490
Okay, just to let you know,
to do that will require

00:36:05.490 --> 00:36:09.270
a VO2 max of 30 milliliters of oxygen

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:11.160
per minute per kilogram.

00:36:11.160 --> 00:36:14.130
And the person will say,
okay, what does that mean?

00:36:14.130 --> 00:36:16.710
We'll say, well, that's a
measure of your maximal uptake

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:21.710
of oxygen, and that declines
at about 8% to 10% per decade.

00:36:22.890 --> 00:36:24.990
So if you have to be at 30,

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:27.720
and let's just assume you're
going to be doing that at 90,

00:36:27.720 --> 00:36:29.920
so what do you need to
be at 80, 70, 60, 50.

00:36:31.426 --> 00:36:33.210
Okay, here's what it
would need to be at 50.

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:34.530
Okay, what are you now?

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:36.060
Ah, there's a big gap.

00:36:36.060 --> 00:36:38.148
You're below where you need to be now.

00:36:38.148 --> 00:36:40.710
So you're obviously higher than 30 now,

00:36:40.710 --> 00:36:43.348
but if you're only at 42 now

00:36:43.348 --> 00:36:45.930
and you need to be at 30 and 40 years,

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:47.130
you're not going to cut it.

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:48.690
You have to be a lot fitter.

00:36:48.690 --> 00:36:50.271
Okay, now let's do the same exercise

00:36:50.271 --> 00:36:52.920
around strength and stability.

00:36:52.920 --> 00:36:54.870
And without exception, most people,

00:36:54.870 --> 00:36:55.920
when they do this exercise,

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:58.890
will find out they're well
below where they need to be.

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:03.890
So the gravity of aging is more
vicious than people realize,

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:07.140
and therefore the height
of your glider needs to be

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:10.650
much higher than you think
it is when you're our age,

00:37:10.650 --> 00:37:12.480
if you want to be able to do the things

00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:15.150
we probably want to be
able to do when we're 90.

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:17.160
- I absolutely love this approach.

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:18.750
I've never done it in terms of my health.

00:37:18.750 --> 00:37:20.250
I've always thought about
what I want to accomplish

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:22.347
in the next three to six
months or next year or so.

00:37:22.347 --> 00:37:23.970
- And by the way, that's a great approach.

00:37:23.970 --> 00:37:24.900
That's forecasting.

00:37:24.900 --> 00:37:26.190
Forecasting is fantastic.

00:37:26.190 --> 00:37:29.310
Forecasting is really
good at short-term things.

00:37:29.310 --> 00:37:31.410
It doesn't work for long-term things.

00:37:31.410 --> 00:37:33.870
Long-term, you have to do back casting.

00:37:33.870 --> 00:37:35.760
- This back casting approach
really appeals to me

00:37:35.760 --> 00:37:38.400
because in my career, well, I
never anticipate, excuse me.

00:37:38.400 --> 00:37:40.300
I never anticipated I'd be podcasting.

00:37:41.340 --> 00:37:43.830
But that's what I did at some
point as an undergraduate,

00:37:43.830 --> 00:37:45.030
I looked at professors,

00:37:45.030 --> 00:37:46.470
I'm like, that looks
like a pretty good life.

00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:48.120
They seem pretty happy, I
talked to a few of them,

00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:50.610
and then I figured out what
I need to do at each stage

00:37:50.610 --> 00:37:53.790
in order to get to that
next rung on the ladder,

00:37:53.790 --> 00:37:56.280
and just kind of figured it out

00:37:56.280 --> 00:37:59.280
in a back casting kind of
way, as you refer to it.

00:37:59.280 --> 00:38:00.960
I think this is incredibly useful

00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:03.390
because it puts all the
questions about blood work

00:38:03.390 --> 00:38:05.730
and how often to get blood
work and what to measure,

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:08.700
in a really nice context
that's a highly individualized.

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:10.980
I've never heard of this before, so.

00:38:10.980 --> 00:38:12.750
- And I should give a nod to Annie Duke.

00:38:12.750 --> 00:38:15.780
I used to always refer to
this as reverse engineering,

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:17.220
but in Annie Duke's book,

00:38:17.220 --> 00:38:19.770
she wrote about this exact thing
and called it back casting,

00:38:19.770 --> 00:38:22.320
and I was like, I like the
term back casting better.

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:24.480
I think it's more intuitive
than reverse engineering.

00:38:24.480 --> 00:38:27.204
- Yeah, there's a real
genius to it and I think it,

00:38:27.204 --> 00:38:28.037
'cause it sets so many things

00:38:28.037 --> 00:38:30.780
into the appropriate
bins and trajectories.

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:33.960
I've heard you talk before
about some of the prime movers

00:38:33.960 --> 00:38:37.623
for longevity and all-risk mortality.

00:38:38.460 --> 00:38:42.300
And I'd love for you to review
a little bit of that for us.

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:44.070
I think we all know
that we shouldn't smoke

00:38:44.070 --> 00:38:46.560
because it's very likely
that we'll die earlier

00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:48.870
if we smoke nicotine.

00:38:48.870 --> 00:38:51.000
I'm neither a marijuana
nor a nicotine smoker,

00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:52.950
so I feel on stable ground there.

00:38:52.950 --> 00:38:54.840
But anytime we see smoking nowadays,

00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:56.430
people really want to distinguish

00:38:56.430 --> 00:38:58.920
between cannabis and nicotine.

00:38:58.920 --> 00:39:00.960
So I am curious about
any differences there

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:03.243
in terms of impact on longevity.

00:39:04.620 --> 00:39:06.450
But in that context,

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:09.990
what are the things that
anyone and everyone can do,

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:13.830
should do, to live longer basically?

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:14.780
- How long you got?

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:17.140
- Well, you tell me.

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:19.590
You tell me.

00:39:19.590 --> 00:39:20.700
I'd like to live, to be,

00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:23.460
I'd like my final decade
to be between 90 and 100.

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:24.630
- Oh no, I meant how long do you,

00:39:24.630 --> 00:39:25.952
yeah yeah.
- No no, I'm just kidding.

00:39:25.952 --> 00:39:26.785
I'm just kidding.
- And will we spend

00:39:26.785 --> 00:39:28.170
from now until you're
90 talking about this?

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:29.280
- Well, there's a risk of that.

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:30.990
But top contour is fine,

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:32.460
I know you've done a
lot of content on this,

00:39:32.460 --> 00:39:33.810
and we will give people links

00:39:33.810 --> 00:39:35.700
to some of that more in depth content.

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:38.400
But, you know, let's say
we were on a short flight

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:42.300
from here to San Diego,
we're in Los Angeles now,

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:43.980
and we got take off and landing,

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:45.690
and we don't want to
kink our neck too much

00:39:45.690 --> 00:39:46.523
by doing this thing.

00:39:46.523 --> 00:39:48.000
So if I just said, hey, you know,

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:52.380
gimme the extended version
of the three by five card.

00:39:52.380 --> 00:39:53.630
What does that look like?

00:39:55.110 --> 00:39:56.880
- So, let's start with
a couple of the things

00:39:56.880 --> 00:39:57.810
that you've already highlighted.

00:39:57.810 --> 00:40:00.480
So smoking, how much does
smoking increase your risk

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:01.890
of all-cause mortality?

00:40:01.890 --> 00:40:04.230
And the reason we like to
talk about what's called ACM,

00:40:04.230 --> 00:40:05.700
or all-cause mortality,

00:40:05.700 --> 00:40:08.850
is it's really agnostic to how you die.

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:10.740
And that doesn't always make sense.

00:40:10.740 --> 00:40:12.720
I mean, if you're talking about, you know,

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:14.100
a very specific intervention,

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:15.840
like a anti-cancer therapeutic,

00:40:15.840 --> 00:40:17.850
you really care about
cancer-specific mortality

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:19.920
or heart-specific mortality.

00:40:19.920 --> 00:40:21.480
But when we talk about
these sort of broad things,

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:22.830
we like to talk about ACM.

00:40:22.830 --> 00:40:24.660
So, you know, using smoking,

00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:29.310
smoking is approximately a 40%
increase in the risk of ACM.

00:40:29.310 --> 00:40:31.710
- What does that translate to in,

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:35.127
that means I'm shortening my life by 40%?

00:40:35.127 --> 00:40:36.720
- No, it means at any point in time,

00:40:36.720 --> 00:40:39.780
there's a 40% greater risk
that you're going to die

00:40:39.780 --> 00:40:41.040
relative to a non-smoker

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:42.390
and a never smoker.
- Got it.

00:40:42.390 --> 00:40:43.223
- Yeah.

00:40:43.223 --> 00:40:44.056
So it's important to distinguish.

00:40:44.056 --> 00:40:46.170
It doesn't mean your lifespan
is going to be 40% less.

00:40:46.170 --> 00:40:47.760
It means at any point
in time, standing there,

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:50.610
your risk of death is 40% higher.

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:52.380
And, by the way, that'll
catch up with you, right?

00:40:52.380 --> 00:40:54.780
At some point that catches up.

00:40:54.780 --> 00:40:55.860
High blood pressure.

00:40:55.860 --> 00:41:00.860
It's about a 20 to 25% increase
in all-cause mortality.

00:41:01.200 --> 00:41:02.490
You take something really extreme,

00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:04.260
like endstage kidney disease.

00:41:04.260 --> 00:41:06.570
So these are patients
that are on dialysis,

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:08.280
waiting for an organ.

00:41:08.280 --> 00:41:10.260
And again, there's a
confounder there because

00:41:10.260 --> 00:41:11.850
there's what's the underlying condition

00:41:11.850 --> 00:41:12.683
that leads you to that.

00:41:12.683 --> 00:41:14.820
It's, you know, profound
hypertension, you know,

00:41:14.820 --> 00:41:17.700
significant Type II diabetes
that's been uncontrolled.

00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:18.533
You know, that's enormous,

00:41:18.533 --> 00:41:23.100
that's about 175% increase in ACM.

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:24.750
So the hazard ratio is like 2.75.

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:31.230
Type II diabetes is probably
about a 1.25 as well.

00:41:31.230 --> 00:41:32.310
So a 25% increase.

00:41:32.310 --> 00:41:33.720
So another question is
like, how do you improve?

00:41:33.720 --> 00:41:35.730
So what are the things that improve those?

00:41:35.730 --> 00:41:39.810
So now here we do this by
comparing low to high achievers

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:40.643
on other metrics.

00:41:40.643 --> 00:41:45.540
So if you look at low muscle
mass versus high muscle mass,

00:41:45.540 --> 00:41:47.850
what is the improvement?

00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:49.170
And it's pretty significant.

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:50.610
It's about three X.

00:41:50.610 --> 00:41:52.560
So if you compare low muscle mass people

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:54.810
to high muscle mass people as they age,

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:56.580
the low muscle mass people have about

00:41:56.580 --> 00:41:59.430
a three X hazard ratio, or 200% increase

00:41:59.430 --> 00:42:01.170
in all-cause mortality.

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:03.330
Now, if you look at the
data more carefully,

00:42:03.330 --> 00:42:06.570
you realize that it's probably less

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:09.330
the muscle mass fully doing that,

00:42:09.330 --> 00:42:12.240
and it's more the high
association with strength.

00:42:12.240 --> 00:42:15.360
And when you start to
just tease out strength,

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:17.610
you can realize that
strength could be probably

00:42:17.610 --> 00:42:22.610
3 1/2 X as a hazard ratio,
meaning about 250% greater risk,

00:42:24.210 --> 00:42:26.670
if you have low strength to high strength.

00:42:26.670 --> 00:42:29.250
- And high strength is
the ability to move loads

00:42:29.250 --> 00:42:31.410
at 80 to 90% of one repetition?

00:42:31.410 --> 00:42:32.940
- It's all defined by given studies.

00:42:32.940 --> 00:42:36.750
So, the most common things
that are used are actually,

00:42:36.750 --> 00:42:39.300
they're used for the
purposes of experiments

00:42:39.300 --> 00:42:40.777
that make it easy to do.

00:42:40.777 --> 00:42:42.150
And I don't even think
they're the best metrics.

00:42:42.150 --> 00:42:46.950
So they're usually using like
grip strength, leg extensions,

00:42:46.950 --> 00:42:50.340
and like wall sits,
squats, things like that.

00:42:50.340 --> 00:42:53.850
So how long can you sit in a
squatted position at 90 degrees

00:42:53.850 --> 00:42:54.683
without support,

00:42:54.683 --> 00:42:56.683
would be a great demonstration
of quad strength.

00:42:56.683 --> 00:43:00.300
A leg extension, you know,
how much weight can you hold

00:43:00.300 --> 00:43:03.150
for how long, relative to
body weight, things like that.

00:43:03.150 --> 00:43:05.340
You know, we have a whole
strength program that we do

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:07.260
with our patients, we have
something called the SMA.

00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:08.910
So it's the strength metrics assessment.

00:43:08.910 --> 00:43:13.770
And we put them through 11
tests that are really difficult,

00:43:13.770 --> 00:43:15.678
you know, like a dead hang is one of them.

00:43:15.678 --> 00:43:16.650
Like how long can you dead
hang your body weight,

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:17.640
stuff like that.

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:20.850
So we're trying to be more
granular in that insight,

00:43:20.850 --> 00:43:23.340
but tie it back to these principles.

00:43:23.340 --> 00:43:25.350
If you look at cardio respiratory fitness,

00:43:25.350 --> 00:43:26.880
it's even more profound.

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:30.660
So if you look at people
who are in the bottom 25%

00:43:30.660 --> 00:43:33.510
for their age and sex,
in terms of VO2 max,

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:37.410
and you compare them to
the people that are just at

00:43:37.410 --> 00:43:40.650
the 50th to 75th percentile,

00:43:40.650 --> 00:43:43.260
you're talking about a
two X difference roughly,

00:43:43.260 --> 00:43:45.900
in the risk of ACM.

00:43:45.900 --> 00:43:50.070
If you compare the bottom
25% to the top 2.5%,

00:43:50.070 --> 00:43:51.270
so you're talking about

00:43:51.270 --> 00:43:54.840
bottom quarter to the
elite, for a given age,

00:43:54.840 --> 00:43:57.660
you're talking about five X.

00:43:57.660 --> 00:44:00.030
- Wow!
- 400% difference

00:44:00.030 --> 00:44:01.260
in all-cause mortality.

00:44:01.260 --> 00:44:03.810
That's probably the single
strongest association I've seen

00:44:03.810 --> 00:44:05.910
for any modifiable behavior.

00:44:05.910 --> 00:44:06.860
- Incredible.

00:44:06.860 --> 00:44:07.980
So when you say elite,

00:44:07.980 --> 00:44:10.200
these are people that
are running marathons

00:44:10.200 --> 00:44:11.250
at a pretty rapid clip.

00:44:11.250 --> 00:44:12.083
- Not necessarily.

00:44:12.083 --> 00:44:13.830
It's just like what the
VO2 max is for that,

00:44:13.830 --> 00:44:16.743
like my VO2 max would be in
the elite for my age group.

00:44:17.755 --> 00:44:20.610
My VO2, but again, I'm
training very deliberately

00:44:20.610 --> 00:44:21.690
to make sure that it's in that.

00:44:21.690 --> 00:44:24.870
So I wouldn't consider myself
elite at anything anymore,

00:44:24.870 --> 00:44:28.560
but I still maintain a VO2
max that is elite for my age.

00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:32.342
- I consider you an elite
physician and podcaster.

00:44:32.342 --> 00:44:33.570
And guy all around.

00:44:33.570 --> 00:44:34.403
But true.

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:38.640
But in terms of, okay, so-

00:44:38.640 --> 00:44:39.741
- But the point is like,

00:44:39.741 --> 00:44:41.010
you don't have to be a world class athlete

00:44:41.010 --> 00:44:41.970
to be elite here, yeah.

00:44:41.970 --> 00:44:43.590
- Got it.

00:44:43.590 --> 00:44:44.850
So maybe we could talk a little bit about

00:44:44.850 --> 00:44:47.380
the specifics around the
training to get into the

00:44:48.314 --> 00:44:49.770
top two tiers there,

00:44:49.770 --> 00:44:52.740
because it seems that those
are enormous positive effects

00:44:52.740 --> 00:44:54.600
of cardiovascular exercise,

00:44:54.600 --> 00:44:58.320
far greater than the sorts
of numbers that I see around,

00:44:58.320 --> 00:45:00.450
let's just say supplement
A or supplement B.

00:45:00.450 --> 00:45:02.180
- Well, and that's, you know,

00:45:02.180 --> 00:45:03.930
like this is my whole
pet peeve in life, right?

00:45:03.930 --> 00:45:06.600
It's like, I just can't get enough

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:08.550
of the machinating and arguing

00:45:08.550 --> 00:45:11.460
about this supplement
versus that supplement.

00:45:11.460 --> 00:45:15.330
And I feel like you shouldn't
be having those arguments

00:45:15.330 --> 00:45:18.600
until you have your exercise
house in order, you know.

00:45:18.600 --> 00:45:20.950
You shouldn't be arguing about

00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:24.060
this nuance if you're carnivore diet,

00:45:24.060 --> 00:45:25.980
versus this nuance if you're paleo diet,

00:45:25.980 --> 00:45:28.440
versus this nuance if you're vegan diet,

00:45:28.440 --> 00:45:31.860
like, until you can deadlift
your body weight for 10 reps.

00:45:31.860 --> 00:45:33.720
Like then you can come and
talk about those things,

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:35.280
or something like, let's just
come up with some metrics.

00:45:35.280 --> 00:45:39.251
Like until your VO2 max is at
least to the 75th percentile

00:45:39.251 --> 00:45:42.270
and you're able to dead
hang for at least a minute,

00:45:42.270 --> 00:45:44.160
and you're able to wall
sit for at least two,

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:45.570
like we could rattle off a bunch of

00:45:45.570 --> 00:45:47.763
relatively low hanging fruit.

00:45:48.840 --> 00:45:49.980
I wish there was a rule that said like

00:45:49.980 --> 00:45:52.500
you couldn't talk about
anything else health-related.

00:45:52.500 --> 00:45:53.880
- We can make that rule.

00:45:53.880 --> 00:45:54.810
- [Peter] No one'll listen to it.

00:45:54.810 --> 00:45:55.643
- I don't know about that.

00:45:55.643 --> 00:45:56.820
We can make whatever rules we want.

00:45:56.820 --> 00:45:58.590
We can call it Attia's rule.

00:45:58.590 --> 00:45:59.877
One thing I've done
before on this podcast,

00:45:59.877 --> 00:46:01.110
and on social media is,

00:46:01.110 --> 00:46:03.270
just borrowing from the
tradition in science,

00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:05.070
which is it's inappropriate
to name something

00:46:05.070 --> 00:46:08.820
after yourself, unless you
were a scientist before 1950.

00:46:08.820 --> 00:46:10.350
But it's totally
appropriate to name things

00:46:10.350 --> 00:46:11.183
after other people.

00:46:11.183 --> 00:46:12.510
So I'm going to call it Attia's rule.

00:46:12.510 --> 00:46:16.156
Until you can do the following
things, don't talk about

00:46:16.156 --> 00:46:17.057
supplements.
- Please refrain

00:46:17.057 --> 00:46:18.240
from talking about
supplements and nutrition.

00:46:18.240 --> 00:46:21.000
- There it is, hereafter,
thought of, referred to,

00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:23.340
and referenced as Attia's rule.

00:46:23.340 --> 00:46:24.660
I coined the phrase, not him.

00:46:24.660 --> 00:46:28.020
So there's no ego involved,
but it is now Attia's rule.

00:46:28.020 --> 00:46:28.853
Watch out.

00:46:28.853 --> 00:46:30.240
Hashtag Attia's rule.

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:31.350
- Oh God!

00:46:31.350 --> 00:46:33.360
- Wikipedia entry, Attia's rule.

00:46:33.360 --> 00:46:36.270
In all seriousness, and
I am serious about that.

00:46:36.270 --> 00:46:39.510
Dead hang for about a minute
seems like a really good goal

00:46:39.510 --> 00:46:40.777
for a lot of people.

00:46:40.777 --> 00:46:42.106
At least-
- That's our goal.

00:46:42.106 --> 00:46:43.890
I think we have a minute
and a half is the goal

00:46:43.890 --> 00:46:45.030
for a 40-year-old woman.

00:46:45.030 --> 00:46:46.830
Two minutes is the goal
for a 40-year-old man.

00:46:46.830 --> 00:46:51.480
So we adjust them up and
down, based on age and gender.

00:46:51.480 --> 00:46:52.313
- Great.

00:46:52.313 --> 00:46:53.940
And then the wall sit,
what are some numbers?

00:46:53.940 --> 00:46:54.773
- We don't use a wall sit,

00:46:54.773 --> 00:46:58.830
we do just a straight squat,
air squat at 90 degrees.

00:46:58.830 --> 00:47:01.050
And I believe two minutes is the standard

00:47:01.050 --> 00:47:02.670
for both men and women at 40.

00:47:02.670 --> 00:47:03.503
- Great.

00:47:03.503 --> 00:47:05.917
And then, because for some
people thinking in terms

00:47:05.917 --> 00:47:07.350
of VO2 max is a little more complicated,

00:47:07.350 --> 00:47:09.930
they might not have access to
the equipment to measure it,

00:47:09.930 --> 00:47:13.530
et cetera, what can we
talk about, think about,

00:47:13.530 --> 00:47:14.580
in terms of cardiovascular.

00:47:14.580 --> 00:47:17.190
So run a mile at seven minutes or less,

00:47:17.190 --> 00:47:18.023
eight minutes or less?

00:47:18.023 --> 00:47:18.856
- Oh, that's a good question.

00:47:18.856 --> 00:47:22.650
So there are really good
VO2 max estimators online,

00:47:22.650 --> 00:47:26.190
and you can plug in your activity de jure,

00:47:26.190 --> 00:47:29.340
so be it a bike, run, or rowing machine,

00:47:29.340 --> 00:47:31.440
and it can give you a sense of that.

00:47:31.440 --> 00:47:34.230
And I don't, I used to know all of those.

00:47:34.230 --> 00:47:35.280
- Oh, that's okay.
- But now that I just

00:47:35.280 --> 00:47:37.650
actually do the testing,
I don't recall them.

00:47:37.650 --> 00:47:39.390
But it's exactly that line of thinking,

00:47:39.390 --> 00:47:41.310
like, can you run a mile in this time.

00:47:41.310 --> 00:47:44.040
If you can, your VO2 max
is approximately this.

00:47:44.040 --> 00:47:44.873
- [Andrew] Great.

00:47:44.873 --> 00:47:49.440
- And I think, somewhere
in my podcast realm,

00:47:49.440 --> 00:47:52.740
I've got all those charts posted of like,

00:47:52.740 --> 00:47:56.190
this is by age, by sex.

00:47:56.190 --> 00:47:59.010
This is what the VO2 max is
in each of those buckets.

00:47:59.010 --> 00:48:01.080
- Terrific, we'll provide links to those.

00:48:01.080 --> 00:48:02.850
We'll have our people find those links.

00:48:02.850 --> 00:48:05.670
And then you mentioned
deadlifting body weight 10 times.

00:48:05.670 --> 00:48:06.930
- I just made that one up.

00:48:06.930 --> 00:48:08.997
That's not one that we include, but-

00:48:08.997 --> 00:48:10.547
- [Andrew] Something like that?

00:48:11.490 --> 00:48:12.480
- We use farmer carries.

00:48:12.480 --> 00:48:14.220
So we'll say for a male,

00:48:14.220 --> 00:48:17.520
you should be able to farmer
carry your body weight for,

00:48:17.520 --> 00:48:19.290
I think we have two minutes.

00:48:19.290 --> 00:48:20.266
- [Andrew] Great.

00:48:20.266 --> 00:48:21.960
- So that's half your
body weight in each hand.

00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:24.900
You should be able to walk
with that for two minutes.

00:48:24.900 --> 00:48:27.300
For women, I think we're
doing 75% of body weight

00:48:27.300 --> 00:48:28.814
or something like that, yeah.

00:48:28.814 --> 00:48:29.647
- Great.

00:48:29.647 --> 00:48:30.540
I love it.

00:48:30.540 --> 00:48:32.970
As indirect measures of how healthy and,

00:48:32.970 --> 00:48:33.930
- Yeah.
- we are.

00:48:33.930 --> 00:48:34.980
And how long we're going to live.

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:37.440
- It's basically grip
strength, it's mobility.

00:48:37.440 --> 00:48:39.120
I mean, again, walking
with that much weight,

00:48:39.120 --> 00:48:42.120
for some people initially, is really hard.

00:48:42.120 --> 00:48:43.980
You know, we use different
things like vertical jump,

00:48:43.980 --> 00:48:45.990
ground contact time if
you're jumping off a box,

00:48:45.990 --> 00:48:46.823
things like that.

00:48:46.823 --> 00:48:48.840
So it's really trying to capture,

00:48:48.840 --> 00:48:49.890
and it's an evolution, right?

00:48:49.890 --> 00:48:51.840
Like I think the test is going to get

00:48:51.840 --> 00:48:55.135
only more and more involved
as we get involved,

00:48:55.135 --> 00:48:56.430
'cause it took us about a year.

00:48:56.430 --> 00:48:59.700
Beth Lewis did the majority
of the work to develop this.

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:01.920
Beth runs our strength
and stability program

00:49:01.920 --> 00:49:02.910
in the practice.

00:49:02.910 --> 00:49:04.830
And, you know, basically I
just tasked her with like, hey,

00:49:04.830 --> 00:49:07.140
go out to the literature and come up with

00:49:07.140 --> 00:49:09.510
all of the best movements
that we think are proxies

00:49:09.510 --> 00:49:12.810
for what you need to be
like the most kick-ass,

00:49:12.810 --> 00:49:14.550
you know, what we call
centenarian decathlete,

00:49:14.550 --> 00:49:16.470
which is the person living
in their marginal decade

00:49:16.470 --> 00:49:17.640
at the best.

00:49:17.640 --> 00:49:19.140
- Well, what I'm about to say is certainly

00:49:19.140 --> 00:49:20.490
a mechanistic leap,

00:49:20.490 --> 00:49:22.950
but if you look at the literature on

00:49:22.950 --> 00:49:25.966
exercise-related neurogenesis in mice,

00:49:25.966 --> 00:49:30.900
or brain atrophy or brain
hypertrophy, et cetera,

00:49:30.900 --> 00:49:32.100
in animal models,

00:49:32.100 --> 00:49:34.620
it's very clear that the best
way to get a nervous system

00:49:34.620 --> 00:49:37.800
to atrophy, to lose
neurons, shrink neurons,

00:49:37.800 --> 00:49:39.840
and or lose connections between neurons,

00:49:39.840 --> 00:49:42.090
is to stop that animal from moving.

00:49:42.090 --> 00:49:44.610
Or to de-enrich its environment,

00:49:44.610 --> 00:49:47.520
deprive it of some sensory input
or multiple sensory inputs.

00:49:47.520 --> 00:49:50.940
And the best way to enhance
the size of neurons,

00:49:50.940 --> 00:49:52.140
the number of connections between neurons,

00:49:52.140 --> 00:49:53.400
and maybe even the number of neurons,

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:55.230
is to enrich its environment
and get it moving

00:49:55.230 --> 00:49:56.820
while enriching that environment.

00:49:56.820 --> 00:49:59.220
- You know, Andrew, I think
it's very difficult for me

00:49:59.220 --> 00:50:00.870
to say that the same
is not true in humans.

00:50:00.870 --> 00:50:04.143
And so the first time this
became clear to me was in 2014,

00:50:04.980 --> 00:50:08.730
I had an analyst, Dan
Pelletier, and I said, Dan,

00:50:08.730 --> 00:50:11.880
I'm going to give you a project
that is vexing me to no end,

00:50:11.880 --> 00:50:16.380
which is, I want you to look
at all of the literature

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:21.270
that we have, both mechanistic
and clinical trial data,

00:50:21.270 --> 00:50:23.973
that talks about Alzheimer's prevention.

00:50:24.829 --> 00:50:28.020
And I want to know every
single type of input.

00:50:28.020 --> 00:50:30.270
And I want to have a clear sense of

00:50:30.270 --> 00:50:33.840
via what mechanism does it
offer what mode of protection?

00:50:33.840 --> 00:50:36.000
And it took Dan, and this was obviously,

00:50:36.000 --> 00:50:38.373
we iterated a lot on this together.

00:50:39.630 --> 00:50:42.930
And he came back with kind
of an amazing presentation

00:50:42.930 --> 00:50:47.125
that took, I don't know, nine
months to a year of work.

00:50:47.125 --> 00:50:50.040
And what amazed me was when
he came back to it, he said,

00:50:50.040 --> 00:50:53.637
the single greatest efficacy
we can point to is exercise.

00:50:53.637 --> 00:50:56.520
And I was like Dan, that's
got to be nonsense, dude.

00:50:56.520 --> 00:50:59.730
There's no way exercise is the
single best thing you can do

00:50:59.730 --> 00:51:00.563
for the brain.

00:51:00.563 --> 00:51:02.490
There has to be some drug you've missed.

00:51:02.490 --> 00:51:06.390
There has to be some other
thing that you've missed.

00:51:06.390 --> 00:51:09.617
And he's like, no, like this
is hands down the best thing

00:51:09.617 --> 00:51:11.251
'cause you're, you know,

00:51:11.251 --> 00:51:12.690
it's not just what it's doing to BDNF.

00:51:12.690 --> 00:51:14.733
It's not just what it's doing
to vascular endothelium.

00:51:14.733 --> 00:51:16.710
It's not just what it's
doing to glucose disposal

00:51:16.710 --> 00:51:18.210
and insulin signaling
and all these things.

00:51:18.210 --> 00:51:22.230
It's just touching every
aspect of the brain.

00:51:22.230 --> 00:51:24.510
And I was very skeptical
for about six months,

00:51:24.510 --> 00:51:26.850
kind of really pushed
on him and I was like,

00:51:26.850 --> 00:51:28.140
I think you're missing something, Dan,

00:51:28.140 --> 00:51:29.310
I think you're missing something.

00:51:29.310 --> 00:51:31.500
And then finally in the end,
looped in Richard Isaacson,

00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:33.360
who's a neurologist that
we work with really closely

00:51:33.360 --> 00:51:34.470
on Alzheimer's prevention.

00:51:34.470 --> 00:51:36.630
And you know, ultimately
it turned into a paper

00:51:36.630 --> 00:51:40.140
that we wrote basically,
you know, about this topic,

00:51:40.140 --> 00:51:41.400
and a few others.

00:51:41.400 --> 00:51:42.357
'Cause again, I thought, oh,

00:51:42.357 --> 00:51:44.430
are you sure it's not EPA and DHA?

00:51:44.430 --> 00:51:46.020
Like that's got to have a bigger impact.

00:51:46.020 --> 00:51:49.020
And again, there are a lot of
things that I think do matter

00:51:49.020 --> 00:51:50.790
and there's a whole host
of things that we do

00:51:50.790 --> 00:51:52.470
for Alzheimer's prevention.

00:51:52.470 --> 00:51:53.970
But I think you're absolutely right.

00:51:53.970 --> 00:51:56.010
There's not one thing
that I'll tell patients

00:51:56.010 --> 00:51:58.230
is more important than exercising.

00:51:58.230 --> 00:51:59.063
And by the way,

00:51:59.063 --> 00:52:03.540
it's not the sort of pathetic
recommendations that are made.

00:52:03.540 --> 00:52:06.210
Like it's, you have to exercise a lot more

00:52:06.210 --> 00:52:08.160
if you want to get this maximum benefit.

00:52:08.160 --> 00:52:10.380
You will get, you know,
the maximum benefit comes

00:52:10.380 --> 00:52:12.120
going from nothing to something.

00:52:12.120 --> 00:52:14.790
So if you go from being
completely sedentary

00:52:14.790 --> 00:52:17.163
to doing 15 MET hours per week,

00:52:18.000 --> 00:52:21.990
you'll get probably a
50% reduction in risk.

00:52:21.990 --> 00:52:23.610
- Wow.
- So a MET hour,

00:52:23.610 --> 00:52:25.170
a MET, just for people who don't know,

00:52:25.170 --> 00:52:26.703
is a metabolic equivalent.

00:52:27.642 --> 00:52:29.940
So we're exerting about 1.3
METs sitting here talking.

00:52:29.940 --> 00:52:31.170
If we were sitting here being quiet,

00:52:31.170 --> 00:52:32.470
it would be about one MET.

00:52:33.540 --> 00:52:36.840
Walking really briskly
would be about five METs.

00:52:36.840 --> 00:52:40.170
So 15 MET hours per week would be

00:52:40.170 --> 00:52:42.540
three one hour really brisk walks.

00:52:42.540 --> 00:52:44.430
That's not a lot of work.

00:52:44.430 --> 00:52:47.580
But just going from doing
nothing to doing that

00:52:47.580 --> 00:52:51.450
would give you 50% of the
benefit that you would get

00:52:51.450 --> 00:52:53.040
from going all the way.

00:52:53.040 --> 00:52:57.390
Now I, again, I think, I'm
personally a little skeptical of

00:52:57.390 --> 00:52:59.970
how much that's, I think it's
probably a bit less than that.

00:52:59.970 --> 00:53:02.730
I think there's more upside
than people appreciate.

00:53:02.730 --> 00:53:05.160
But the studies, I don't
think, can truly capture that.

00:53:05.160 --> 00:53:10.160
But look, you know, there's
no reason to not be exercising

00:53:10.560 --> 00:53:13.050
more than that, and capture more benefit,

00:53:13.050 --> 00:53:15.600
even though the rate at
which you accrue it is less.

00:53:15.600 --> 00:53:17.760
And it also speaks to the
health span side of this,

00:53:17.760 --> 00:53:19.860
which is not necessarily
captured in those data.

00:53:19.860 --> 00:53:20.760
The health span gets back to

00:53:20.760 --> 00:53:22.740
the functional piece we
opened with, which is

00:53:22.740 --> 00:53:24.930
what do you want to be doing
in your marginal decade?

00:53:24.930 --> 00:53:26.820
Do you want to be able to
pick up a great-grandkid,

00:53:26.820 --> 00:53:28.140
if they come running at you?

00:53:28.140 --> 00:53:29.880
Do you want to be able
to get up off the floor?

00:53:29.880 --> 00:53:31.530
Do you want to be able to
play on the floor with a kid

00:53:31.530 --> 00:53:33.660
and then get up on your own?

00:53:33.660 --> 00:53:36.780
- And I think most people are
thinking final years of life,

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:37.613
they're trying to think, you know,

00:53:37.613 --> 00:53:40.080
how can they take
themselves to the bathroom?

00:53:40.080 --> 00:53:43.368
They're thinking, how can
they sit up off the toilet?

00:53:43.368 --> 00:53:44.880
I mean you have like really base,

00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:48.750
vegetative type functions,
right, at some level.

00:53:48.750 --> 00:53:51.270
I love this, again, this
idea of marginal decade

00:53:51.270 --> 00:53:53.700
and using that as a way to back cast,

00:53:53.700 --> 00:53:58.700
to actual methods and
behaviors and protocols

00:53:59.190 --> 00:54:01.832
that one should be doing on a daily basis.

00:54:01.832 --> 00:54:05.190
I'll use anecdata, as it's now called,

00:54:05.190 --> 00:54:08.340
to cite just, I know
three Nobel prize winners,

00:54:08.340 --> 00:54:09.570
which doesn't mean anything

00:54:09.570 --> 00:54:10.830
except that they did beautiful work.

00:54:10.830 --> 00:54:12.870
But the point is that
they're all in their 90s.

00:54:12.870 --> 00:54:15.840
So I'll name them 'cause
I'm complimenting them

00:54:15.840 --> 00:54:16.920
for what they've done.

00:54:16.920 --> 00:54:18.660
Not just their work, but
what I'm about to describe.

00:54:18.660 --> 00:54:20.373
So Eric Kandel at Columbia.

00:54:21.390 --> 00:54:23.430
Nobel prize winner for work on memory.

00:54:23.430 --> 00:54:25.410
Torsten Wiesel, work on neuroplasticity.

00:54:25.410 --> 00:54:28.680
And then Richard Axel,
who's also at Columbia,

00:54:28.680 --> 00:54:30.480
Nobel prize winning work
for molecular biology

00:54:30.480 --> 00:54:33.450
of smelling and molecular
biology generally.

00:54:33.450 --> 00:54:35.820
All three of them still alive,

00:54:35.820 --> 00:54:38.190
Richard's younger
compared to the other two.

00:54:38.190 --> 00:54:41.520
All three of them either
swim, jog, or play tennis,

00:54:41.520 --> 00:54:43.830
or racquetball I think is Richard's thing,

00:54:43.830 --> 00:54:45.750
multiple times per week.

00:54:45.750 --> 00:54:48.930
They're all cognitively
still extremely sharp,

00:54:48.930 --> 00:54:50.910
still interested in the
arts, doing science,

00:54:50.910 --> 00:54:52.770
curious about science,
running laboratories,

00:54:52.770 --> 00:54:54.270
writing books, going on podcasts.

00:54:54.270 --> 00:54:55.440
I mean, it's incredible.

00:54:55.440 --> 00:54:57.060
Again, that's anecdata.

00:54:57.060 --> 00:54:59.790
But I was kind of surprised to learn that

00:54:59.790 --> 00:55:01.980
colleagues that were so
intellectually strong

00:55:01.980 --> 00:55:04.020
were also so obsessed with exercise.

00:55:04.020 --> 00:55:06.570
I mean, they really are obsessed
with their exercise routine

00:55:06.570 --> 00:55:09.990
and early on linked that to their,

00:55:09.990 --> 00:55:12.810
some of their intellectual
vigor over time.

00:55:12.810 --> 00:55:14.760
I want to just also use
that as a jumping off point

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:18.000
to ask about one kind of
niche thing, but it comes up.

00:55:18.000 --> 00:55:19.950
I don't think I'm going to out
which one of those told me this,

00:55:19.950 --> 00:55:21.990
but one of those three individuals

00:55:21.990 --> 00:55:24.870
chews an excessive amount of Nicorette.

00:55:24.870 --> 00:55:28.530
Used to be a smoker and I asked
him why and he said because,

00:55:28.530 --> 00:55:31.080
in his estimation,

00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:33.540
it's protective against
Parkinson's and Alzheimer's,

00:55:33.540 --> 00:55:35.670
or at least the

00:55:35.670 --> 00:55:39.720
nicotinic acetylcholine
augmentation of nicotine,

00:55:39.720 --> 00:55:43.200
'cause nicotine is an
acetylcholine receptor obviously,

00:55:43.200 --> 00:55:47.100
is known to create a state of
focus and neural enhancement.

00:55:47.100 --> 00:55:49.727
What are your thoughts about
not smoking, let's just,

00:55:49.727 --> 00:55:53.130
I just want to be really clear,
people, don't smoke nicotine,

00:55:53.130 --> 00:55:54.630
vape nicotine, it's going
to shorten your life.

00:55:54.630 --> 00:55:57.870
Just terrible idea, addictive,
et cetera, in my opinion.

00:55:57.870 --> 00:56:02.550
But what are your thoughts
about augmenting acetylcholine,

00:56:02.550 --> 00:56:03.780
through the use of nicotine,

00:56:03.780 --> 00:56:06.120
in order to keep the
brain healthy and focused?

00:56:06.120 --> 00:56:07.830
Again, this is one Nobel prize winner,

00:56:07.830 --> 00:56:09.630
so it's truly N of one,

00:56:09.630 --> 00:56:11.940
but he's so convinced
that this matches up with

00:56:11.940 --> 00:56:14.430
the mechanistic data on
acetylcholine and cognition,

00:56:14.430 --> 00:56:16.130
that I'd love to hear your thoughts on it.

00:56:16.130 --> 00:56:19.470
- So I can't speak to the AD
prevention component of it.

00:56:19.470 --> 00:56:21.990
I'd have to run that by
a couple of my colleagues

00:56:21.990 --> 00:56:24.240
who I collaborate with on that.

00:56:24.240 --> 00:56:25.800
But I can definitely speak to

00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:27.430
the cognitive enhancement piece of it.

00:56:27.430 --> 00:56:31.830
And I actually did an AMA on
this, probably a year ago,

00:56:31.830 --> 00:56:34.440
where I went into all of
the gory details of it

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:37.140
and talked about my own use of nicotine,

00:56:37.140 --> 00:56:38.730
which I'll cycle on and off,

00:56:38.730 --> 00:56:40.080
I've been doing it for the last 10 years.

00:56:40.080 --> 00:56:42.000
I haven't-
- What form do you take it in?

00:56:42.000 --> 00:56:43.320
- I used to use the gum.

00:56:43.320 --> 00:56:44.370
I don't like the gum anymore,

00:56:44.370 --> 00:56:48.153
so now I like these little lozenges that,

00:56:49.307 --> 00:56:51.210
and I'll tell you a
funny story about this.

00:56:51.210 --> 00:56:55.470
So our mutual acquaintance,
David Sinclair,

00:56:55.470 --> 00:56:59.700
mentioned a company to me a year ago.

00:56:59.700 --> 00:57:02.130
He's like, hey, have you
heard of this company?

00:57:02.130 --> 00:57:03.450
And I forget the name of the company,

00:57:03.450 --> 00:57:04.920
but he gave me some name.

00:57:04.920 --> 00:57:08.457
So I go online and it's like
this company selling nicotine.

00:57:08.457 --> 00:57:10.470
And I'm like, I wonder why
he's asking me to do this.

00:57:10.470 --> 00:57:12.300
Well, I'll just order a bunch

00:57:12.300 --> 00:57:15.090
and then we'll figure out
why, 'cause we were, you know,

00:57:15.090 --> 00:57:17.310
there was some reason we were doing this

00:57:17.310 --> 00:57:19.200
potentially through investment.

00:57:19.200 --> 00:57:21.780
So I get a, like literally
order like a lifetime supply

00:57:21.780 --> 00:57:22.920
of this stuff.

00:57:22.920 --> 00:57:23.970
And it's pretty good, it's actually,

00:57:23.970 --> 00:57:25.410
it's a really nice little patch, 'cause I,

00:57:25.410 --> 00:57:26.730
the thing I didn't like about the gum was

00:57:26.730 --> 00:57:29.103
I hated just the taste of it.

00:57:31.110 --> 00:57:33.030
So then the next week I'm
talking to David and I'm like,

00:57:33.030 --> 00:57:34.860
by the way, I ordered
all that nicotine stuff

00:57:34.860 --> 00:57:35.693
you told me about.

00:57:35.693 --> 00:57:36.900
He's like what?

00:57:36.900 --> 00:57:39.930
And he goes, oh, the company's
name was something else.

00:57:39.930 --> 00:57:41.929
It was totally unrelated.

00:57:41.929 --> 00:57:43.656
It's like, oh God.

00:57:43.656 --> 00:57:48.600
So the short answer is, I
think this stuff is absolutely

00:57:48.600 --> 00:57:52.143
a concentration-enhancing substance.

00:57:53.040 --> 00:57:54.777
It is addictive and people
need to be wary of that.

00:57:54.777 --> 00:57:56.430
Now it's not addictive to everybody.

00:57:56.430 --> 00:58:00.600
I personally experience no
addiction to it whatsoever.

00:58:00.600 --> 00:58:04.290
So I can, I could do it
every day for 30 days

00:58:04.290 --> 00:58:06.420
and stop and experience no withdrawal.

00:58:06.420 --> 00:58:09.320
I could forget about it, it
doesn't really seem to matter.

00:58:10.560 --> 00:58:12.450
You have to be careful
with the dose, truthfully.

00:58:12.450 --> 00:58:14.610
I mean, remember one cigarette is about

00:58:14.610 --> 00:58:16.650
one milligram of nicotine,

00:58:16.650 --> 00:58:20.250
and a lot of these lozenges will plow

00:58:20.250 --> 00:58:23.430
four to eight milligrams
into you in one shot.

00:58:23.430 --> 00:58:28.430
And for someone who is, you
know, naive to that, like I am,

00:58:28.500 --> 00:58:31.410
four milligrams is a lot
of nicotine in one bolus.

00:58:31.410 --> 00:58:34.440
So you just have to be very mindful of it.

00:58:34.440 --> 00:58:38.610
I got a lot of flack when I did this AMA,

00:58:38.610 --> 00:58:41.790
for obvious reasons, but
people were like, how can you,

00:58:41.790 --> 00:58:43.860
as a doctor, encourage
people to use nicotine.

00:58:43.860 --> 00:58:44.940
And I was like, first of all,

00:58:44.940 --> 00:58:46.140
I'm not encouraging anybody to use it.

00:58:46.140 --> 00:58:49.040
I just want to be able to talk
about the biochemistry of it.

00:58:49.923 --> 00:58:52.220
And if disclosing that I
use it from time to time

00:58:52.220 --> 00:58:54.813
is an endorsement then
I apologize for that.

00:58:55.920 --> 00:58:58.290
But on the list of things that you can do

00:58:58.290 --> 00:59:01.110
to make your brain a little more focused,

00:59:01.110 --> 00:59:03.300
I would consider this infinitely safer

00:59:03.300 --> 00:59:04.560
than what a lot of people are doing,

00:59:04.560 --> 00:59:06.330
which is using stimulants.

00:59:06.330 --> 00:59:10.290
I mean, to me, you know, I
just tell patients outright,

00:59:10.290 --> 00:59:13.530
like we are under no circumstance
prescribing stimulants.

00:59:13.530 --> 00:59:16.410
I mean, yeah, we're not
giving anybody Adderall.

00:59:16.410 --> 00:59:20.010
We're not giving anybody
Vyvanse, or any of these things.

00:59:20.010 --> 00:59:22.050
Not to say they don't have
an appropriate clinical use,

00:59:22.050 --> 00:59:24.780
but they should be prescribed
under the care of somebody

00:59:24.780 --> 00:59:27.360
who's really monitoring
the use case for it.

00:59:27.360 --> 00:59:30.930
And using that as a tool
to enhance, you know,

00:59:30.930 --> 00:59:32.250
concentration in cognitive performance

00:59:32.250 --> 00:59:33.810
is not something we're comfortable doing.

00:59:33.810 --> 00:59:36.540
- Yeah, it's rampant on college campuses.

00:59:36.540 --> 00:59:37.830
- [Peter] I can only imagine.

00:59:37.830 --> 00:59:39.810
- Armodafinil, modafinil
which are slightly different,

00:59:39.810 --> 00:59:42.990
of course, but, so non-clinical use,

00:59:42.990 --> 00:59:46.080
not prescribed for ADHD,
but just, it's rampant.

00:59:46.080 --> 00:59:48.267
Recreational use, study-based use.

00:59:48.267 --> 00:59:50.490
- But the data I've seen on modafinil

00:59:50.490 --> 00:59:53.580
suggests that it only really
provides a nootropic benefit.

00:59:53.580 --> 00:59:55.470
in someone who is deprived of sleep.

00:59:55.470 --> 00:59:59.400
Is there data that in a
totally well-rested person,

00:59:59.400 --> 01:00:01.440
there is a nootropic benefit of modafinil?

01:00:01.440 --> 01:00:02.273
- I don't know.

01:00:02.273 --> 01:00:04.058
I have one experience with armodafinil,

01:00:04.058 --> 01:00:06.240
where I took a half a recommended dose,

01:00:06.240 --> 01:00:08.430
this was prescribed by a doctor.

01:00:08.430 --> 01:00:09.630
I went to give a talk.

01:00:09.630 --> 01:00:13.170
This was in Hawaii and I'm
four hours into the talk.

01:00:13.170 --> 01:00:16.200
My co-speaker came up to
me and just said, well,

01:00:16.200 --> 01:00:17.910
first of all, you got a little bit of spit

01:00:17.910 --> 01:00:18.810
in the corner of your mouth.

01:00:18.810 --> 01:00:20.850
And second of all, you haven't
blinked in three minutes.

01:00:20.850 --> 01:00:23.940
And third, there's only two
people left in the audience.

01:00:23.940 --> 01:00:27.540
I was so lazered in that
I kind forgot the context.

01:00:27.540 --> 01:00:29.940
Now I'm a little bit of a,
kind of a tunnel vision,

01:00:29.940 --> 01:00:33.630
OCD-type anyway, but one,
that was all it took,

01:00:33.630 --> 01:00:35.160
I never took any more of it.

01:00:35.160 --> 01:00:37.230
It was a powerful stimulant.

01:00:37.230 --> 01:00:41.400
I take 300 milligrams of
Alpha-GPC now and again,

01:00:41.400 --> 01:00:43.920
before some cognitive work,
sometimes before workouts,

01:00:43.920 --> 01:00:47.310
and I do subjectively feel
that it narrows my focus

01:00:47.310 --> 01:00:49.140
in a nice way.

01:00:49.140 --> 01:00:51.600
But I don't take it more
than once or twice a day,

01:00:51.600 --> 01:00:53.220
and more than once or twice a week.

01:00:53.220 --> 01:00:55.146
- See, this is an example of where,

01:00:55.146 --> 01:00:57.090
you know how we're talking
about exercise versus

01:00:57.090 --> 01:01:00.060
sort of nutrition and
supplements for longevity.

01:01:00.060 --> 01:01:01.590
I think there may be a
whole bunch of things

01:01:01.590 --> 01:01:03.630
that are kind of interesting around focus,

01:01:03.630 --> 01:01:06.210
but nothing would compare
to changing our environment.

01:01:06.210 --> 01:01:09.380
Like I think that if I
compare my focus today

01:01:09.380 --> 01:01:12.690
to my focus when I was in
college, there's no comparison.

01:01:12.690 --> 01:01:16.140
Like in college, I was truly a robot.

01:01:16.140 --> 01:01:17.970
But I think a large part of it was,

01:01:17.970 --> 01:01:19.410
there was no distraction.

01:01:19.410 --> 01:01:20.550
There's no email.

01:01:20.550 --> 01:01:21.870
There's no social media.

01:01:21.870 --> 01:01:22.793
There's no internet.

01:01:22.793 --> 01:01:25.800
I mean, I was in college
when Mosaic launched

01:01:25.800 --> 01:01:26.790
in the early '90s.

01:01:26.790 --> 01:01:29.880
Like I, you know, and you
had to walk like a mile

01:01:29.880 --> 01:01:32.580
to get to the computer lab
on a big Sun workstation

01:01:32.580 --> 01:01:36.360
to do anything in, you know,
some computer code language.

01:01:36.360 --> 01:01:39.930
So when you're sitting
in your room studying,

01:01:39.930 --> 01:01:41.310
there was no distraction.

01:01:41.310 --> 01:01:43.710
And I think that's a far greater component

01:01:43.710 --> 01:01:45.510
of what it means to be focused,

01:01:45.510 --> 01:01:46.590
than the challenges we have today.

01:01:46.590 --> 01:01:49.410
So, you know, my thoughts
on this would be,

01:01:49.410 --> 01:01:52.140
if we really wanted to
return to a state of focus,

01:01:52.140 --> 01:01:55.140
we're going to have to
individually do something about,

01:01:55.140 --> 01:01:56.190
you know, our environment.

01:01:56.190 --> 01:01:58.080
And I don't know what the answer is.

01:01:58.080 --> 01:02:00.960
Like, I've tried every
little trick I can think of,

01:02:00.960 --> 01:02:03.600
like closing my browsers
when I'm writing and stuff,

01:02:03.600 --> 01:02:05.730
but, you know, I'm just
not strong enough willed.

01:02:05.730 --> 01:02:08.040
Like I'll pick up my
phone every 20 minutes

01:02:08.040 --> 01:02:09.780
to look and see if I missed a text message

01:02:09.780 --> 01:02:10.890
or something stupid.

01:02:10.890 --> 01:02:12.090
- That's pretty infrequent.

01:02:12.090 --> 01:02:15.131
I did an episode on habits
and looking at the data.

01:02:15.131 --> 01:02:19.050
It seems that people
are getting interrupted

01:02:19.050 --> 01:02:22.560
or interrupting themselves
about once every three minutes

01:02:22.560 --> 01:02:25.230
in the typical workplace,
now that typical has changed

01:02:25.230 --> 01:02:27.090
with a lot more people working at home.

01:02:27.090 --> 01:02:28.887
I do put my phone away
when I try and work,

01:02:28.887 --> 01:02:31.800
but nothing focuses me like a deadline.

01:02:31.800 --> 01:02:34.200
A little bit of fear-based urgency.

01:02:34.200 --> 01:02:35.490
That's it, grant deadlines, you know,

01:02:35.490 --> 01:02:36.870
drop deadlines as I call them,

01:02:36.870 --> 01:02:38.760
or podcasts we're going to record today,

01:02:38.760 --> 01:02:40.680
that nothing works quite like it.

01:02:40.680 --> 01:02:42.960
But such is life.

01:02:42.960 --> 01:02:47.040
Well, thanks for that
offshoot about nicotine.

01:02:47.040 --> 01:02:50.430
Again, you're not recommending
it, I'm not recommending it,

01:02:50.430 --> 01:02:54.330
but it's clear that augmenting
the acetylcholine system,

01:02:54.330 --> 01:02:57.150
which is what nicotine
does in its various forms,

01:02:57.150 --> 01:02:59.790
and some related type pharmacology,

01:02:59.790 --> 01:03:01.860
does enhance focus and pretty potently.

01:03:01.860 --> 01:03:03.300
So I think it's going to
be an interesting area

01:03:03.300 --> 01:03:06.363
for real clinical trials
and things of that sort.

01:03:07.560 --> 01:03:10.440
Love to chat about hormone therapies,

01:03:10.440 --> 01:03:12.120
and hormones generally.

01:03:12.120 --> 01:03:14.010
When Robert Sapolsky came on the podcast,

01:03:14.010 --> 01:03:16.050
we talked a little bit about menopause

01:03:16.050 --> 01:03:17.070
and the data around menopause.

01:03:17.070 --> 01:03:20.610
He's very interested
in these findings that,

01:03:20.610 --> 01:03:22.170
I think I'm going to get this right,

01:03:22.170 --> 01:03:26.970
that whether or not women
benefit from estrogen therapy

01:03:26.970 --> 01:03:28.080
to offset menopause

01:03:28.080 --> 01:03:30.900
really depends on when
that therapy is initiated.

01:03:30.900 --> 01:03:32.250
I don't know if you're
aware of those data,

01:03:32.250 --> 01:03:36.720
but he claimed that if
they begin estrogen therapy

01:03:36.720 --> 01:03:40.080
in the middle to tail end of menopause,

01:03:40.080 --> 01:03:43.080
the outcomes can be quite bad.

01:03:43.080 --> 01:03:45.780
Whereas if they initiate
those estrogen therapies

01:03:45.780 --> 01:03:48.660
as they enter menopause
or even before menopause,

01:03:48.660 --> 01:03:50.700
then the outcomes can be quite good.

01:03:50.700 --> 01:03:52.620
I don't know what percentage
of the patients you treat

01:03:52.620 --> 01:03:53.820
are male versus female,

01:03:53.820 --> 01:03:55.890
and what ages those
patients are of course,

01:03:55.890 --> 01:03:59.280
but what are your thoughts about
estrogen therapy for women,

01:03:59.280 --> 01:04:02.370
menopause, and hormone
therapies generally for women,

01:04:02.370 --> 01:04:03.570
maybe even testosterone therapy,

01:04:03.570 --> 01:04:04.590
you hear about that these days,

01:04:04.590 --> 01:04:05.880
and then we'll talk about men.

01:04:05.880 --> 01:04:10.800
- So our practice is
probably 70 30 male female.

01:04:10.800 --> 01:04:12.540
So we have lots of women,

01:04:12.540 --> 01:04:15.210
and this is a very important topic.

01:04:15.210 --> 01:04:19.213
It's also probably, let me think.

01:04:19.213 --> 01:04:20.910
I just want to make sure
I'm not being hyperbolic

01:04:20.910 --> 01:04:21.783
when I say this.

01:04:22.710 --> 01:04:24.060
Yeah, I don't think I am.

01:04:24.060 --> 01:04:26.280
It's hands down the biggest screw up

01:04:26.280 --> 01:04:29.373
of the entire medical
field in the last 25 years.

01:04:30.549 --> 01:04:32.910
Now, again, it's possible in
the next hour I'll think of,

01:04:32.910 --> 01:04:34.860
nope, there's a bigger screw up.

01:04:34.860 --> 01:04:35.693
- Another giant screw up.

01:04:35.693 --> 01:04:37.380
- Yeah, but I don't think I will.

01:04:37.380 --> 01:04:41.370
I'm pretty confident that
I won't be able to think of

01:04:41.370 --> 01:04:46.063
a bigger act of incompetence

01:04:48.030 --> 01:04:50.550
than what happened with the
women's health initiative

01:04:50.550 --> 01:04:52.890
in the late '90s and early 2000s,

01:04:52.890 --> 01:04:55.800
which is effectively the study

01:04:55.800 --> 01:04:58.260
that turned the entire medical field

01:04:58.260 --> 01:05:00.870
off hormone replacement therapy for women.

01:05:00.870 --> 01:05:02.310
So it's important, I think,

01:05:02.310 --> 01:05:04.380
to explain what this study looked at.

01:05:04.380 --> 01:05:07.170
So this was a study that was conducted

01:05:07.170 --> 01:05:12.170
in response to the widely held
belief in the '70s and '80s

01:05:14.010 --> 01:05:16.980
that women should be placed on hormones

01:05:16.980 --> 01:05:19.560
as they're going through menopause, right?

01:05:19.560 --> 01:05:22.500
Menopause is, I guess maybe
I'll even take a step back.

01:05:22.500 --> 01:05:23.940
I don't know how much your
audience is familiar with

01:05:23.940 --> 01:05:25.380
how estrogen progesterone work.

01:05:25.380 --> 01:05:26.373
Is it worth going into that stuff?

01:05:26.373 --> 01:05:29.100
- Yeah probably worth mentioning
a bit of the top contour.

01:05:29.100 --> 01:05:30.420
Some of them might be familiar with it,

01:05:30.420 --> 01:05:33.240
we've done episodes on estrogen
testosterone, but frankly,

01:05:33.240 --> 01:05:34.080
as I think back to those,

01:05:34.080 --> 01:05:35.370
we didn't really go into the biology

01:05:35.370 --> 01:05:37.293
of estrogen testosterone enough.

01:05:38.130 --> 01:05:40.350
- Yeah, so, I mean, actually
an interesting aside

01:05:40.350 --> 01:05:41.790
that I always tell my female patients

01:05:41.790 --> 01:05:43.140
who get a kick out of this.

01:05:44.130 --> 01:05:47.340
When you look at a woman's
labs, you'll see her estrogen,

01:05:47.340 --> 01:05:50.910
her progesterone, her FSH,
her LH, her testosterone,

01:05:50.910 --> 01:05:52.830
her sex hormone binding
globulin, and all these things.

01:05:52.830 --> 01:05:55.320
But based on the units
they're reported in,

01:05:55.320 --> 01:05:57.450
it's a very distorting picture

01:05:57.450 --> 01:06:00.930
of what the most common
androgen is in her body.

01:06:00.930 --> 01:06:02.940
If you actually convert
them to the same units,

01:06:02.940 --> 01:06:05.790
she has much more testosterone
in her body than estrogen.

01:06:07.290 --> 01:06:09.017
- Interesting.
- Yeah.

01:06:09.017 --> 01:06:09.960
- I did not know that.
- Yeah.

01:06:09.960 --> 01:06:11.490
- Then again, I've never been a woman

01:06:11.490 --> 01:06:12.580
getting my hormone profile.

01:06:12.580 --> 01:06:14.110
- Yeah, yeah.

01:06:14.110 --> 01:06:14.943
So even though a woman's testosterone

01:06:14.943 --> 01:06:18.720
is much less than a man's level,

01:06:18.720 --> 01:06:21.696
it's still more than she
has estrogen in her body.

01:06:21.696 --> 01:06:22.592
- [Andrew] Wow!

01:06:22.592 --> 01:06:23.425
- So phenotypically, right,

01:06:23.425 --> 01:06:25.890
estrogen is the hormone
that's dominating and test,

01:06:25.890 --> 01:06:28.350
so it's the, she has much
higher estrogen than a man

01:06:28.350 --> 01:06:30.330
and much lower testosterone than a man,

01:06:30.330 --> 01:06:31.830
but in absolute amounts,

01:06:31.830 --> 01:06:33.450
she has more testosterone than estrogen.

01:06:33.450 --> 01:06:34.500
Just worth pointing that out.

01:06:34.500 --> 01:06:35.698
- [Andrew] Incredible.

01:06:35.698 --> 01:06:38.550
- So, you know, what's
happening to a woman

01:06:38.550 --> 01:06:40.110
from the age she starts menstruating

01:06:40.110 --> 01:06:41.850
until she goes through menopause,

01:06:41.850 --> 01:06:44.250
outside of pregnancy and birth
control and stuff like that,

01:06:44.250 --> 01:06:46.860
is she has this cycle, you
know, roughly every 28 days,

01:06:46.860 --> 01:06:50.160
but it can vary, where at
the beginning of her period,

01:06:50.160 --> 01:06:52.140
we call that day zero, her basic,

01:06:52.140 --> 01:06:55.020
her estrogen and
progesterone are very low.

01:06:55.020 --> 01:06:56.490
You can't measure them.

01:06:56.490 --> 01:07:00.960
And then what happens is the
estrogen level starts to rise,

01:07:00.960 --> 01:07:04.579
and it rises in response
to a hormone called

01:07:04.579 --> 01:07:07.830
follicle stimulating hormone, FSH,

01:07:07.830 --> 01:07:09.570
that is getting her ready to ovulate.

01:07:09.570 --> 01:07:11.670
And she ovulates at about
the midpoint of her cycle.

01:07:11.670 --> 01:07:13.440
So if we're just going
to make the math easy,

01:07:13.440 --> 01:07:16.050
on day 14 she's going
to release a follicle

01:07:16.050 --> 01:07:17.850
from one of her ovaries.

01:07:17.850 --> 01:07:20.910
And the estrogen level is sort
of rising, rising, rising.

01:07:20.910 --> 01:07:24.150
We love to measure hormones on day five,

01:07:24.150 --> 01:07:26.310
because I want to have a standardized way

01:07:26.310 --> 01:07:27.900
in which I measure her hormones.

01:07:27.900 --> 01:07:30.810
So our women know if we're in the business

01:07:30.810 --> 01:07:32.100
of trying to understand her hormones,

01:07:32.100 --> 01:07:33.330
the day her period starts,

01:07:33.330 --> 01:07:34.710
even if it's just a day of spotting,

01:07:34.710 --> 01:07:37.050
that becomes our benchmark,
and then day five,

01:07:37.050 --> 01:07:39.240
I want to see every hormone on that day.

01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:41.010
And if everything is going well,

01:07:41.010 --> 01:07:43.890
I know what her FSH, LH,
estradiol and progesterone

01:07:43.890 --> 01:07:45.390
should be on that day.

01:07:45.390 --> 01:07:48.210
So the estrogen rises, starts
to come down a little bit

01:07:48.210 --> 01:07:51.570
as she ovulates, and then the
luteinizing hormone kicks on

01:07:51.570 --> 01:07:54.750
because it's now going
to prepare her uterus

01:07:54.750 --> 01:07:59.160
for the lining to accommodate a pregnancy.

01:07:59.160 --> 01:08:01.470
So now you start to see estradiol go back,

01:08:01.470 --> 01:08:03.300
but now, for the first
time, progesterone goes up.

01:08:03.300 --> 01:08:06.420
So progesterone has been
doing nothing for 14 days,

01:08:06.420 --> 01:08:07.710
and now it starts to rise.

01:08:07.710 --> 01:08:09.330
And actually progesterone is the hormone

01:08:09.330 --> 01:08:11.040
that's dominating the second half,

01:08:11.040 --> 01:08:13.290
which is called her luteal cycle.

01:08:13.290 --> 01:08:15.570
So the first 14 days is
the follicular cycle.

01:08:15.570 --> 01:08:17.520
Second is the luteal cycle.

01:08:17.520 --> 01:08:20.040
So once you get to about
the halfway point of that,

01:08:20.040 --> 01:08:23.040
which is now, just to
do the math, 21 days in,

01:08:23.040 --> 01:08:25.380
the body has figured out
if she's pregnant or not.

01:08:25.380 --> 01:08:27.750
And again, most of the time
she's not going to be pregnant.

01:08:27.750 --> 01:08:28.680
So the body says, oh,

01:08:28.680 --> 01:08:30.990
I don't need this lining
that I've been preparing,

01:08:30.990 --> 01:08:32.250
I'm going to shed it.

01:08:32.250 --> 01:08:35.820
So now progesterone and
estrogen start crashing,

01:08:35.820 --> 01:08:37.560
and the lining is what is being shed,

01:08:37.560 --> 01:08:38.710
and that is the menses.

01:08:39.630 --> 01:08:42.630
By the way, it's that last
seven days of that cycle,

01:08:42.630 --> 01:08:45.570
that in a susceptible
woman is what creates

01:08:45.570 --> 01:08:47.280
those PMS symptoms.

01:08:47.280 --> 01:08:48.513
So it's the, actually, this is something

01:08:48.513 --> 01:08:49.590
that you would probably have

01:08:49.590 --> 01:08:51.330
a better understanding of than me.

01:08:51.330 --> 01:08:54.780
There is something about
this in a susceptible woman,

01:08:54.780 --> 01:08:59.070
where the enormous reduction
of progesterone so quickly

01:08:59.070 --> 01:09:01.890
is probably impacting
something in her brain.

01:09:01.890 --> 01:09:04.020
So this is a legitimate thing, right?

01:09:04.020 --> 01:09:05.838
I mean, you know, it's not like,

01:09:05.838 --> 01:09:08.220
oh, she's crazy because she's
having all these PMS symptoms.

01:09:08.220 --> 01:09:09.330
No.

01:09:09.330 --> 01:09:10.440
We know that that's the case

01:09:10.440 --> 01:09:12.240
because if you put women on progesterone

01:09:12.240 --> 01:09:14.757
for those seven days,
those symptoms go away.

01:09:14.757 --> 01:09:15.637
- [Andrew] Interesting.

01:09:15.637 --> 01:09:16.650
- So if you can stabilize
their progesterone

01:09:16.650 --> 01:09:19.650
during the last half
of their luteal phase,

01:09:19.650 --> 01:09:20.670
and sometimes we would just do it

01:09:20.670 --> 01:09:21.840
for the entire luteal phase,

01:09:21.840 --> 01:09:24.120
just put them on a low
dose of progesterone,

01:09:24.120 --> 01:09:25.440
all PMS symptoms vanish.

01:09:25.440 --> 01:09:26.273
- Very interesting.

01:09:26.273 --> 01:09:28.710
I'll have to look up where
the progesterone receptors

01:09:28.710 --> 01:09:29.550
are located in the brain.

01:09:29.550 --> 01:09:33.510
The Allen Brain Institute
now has beautiful data of

01:09:33.510 --> 01:09:35.388
in situ hybridization, which for,

01:09:35.388 --> 01:09:36.240
'cause they don't understand is,

01:09:36.240 --> 01:09:39.810
looking at RNA and sort of
where genes and proteins

01:09:39.810 --> 01:09:41.340
ought to be expressed in the human brain,

01:09:41.340 --> 01:09:43.380
by using actual human
brain tissue sections

01:09:43.380 --> 01:09:44.310
as opposed to just mice.

01:09:44.310 --> 01:09:45.390
So I'll take a look, I think-

01:09:45.390 --> 01:09:46.879
- Yeah, I'm really

01:09:46.879 --> 01:09:48.150
curious, yeah.
- Some insight into what that

01:09:48.150 --> 01:09:50.487
progesterone emotionality link might be,

01:09:50.487 --> 01:09:52.920
and where it might exist,
neural circuit wise.

01:09:52.920 --> 01:09:55.470
- So then, when the
estrogen and progesterone

01:09:55.470 --> 01:09:58.500
reach their nadir again,
that starts the cycle.

01:09:58.500 --> 01:09:59.940
So that just, that cycle is happening

01:09:59.940 --> 01:10:02.100
over and over and over again.

01:10:02.100 --> 01:10:07.100
Okay, so it became well
known in the '50s that, okay,

01:10:07.500 --> 01:10:09.480
a woman's going to stop
menstruating at some point,

01:10:09.480 --> 01:10:10.530
her estrogen goes down.

01:10:10.530 --> 01:10:11.970
Why don't we just give her estrogen?

01:10:11.970 --> 01:10:13.920
'Cause that's clearly going to help

01:10:13.920 --> 01:10:15.480
with some of the symptoms of menopause.

01:10:15.480 --> 01:10:18.090
So what do women experience
when they go through menopause?

01:10:18.090 --> 01:10:20.490
The first symptoms are what
are called vasomotor symptoms.

01:10:20.490 --> 01:10:25.470
So this is usually in the form
of night sweats, hot flashes.

01:10:25.470 --> 01:10:27.000
So, and depending on the woman,

01:10:27.000 --> 01:10:28.680
this can be really significant, right?

01:10:28.680 --> 01:10:31.740
These are women who can
have a hard time sleeping.

01:10:31.740 --> 01:10:33.540
They can be having hot flashes
during the middle of the day.

01:10:33.540 --> 01:10:36.003
They can wake up soaked
in a pool of sweat.

01:10:37.320 --> 01:10:40.740
Those tend to pass
after a couple of years,

01:10:40.740 --> 01:10:43.140
and then they get into sort of the more

01:10:43.140 --> 01:10:45.300
long-term complications of menopause.

01:10:45.300 --> 01:10:48.660
So what we call vaginal
atrophy, vaginal dryness,

01:10:48.660 --> 01:10:50.790
and then the stuff that we
talked about a while ago,

01:10:50.790 --> 01:10:54.150
which is the osteopenia osteoporosis.

01:10:54.150 --> 01:10:56.733
A lot of women will complain of brain fog.

01:10:57.900 --> 01:11:01.770
So, I mean, clearly this was an issue

01:11:01.770 --> 01:11:04.710
and it was recognized 70 years ago.

01:11:04.710 --> 01:11:06.780
Why don't we give women estrogen back

01:11:06.780 --> 01:11:08.103
to replace that hormone?

01:11:09.144 --> 01:11:12.720
And so that went on for a
couple of decades, maybe less,

01:11:12.720 --> 01:11:14.760
maybe a decade, and then it
was realized, wait a minute,

01:11:14.760 --> 01:11:17.943
we were driving up the
risk of uterine cancer.

01:11:20.190 --> 01:11:23.430
And the reason for that is
if you just give estrogen

01:11:23.430 --> 01:11:26.700
with no progesterone to antagonize it,

01:11:26.700 --> 01:11:29.970
you will thicken the endometrium endlessly

01:11:29.970 --> 01:11:33.210
and you will increase
the risk of hyperplasia.

01:11:33.210 --> 01:11:35.160
Well, you'll definitely
undergo hyperplasia,

01:11:35.160 --> 01:11:36.570
and then ultimately dysplasia.

01:11:36.570 --> 01:11:38.010
Dysplasia is precancerous,

01:11:38.010 --> 01:11:39.750
and ultimately we were seeing that.

01:11:39.750 --> 01:11:42.120
So people figured out, well, actually,

01:11:42.120 --> 01:11:44.400
if you want to give estrogen to a woman

01:11:44.400 --> 01:11:45.450
who still has her uterus,

01:11:45.450 --> 01:11:47.520
you have to give her progesterone as well.

01:11:47.520 --> 01:11:49.350
You have to be able to have a hormone

01:11:49.350 --> 01:11:50.850
to oppose the estrogen.

01:11:50.850 --> 01:11:55.770
And then that became effectively
in, call it the 1970s-ish,

01:11:55.770 --> 01:11:59.643
the standard for HRT.

01:12:00.840 --> 01:12:04.740
So in the early 1990s the NIH said, look,

01:12:04.740 --> 01:12:06.840
we haven't really studied this.

01:12:06.840 --> 01:12:10.380
We have a ton of epidemiology that says

01:12:10.380 --> 01:12:14.100
giving women hormones seems to
be doing really good things.

01:12:14.100 --> 01:12:17.700
They feel better, so all
their symptoms go away.

01:12:17.700 --> 01:12:20.130
They seem to have lower
risk of heart disease,

01:12:20.130 --> 01:12:23.520
lower risk of, you know,
cardiovascular, pardon me,

01:12:23.520 --> 01:12:24.720
lower risk of cardiovascular disease,

01:12:24.720 --> 01:12:27.453
lower risk of bone fractures.

01:12:28.500 --> 01:12:29.400
Everything seems to get better.

01:12:29.400 --> 01:12:30.360
Lower risk of diabetes.

01:12:30.360 --> 01:12:31.560
But we haven't tested this

01:12:31.560 --> 01:12:34.560
in a randomized prospective
trial, so let's do this.

01:12:34.560 --> 01:12:36.510
So that became the WHI.

01:12:36.510 --> 01:12:39.180
And it randomized, it
had two parallel arms.

01:12:39.180 --> 01:12:44.180
So it had a group for women
who did not have a uterus.

01:12:44.580 --> 01:12:46.170
So these are women that
had undergone hysterectomy

01:12:46.170 --> 01:12:47.070
for some other reason.

01:12:47.070 --> 01:12:48.510
And then it had a group for women

01:12:48.510 --> 01:12:50.070
that did have their uterus.

01:12:50.070 --> 01:12:53.250
In the first group,
there was a placebo arm

01:12:53.250 --> 01:12:55.200
and then an estrogen only arm.

01:12:55.200 --> 01:12:56.250
And in the other group,

01:12:56.250 --> 01:12:59.643
there was a progesterone plus
estrogen versus a placebo.

01:13:02.160 --> 01:13:05.370
Everything about the way this
study was done is a bit wonky.

01:13:05.370 --> 01:13:08.850
Some of it is justifiable, but
it's important to understand.

01:13:08.850 --> 01:13:13.380
First, the women were all
way outside of menopause.

01:13:13.380 --> 01:13:16.470
So none of these women were started

01:13:16.470 --> 01:13:18.423
when you would normally start HRT.

01:13:19.530 --> 01:13:23.040
And there were probably
several reasons for that,

01:13:23.040 --> 01:13:26.880
but one of them is, and I think
this is a legitimate reason,

01:13:26.880 --> 01:13:28.920
they wanted hard outcomes.

01:13:28.920 --> 01:13:31.339
They wanted to know death rates.

01:13:31.339 --> 01:13:33.870
And if you're doing this
on women in their 50s,

01:13:33.870 --> 01:13:35.520
you just weren't going to get it, right.

01:13:35.520 --> 01:13:36.353
You couldn't-

01:13:36.353 --> 01:13:37.186
- Wait too long.
- Yeah,

01:13:37.186 --> 01:13:38.292
you got to wait too long.

01:13:38.292 --> 01:13:40.320
And this was only going to be
like a seven to 10-year study.

01:13:40.320 --> 01:13:43.200
So they had to do this on
women who were much older.

01:13:43.200 --> 01:13:47.100
They also disproportionately
took much sicker women.

01:13:47.100 --> 01:13:49.140
I believe the prevalence, and again,

01:13:49.140 --> 01:13:50.280
I'm going to get some
of these numbers wrong

01:13:50.280 --> 01:13:53.322
and people are going to get all
phosphorylated, but, you know,

01:13:53.322 --> 01:13:54.420
I mean, I'm in the ballpark, right?

01:13:54.420 --> 01:13:57.240
Something like 30, 40% of
these women were smokers.

01:13:57.240 --> 01:14:00.510
The prevalence of obesity,
diabetes was enormous.

01:14:00.510 --> 01:14:02.760
So they really disproportionately picked

01:14:02.760 --> 01:14:04.620
the most unhealthy population they could,

01:14:04.620 --> 01:14:06.420
that was pretty advanced in age.

01:14:06.420 --> 01:14:08.850
And again, I think part
of that was to say, look,

01:14:08.850 --> 01:14:10.470
we want to make sure
that after seven years,

01:14:10.470 --> 01:14:12.210
we really know if there's a difference

01:14:12.210 --> 01:14:13.593
in these causes of death.

01:14:15.690 --> 01:14:18.930
The other thing is, this is kind of weird,

01:14:18.930 --> 01:14:21.360
although again, I understand
their rationale for it,

01:14:21.360 --> 01:14:25.110
but this is a great
example of be very careful

01:14:25.110 --> 01:14:27.330
when you look at a clinical trial,

01:14:27.330 --> 01:14:29.430
that it remotely represents the patients

01:14:29.430 --> 01:14:30.930
you're interested in treating.

01:14:30.930 --> 01:14:33.843
So they also treated no
patients who were symptomatic.

01:14:35.430 --> 01:14:38.280
The rationale being, if
we include in the study

01:14:38.280 --> 01:14:39.780
patients who are symptomatic,

01:14:39.780 --> 01:14:43.113
those who are randomized
to placebo will drop out.

01:14:45.210 --> 01:14:47.490
- Okay, it makes sense
in terms of study design,

01:14:47.490 --> 01:14:51.420
makes no sense if the study
design is intended to mimic

01:14:51.420 --> 01:14:52.253
the real world.

01:14:52.253 --> 01:14:53.309
- That's right.

01:14:53.309 --> 01:14:54.540
So now let's just keep
track of the three issues.

01:14:54.540 --> 01:14:57.755
We have a disproportionately
unhealthy patient population

01:14:57.755 --> 01:15:00.003
who are not symptomatic,

01:15:00.840 --> 01:15:03.873
and we're starting them more
than 10 years after menopause.

01:15:05.670 --> 01:15:07.440
The next thing that they did, which again,

01:15:07.440 --> 01:15:09.450
I understand why they did it,

01:15:09.450 --> 01:15:13.650
but it's now the fourth
strike against this study, is,

01:15:13.650 --> 01:15:16.110
and I've spoken with the PI of the study

01:15:16.110 --> 01:15:17.730
and asked this question point blank.

01:15:17.730 --> 01:15:20.280
I'm actually going to have her
on my podcast at some point soon

01:15:20.280 --> 01:15:22.620
to go over this in more detail,

01:15:22.620 --> 01:15:26.970
is why did you use conjugated
equine estrogen, an MPA,

01:15:26.970 --> 01:15:29.340
which is a synthetic form of progesterone.

01:15:29.340 --> 01:15:30.390
- [Andrew] Horse-

01:15:30.390 --> 01:15:31.980
- Yes.
- estrogen?

01:15:31.980 --> 01:15:34.350
- It's horse urine, is
they collect horse urine,

01:15:34.350 --> 01:15:36.240
so they're getting, it's-

01:15:36.240 --> 01:15:37.800
- Horses do urinate a lot.

01:15:37.800 --> 01:15:38.970
Or at least when they urinate,

01:15:38.970 --> 01:15:40.560
it seems like a large volume of urine,

01:15:40.560 --> 01:15:42.119
from what I've observed.

01:15:42.119 --> 01:15:43.077
- [Peter] You have a lot
of experience with this?

01:15:43.077 --> 01:15:44.460
- No, but, you know,

01:15:44.460 --> 01:15:46.470
my sister rode horses for a little while,

01:15:46.470 --> 01:15:49.761
my high school girlfriend
had a horse, and that thing,

01:15:49.761 --> 01:15:52.563
I mean the pees were legendary.

01:15:53.820 --> 01:15:54.870
It's a male horse.

01:15:54.870 --> 01:15:56.100
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:15:56.100 --> 01:15:58.740
- So, yeah, so the
conjugated equine estrogen

01:15:58.740 --> 01:16:02.280
is the estrogen that's
collected from female horses.

01:16:02.280 --> 01:16:04.233
And then it's a synthetic progesterone.

01:16:05.070 --> 01:16:08.130
And I said to the person, I said, well,

01:16:08.130 --> 01:16:10.170
why didn't you use what we use today,

01:16:10.170 --> 01:16:12.510
which is bio-identical
estrogen in progesterone.

01:16:12.510 --> 01:16:16.050
Like today, when we put
women on estrogen, we use a,

01:16:16.050 --> 01:16:18.030
it's an FDA product
called the Vivelle-Dot.

01:16:18.030 --> 01:16:21.090
So it's a patch that you just
put on, and it's estradiol,

01:16:21.090 --> 01:16:22.500
but it's bioidentical estradiol.

01:16:22.500 --> 01:16:24.390
And we use what's called
micronized progesterone.

01:16:24.390 --> 01:16:26.610
So bio-identical progesterone.

01:16:26.610 --> 01:16:30.450
And she said, well, at the
time we just wanted to test

01:16:30.450 --> 01:16:31.590
what was currently being used.

01:16:31.590 --> 01:16:32.970
And I said, totally makes sense.

01:16:32.970 --> 01:16:35.700
But again, now you have
four considerations

01:16:35.700 --> 01:16:37.830
that you have to keep in mind, okay.

01:16:37.830 --> 01:16:40.260
So despite those four considerations,

01:16:40.260 --> 01:16:43.680
and I'm going to make a case
for you why I think the MPA

01:16:43.680 --> 01:16:45.870
created a real problem in that study,

01:16:45.870 --> 01:16:47.910
the synthetic progesterone,

01:16:47.910 --> 01:16:52.910
when the preliminary results
were first made available,

01:16:54.150 --> 01:16:56.730
but not yet peer-reviewed
and not yet published,

01:16:56.730 --> 01:17:00.750
there was a huge fiasco, huge
press announcement about it,

01:17:00.750 --> 01:17:05.750
suggesting that the women
receiving the CEE plus MPA,

01:17:07.680 --> 01:17:10.500
in the group with the uterus,

01:17:10.500 --> 01:17:13.200
had a higher incidence of breast cancer.

01:17:13.200 --> 01:17:16.530
And that basically became the
headline that never went away,

01:17:16.530 --> 01:17:19.110
though it turned out not to be true.

01:17:19.110 --> 01:17:20.610
Let's talk about the numbers.

01:17:20.610 --> 01:17:23.820
What was the increase in
the risk of breast cancer

01:17:23.820 --> 01:17:24.653
in that group?

01:17:24.653 --> 01:17:26.280
Which gets to one of my, you know,

01:17:26.280 --> 01:17:28.620
if you ever listen to me on
a podcast rail on something.

01:17:28.620 --> 01:17:32.070
- Listen, I have about 3,800
pet peeves and counting.

01:17:32.070 --> 01:17:35.737
My laboratory staff know these,
know a good number of them.

01:17:35.737 --> 01:17:36.885
So, you do not have
- Here, I'll add to

01:17:36.885 --> 01:17:37.718
one of them.
- to apologize

01:17:37.718 --> 01:17:38.850
for having many pet peeves.

01:17:38.850 --> 01:17:41.310
Because as long as they
have experience in data

01:17:41.310 --> 01:17:42.990
to support them, it provides-

01:17:42.990 --> 01:17:47.010
- So one of my biggest pet
peeves is, and my team knows this

01:17:47.010 --> 01:17:48.840
'cause sometimes they'll
occasionally, you know,

01:17:48.840 --> 01:17:50.610
they'll do this and I'll
have to remind them,

01:17:50.610 --> 01:17:52.860
you never talk about
a relative risk change

01:17:52.860 --> 01:17:55.080
without an absolute risk
accommodating it, right.

01:17:55.080 --> 01:17:57.200
So, what does that look like?

01:17:57.200 --> 01:18:00.330
So the relative risk
increase of breast cancer

01:18:00.330 --> 01:18:04.350
in the estrogen plus MPA
group versus the placebo

01:18:04.350 --> 01:18:08.509
was 25, 27%.

01:18:08.509 --> 01:18:10.209
And that became the only headline.

01:18:11.160 --> 01:18:15.420
HRT increases risk of
breast cancer by 27%.

01:18:15.420 --> 01:18:19.860
Now, I don't think
that's true at all today,

01:18:19.860 --> 01:18:21.270
but let's even look at the data.

01:18:21.270 --> 01:18:22.950
What was the ARR?

01:18:22.950 --> 01:18:25.770
What was the absolute risk increase?

01:18:25.770 --> 01:18:29.490
It was a difference between
five cases per thousand

01:18:29.490 --> 01:18:31.143
and four cases per thousand.

01:18:31.980 --> 01:18:36.980
So the ARR was 0.1%,
one case in a thousand.

01:18:39.412 --> 01:18:41.520
And it's true, going
from four in a thousand

01:18:41.520 --> 01:18:45.660
to five in a thousand is a 25% increase,

01:18:45.660 --> 01:18:48.360
but it's a completely
inappropriate context.

01:18:48.360 --> 01:18:51.930
- I agree, and I feel like
headlines of that sort,

01:18:51.930 --> 01:18:53.220
which have come up recently around

01:18:53.220 --> 01:18:55.560
various dietary interventions,
we won't go there,

01:18:55.560 --> 01:18:57.930
at least not for the time being,

01:18:57.930 --> 01:18:59.430
are nothing short of criminal

01:18:59.430 --> 01:19:03.030
because they really
distort people's thinking.

01:19:03.030 --> 01:19:06.510
But also they steer the course
of science and medicine for,

01:19:06.510 --> 01:19:09.780
as you pointed out, for
decades, if not longer.

01:19:09.780 --> 01:19:12.150
And they can really take
us off our health track

01:19:12.150 --> 01:19:13.740
in serious ways.

01:19:13.740 --> 01:19:17.040
- So I'll bring this meandering
to a close, which is to say,

01:19:17.040 --> 01:19:18.480
even though I could spend the next hour

01:19:18.480 --> 01:19:19.980
talking about all of the ways

01:19:19.980 --> 01:19:21.840
in which this study was flawed,

01:19:21.840 --> 01:19:24.510
and all of the very unethical
things that were done

01:19:24.510 --> 01:19:26.160
by a number of the investigators

01:19:26.160 --> 01:19:30.330
who went out of their way to
mask the truth of this study

01:19:30.330 --> 01:19:33.423
from the world, I'll tell a woman today,

01:19:34.800 --> 01:19:36.030
we're going to start you on this

01:19:36.030 --> 01:19:37.440
when you're going through menopause.

01:19:37.440 --> 01:19:41.550
We're using bio-identical hormones.

01:19:41.550 --> 01:19:45.930
And if your upper bound
risk of breast cancer

01:19:45.930 --> 01:19:48.630
is one case in a thousand,

01:19:48.630 --> 01:19:50.310
you should at least weigh that against

01:19:50.310 --> 01:19:52.020
all of the other benefits,
which I'll talk about.

01:19:52.020 --> 01:19:53.430
Now, there's something else I want to say,

01:19:53.430 --> 01:19:55.123
because a moment ago I
alluded to the fact that

01:19:55.123 --> 01:19:57.150
I think the MPA might have
been the biggest issue

01:19:57.150 --> 01:19:57.990
in that study.

01:19:57.990 --> 01:20:01.470
So there were two findings in
that study that were negative.

01:20:01.470 --> 01:20:04.980
One was the small increase
in the risk of heart disease,

01:20:04.980 --> 01:20:07.800
and the small increase in
the risk of breast cancer.

01:20:07.800 --> 01:20:09.900
But consider the other group,

01:20:09.900 --> 01:20:12.630
we forgot about the group
that didn't have a uterus.

01:20:12.630 --> 01:20:16.290
'Cause remember, those
women got estrogen only

01:20:16.290 --> 01:20:18.030
versus placebo.

01:20:18.030 --> 01:20:20.850
What was the difference
in breast cancer there?

01:20:20.850 --> 01:20:22.440
Well, this is interesting
'cause it didn't reach

01:20:22.440 --> 01:20:27.060
statistical significance, but
its P value was 0.06 or 0.07.

01:20:27.060 --> 01:20:30.303
So it came very close, but it
was in the opposite direction.

01:20:31.410 --> 01:20:34.230
It was a 24% risk reduction,

01:20:34.230 --> 01:20:35.943
about one in a thousand as well.

01:20:36.780 --> 01:20:39.600
So when you had estrogen plus MPA,

01:20:39.600 --> 01:20:42.420
you had a barely
statistically significant,

01:20:42.420 --> 01:20:43.890
the P value was 0.05,

01:20:43.890 --> 01:20:45.900
so it just hit statistical significance,

01:20:45.900 --> 01:20:49.050
one in a thousand cases for breast cancer.

01:20:49.050 --> 01:20:51.750
And then you had one in a thousand cases,

01:20:51.750 --> 01:20:56.490
but P value of 0.07, for reduction
of risk of breast cancer.

01:20:56.490 --> 01:20:58.320
Which to me suggests that the MPA,

01:20:58.320 --> 01:20:59.910
the synthetic progesterone,

01:20:59.910 --> 01:21:02.790
was playing more of a
role than anything else.

01:21:02.790 --> 01:21:06.180
The second thing I point
out is oral estrogen,

01:21:06.180 --> 01:21:10.560
which we no longer use,
does increase coagulability.

01:21:10.560 --> 01:21:12.900
It does increase the ability of the blood

01:21:12.900 --> 01:21:14.280
to clot a little bit.

01:21:14.280 --> 01:21:18.090
And when we look at the
more recent data on HRT,

01:21:18.090 --> 01:21:21.990
using topical estrogen
or patches of estrogen,

01:21:21.990 --> 01:21:22.920
we don't see that at all.

01:21:22.920 --> 01:21:24.060
In fact, we see the opposite now.

01:21:24.060 --> 01:21:25.350
So now we see the risk of heart disease

01:21:25.350 --> 01:21:27.750
going down in women with estradiol.

01:21:27.750 --> 01:21:30.060
- And some women will be
arriving to those treatments

01:21:30.060 --> 01:21:32.640
with mutations and things
like Factor V Leiden

01:21:32.640 --> 01:21:34.140
and other clotting factors.

01:21:34.140 --> 01:21:37.530
Is it appropriate to say that everyone,

01:21:37.530 --> 01:21:39.870
both male and female, should
know whether or not they have

01:21:39.870 --> 01:21:41.970
mutant forms of Factor V Leiden?

01:21:41.970 --> 01:21:44.370
- You know, we don't typically
test people for Factor V.

01:21:44.370 --> 01:21:45.330
My wife actually has it,

01:21:45.330 --> 01:21:47.760
but we didn't learn it
until she had HELLP syndrome

01:21:47.760 --> 01:21:49.510
giving birth to our first daughter.

01:21:50.370 --> 01:21:53.070
But, you know, we kind of look for

01:21:53.070 --> 01:21:56.070
more family history reason to
be testing things like that.

01:21:56.070 --> 01:21:57.420
We take a pretty detailed family history,

01:21:57.420 --> 01:21:59.910
so we'll kind of look for
clotting issues there.

01:21:59.910 --> 01:22:03.630
- What about, so your reflex nowadays

01:22:03.630 --> 01:22:08.520
is to put women on these topical

01:22:08.520 --> 01:22:09.513
estrogen therapies?
- Well it's to basically

01:22:09.513 --> 01:22:11.130
have the discussion, right?

01:22:11.130 --> 01:22:13.880
So here's where we still
struggle, right, is, you know,

01:22:15.090 --> 01:22:18.420
if it were up to me, I'd
prefer for a woman's HRT

01:22:18.420 --> 01:22:20.820
to be provided by her GYN,

01:22:20.820 --> 01:22:23.490
because we want to be able to
work in partnership with the GYN

01:22:23.490 --> 01:22:24.570
who we would like to see

01:22:24.570 --> 01:22:27.480
an endometrial ultrasound done every year.

01:22:27.480 --> 01:22:29.370
That's, you know, some
would argue that's overkill,

01:22:29.370 --> 01:22:31.320
but we think she should
be having a pap smear

01:22:31.320 --> 01:22:32.460
every year as well.

01:22:32.460 --> 01:22:34.020
So if we're looking at the cervix,

01:22:34.020 --> 01:22:35.100
we want to look at the endometrium,

01:22:35.100 --> 01:22:37.080
we want to make sure the
lining isn't too thick.

01:22:37.080 --> 01:22:39.390
The other thing I should
say, Andrew, is today,

01:22:39.390 --> 01:22:43.050
we now realize that not all
women can tolerate estro,

01:22:43.050 --> 01:22:44.790
pardon me, progesterone.

01:22:44.790 --> 01:22:45.810
So you have to be careful.

01:22:45.810 --> 01:22:48.570
So assuming, again, a
woman still has her uterus,

01:22:48.570 --> 01:22:51.150
the estrogen solves most of the problems,

01:22:51.150 --> 01:22:52.320
but then you have to decide,

01:22:52.320 --> 01:22:54.360
can she tolerate the progesterone?

01:22:54.360 --> 01:22:56.340
And it needs to be, if given systemically

01:22:56.340 --> 01:22:58.500
like a hundred to 200 milligrams.

01:22:58.500 --> 01:23:01.170
And for some women, that is
a lifesaving intervention.

01:23:01.170 --> 01:23:03.540
I mean, they start sleeping
better, their hair gets thicker,

01:23:03.540 --> 01:23:04.710
they feel better.

01:23:04.710 --> 01:23:07.500
But for some women, it
literally drives them crazy.

01:23:07.500 --> 01:23:10.020
It's probably the reciprocal
of what we were seeing

01:23:10.020 --> 01:23:12.090
in the case of women with PMS.

01:23:12.090 --> 01:23:15.270
So in those situations we say, great,

01:23:15.270 --> 01:23:16.770
we're done with oral progesterone.

01:23:16.770 --> 01:23:19.320
We just use a progesterone coated IUD.

01:23:19.320 --> 01:23:22.770
So then you get the local
progesterone in the uterus

01:23:22.770 --> 01:23:25.980
for protection, and the systemic estrogen.

01:23:25.980 --> 01:23:26.880
- Fascinating.

01:23:26.880 --> 01:23:30.693
What about oral contraception in women?

01:23:33.480 --> 01:23:36.150
So the use of estrogen
chronically through people's,

01:23:36.150 --> 01:23:38.460
you know, college years or 20s, 30s,

01:23:38.460 --> 01:23:39.860
maybe even teens, who knows.

01:23:41.010 --> 01:23:44.620
What's known about the
long-term effects, if any?

01:23:44.620 --> 01:23:45.453
- I got to be honest with you,

01:23:45.453 --> 01:23:47.610
I don't think I know
enough to comment on it.

01:23:47.610 --> 01:23:48.810
It's not something that really impacts

01:23:48.810 --> 01:23:50.253
my patient population.

01:23:52.380 --> 01:23:55.290
At least in what I see,
more women are using IUDs

01:23:55.290 --> 01:23:58.320
for contraception than OCs.

01:23:58.320 --> 01:24:03.320
I mean, we use OCs sometimes
in women who are premenopausal,

01:24:03.540 --> 01:24:07.410
for symptomatic control,
but we'll typically use like

01:24:07.410 --> 01:24:11.400
a low low estrin, so a very
low synthetic estrogen,

01:24:11.400 --> 01:24:13.170
which I don't like using these very much.

01:24:13.170 --> 01:24:14.130
But if it's the only thing that we can get

01:24:14.130 --> 01:24:15.480
to control certain symptoms,

01:24:15.480 --> 01:24:17.733
and we'll use it like half her cycle.

01:24:18.930 --> 01:24:21.300
But it's typically not something

01:24:21.300 --> 01:24:23.340
we're that experienced with.

01:24:23.340 --> 01:24:25.920
- What about testosterone,
because you mentioned that,

01:24:25.920 --> 01:24:28.683
you know, nanogram per mill,

01:24:30.300 --> 01:24:33.856
when you said everything
to the same, you know,

01:24:33.856 --> 01:24:34.689
I guess it's nanogram per deciliter,

01:24:34.689 --> 01:24:35.750
- Yeah yeah.
- as it would be

01:24:35.750 --> 01:24:36.810
to kind of normalize

01:24:36.810 --> 01:24:38.010
everything.
- Versus picogram per ML.

01:24:38.010 --> 01:24:38.843
- Right, yeah.

01:24:38.843 --> 01:24:42.115
And so, what Peter was
pointing out before is that

01:24:42.115 --> 01:24:42.948
you look at your charts

01:24:42.948 --> 01:24:44.010
and they're all in these
different measures,

01:24:44.010 --> 01:24:45.210
and so when you normalize,

01:24:45.210 --> 01:24:47.700
testosterone is actually
higher than estrogen in women.

01:24:47.700 --> 01:24:48.900
That's a surprise to me.

01:24:50.730 --> 01:24:53.550
Do you prescribe testosterone
therapy to women ever?

01:24:53.550 --> 01:24:56.760
- We do sometimes, but I do
it with much more caution

01:24:56.760 --> 01:24:58.620
because I don't have the data, right.

01:24:58.620 --> 01:25:02.550
So where I'll, you know,
what we'll say is, look,

01:25:02.550 --> 01:25:04.770
I mean, we're now really
outside of an area

01:25:04.770 --> 01:25:06.510
where I can point to a lot of data.

01:25:06.510 --> 01:25:09.360
Like when it comes to
estrogen and progesterone,

01:25:09.360 --> 01:25:11.880
I'll happily go toe to toe with anybody

01:25:11.880 --> 01:25:14.190
who wants to make the
case that it's dangerous.

01:25:14.190 --> 01:25:16.770
Similarly, when it comes to
using testosterone in men,

01:25:16.770 --> 01:25:20.190
I'll spend all day, and I can
go through that literature

01:25:20.190 --> 01:25:21.870
until the other person cries

01:25:21.870 --> 01:25:24.300
and wants to just call uncle, right.

01:25:24.300 --> 01:25:25.133
When it comes to-
- And then you

01:25:25.133 --> 01:25:26.040
prescribe them testosterone.

01:25:26.040 --> 01:25:27.840
- When it comes to estrogen in,

01:25:27.840 --> 01:25:30.480
testosterone in women,
don't have that data.

01:25:30.480 --> 01:25:32.820
And I'd love to see that trial done.

01:25:32.820 --> 01:25:37.140
So what's the sweet spot,
how do we reconcile that?

01:25:37.140 --> 01:25:39.840
So it's not something I consider standard.

01:25:39.840 --> 01:25:42.480
And basically, if a woman
is, if her testosterone,

01:25:42.480 --> 01:25:45.390
first of all, is
staggeringly low, and again,

01:25:45.390 --> 01:25:47.700
even though her testosterone's
low compared to a male,

01:25:47.700 --> 01:25:48.780
we still have a range.

01:25:48.780 --> 01:25:50.730
So if it's really at the
bottom of that range,

01:25:50.730 --> 01:25:53.130
she's really having difficulty
putting on muscle mass

01:25:53.130 --> 01:25:55.560
and really complaining of low libido,

01:25:55.560 --> 01:25:56.940
I think in that situation,

01:25:56.940 --> 01:26:01.860
we'll go ahead and use topical
testosterone, and, you know,

01:26:01.860 --> 01:26:05.850
replace her to a level that is
still physiologically normal.

01:26:05.850 --> 01:26:08.130
- Yeah, that's key because
when people hear HRT,

01:26:08.130 --> 01:26:11.250
they think about, super
physiological seems to be the term.

01:26:11.250 --> 01:26:14.100
- Yeah, like I've never
seen a single symptom

01:26:14.100 --> 01:26:16.200
in a single woman that
I've put testosterone on,

01:26:16.200 --> 01:26:18.330
in terms of like acne, body
hair, things like that.

01:26:18.330 --> 01:26:20.940
Like those are real symptoms
that you have to be aware of,

01:26:20.940 --> 01:26:23.910
but, you know, like clitoral enlargement

01:26:23.910 --> 01:26:25.770
and things like that,
like that doesn't happen

01:26:25.770 --> 01:26:28.023
under physiologic normal conditions.

01:26:29.592 --> 01:26:30.660
- I'd love to talk a little bit about

01:26:30.660 --> 01:26:33.093
hormone replacement therapy in men.

01:26:34.500 --> 01:26:36.300
When one looks on social
media and the internet,

