we come to the decision

01:37:03.690 --> 01:37:06.900
that this person is a good candidate

01:37:06.900 --> 01:37:08.844
for testosterone replacement therapy.

01:37:08.844 --> 01:37:10.860
The next question is,

01:37:10.860 --> 01:37:12.900
what's the method we're going to do.

01:37:12.900 --> 01:37:15.480
Are we going to do it
indirectly or directly?

01:37:15.480 --> 01:37:19.653
Now we used to use a lot
of Clomid in our practice.

01:37:20.520 --> 01:37:21.840
And have you talked
about Clomid on the pod?

01:37:21.840 --> 01:37:23.730
- Haven't talked too much about it.

01:37:23.730 --> 01:37:26.340
No, we've talked a little
bit about the fact that

01:37:26.340 --> 01:37:28.470
some people taking things like anastrozole

01:37:28.470 --> 01:37:31.140
to reduce aromatase activity,

01:37:31.140 --> 01:37:34.740
can potentially run into
trouble because they think,

01:37:34.740 --> 01:37:37.740
oh well, more testosterone,
good, lower estrogen, bad,

01:37:37.740 --> 01:37:40.110
and then they end up with
issues like joint pain,

01:37:40.110 --> 01:37:42.900
memory issues, and severe drops in libido.

01:37:42.900 --> 01:37:43.887
And I think a lot of the reason why-

01:37:43.887 --> 01:37:45.450
- And even fat accumulation.

01:37:45.450 --> 01:37:47.460
So if estrogen is too low,

01:37:47.460 --> 01:37:49.470
you can develop adiposity in a way

01:37:49.470 --> 01:37:50.303
that you wouldn't otherwise.

01:37:50.303 --> 01:37:52.230
There was a great New
England Journal paper,

01:37:52.230 --> 01:37:54.580
it's probably 10 years
old now, that looked at,

01:37:55.440 --> 01:37:56.940
I believe it was five different doses

01:37:56.940 --> 01:37:57.990
of testosterone cypionate.

01:37:57.990 --> 01:37:59.670
So these men were chemically castrated

01:37:59.670 --> 01:38:01.200
and divided into 10 groups.

01:38:01.200 --> 01:38:02.250
It's pretty remarkable-

01:38:02.250 --> 01:38:03.450
- [Andrew] That somebody
signed up for this study.

01:38:03.450 --> 01:38:07.080
- Yeah, so you were with
and without anastrozole

01:38:07.080 --> 01:38:09.090
and five doses of testosterone.

01:38:09.090 --> 01:38:12.450
So now you basically had
five testosterone levels,

01:38:12.450 --> 01:38:15.390
plus or minus high or low estradiol.

01:38:15.390 --> 01:38:17.160
And the results were really clear

01:38:17.160 --> 01:38:19.290
that the higher your testosterone

01:38:19.290 --> 01:38:21.600
and the more your estradiol
was in kind of that

01:38:21.600 --> 01:38:24.270
30 to 50 range, the better you were.

01:38:24.270 --> 01:38:26.100
So if estrogen was too low,

01:38:26.100 --> 01:38:28.170
even in the presence of high testosterone,

01:38:28.170 --> 01:38:30.750
the outcomes were less significant.

01:38:30.750 --> 01:38:32.940
- And this is 30 to 50
nanograms per decile,

01:38:32.940 --> 01:38:35.730
not 30 to 50% of one's testosterone.

01:38:35.730 --> 01:38:37.827
Okay, great.

01:38:37.827 --> 01:38:39.360
No, we haven't talked, but Clomid is,

01:38:39.360 --> 01:38:41.040
you know, we have not
talked a lot about Clomid.

01:38:41.040 --> 01:38:42.570
I'd love to get your thoughts on Clomid.

01:38:42.570 --> 01:38:45.180
- So Clomiphene is a fertility drug.

01:38:45.180 --> 01:38:46.770
It's a synthetic hormone.

01:38:46.770 --> 01:38:49.107
It's actually two drugs, M Clomiphene

01:38:49.107 --> 01:38:50.880
and I forget the other one.

01:38:50.880 --> 01:38:55.880
And it tells the pituitary
to secrete FSH and LH.

01:38:58.710 --> 01:39:03.710
So you, and so the advantage
of Clomid is it's oral,

01:39:03.739 --> 01:39:06.180
and it's meant to be taken orally.

01:39:06.180 --> 01:39:08.490
So, you know, a typical
starting dose would be like

01:39:08.490 --> 01:39:10.593
50 milligrams three times a week.

01:39:11.640 --> 01:39:15.270
And if you do that, you'll
notice, in most men,

01:39:15.270 --> 01:39:17.730
especially young men, FSH and LH goes up.

01:39:17.730 --> 01:39:19.590
In any man, the FSH and LH go up,

01:39:19.590 --> 01:39:21.810
but if a man still has testicular reserve,

01:39:21.810 --> 01:39:24.410
he'll make lots of testosterone
in response to that.

01:39:26.313 --> 01:39:27.630
'Cause that's the first order question

01:39:27.630 --> 01:39:30.180
we're trying to answer is do you,

01:39:30.180 --> 01:39:34.140
is your failure to make
testosterone central or peripheral?

01:39:34.140 --> 01:39:36.210
- Yeah, and I think,
just want to point out,

01:39:36.210 --> 01:39:37.140
again, correct me if I'm wrong,

01:39:37.140 --> 01:39:39.600
but my understanding is
that a lot of the drugs

01:39:39.600 --> 01:39:42.540
that we're talking about,
the synthetic compounds,

01:39:42.540 --> 01:39:45.630
testosterone, estrogen, things
related to growth hormone,

01:39:45.630 --> 01:39:48.600
et cetera, were discovered and designed

01:39:48.600 --> 01:39:50.760
in order to treat, and excuse me,

01:39:50.760 --> 01:39:52.140
in order to isolate and treat

01:39:52.140 --> 01:39:53.490
exactly these kinds of syndromes,

01:39:53.490 --> 01:39:56.190
whether or not it was the
hypothalamus, the pituitary,

01:39:56.190 --> 01:39:59.087
or the target tissue, the
ovaries or the testes.

01:39:59.087 --> 01:39:59.920
Correct?

01:39:59.920 --> 01:40:00.753
- Correct, yeah.

01:40:00.753 --> 01:40:02.300
I mean, I think, the easiest
way to go about doing this

01:40:02.300 --> 01:40:04.650
is just give the hormone that's missing,

01:40:04.650 --> 01:40:07.710
without attention to
where the deficiency is.

01:40:07.710 --> 01:40:12.240
Why this becomes relevant is
if you have a 35-year-old guy

01:40:12.240 --> 01:40:14.580
whose testosterone is low,

01:40:14.580 --> 01:40:16.620
but you can demonstrate that it's low

01:40:16.620 --> 01:40:18.540
because he's not getting
enough of a signal

01:40:18.540 --> 01:40:19.860
from the pituitary,

01:40:19.860 --> 01:40:22.080
why would you bother giving
him more testosterone

01:40:22.080 --> 01:40:24.990
when he has the Leydig
cells and the Sertoli cells

01:40:24.990 --> 01:40:25.830
to make testosterone.

01:40:25.830 --> 01:40:27.093
He just needs the signal.

01:40:28.350 --> 01:40:29.820
Sometimes, though not always,

01:40:29.820 --> 01:40:33.025
just a course of Clomid can wake him up,

01:40:33.025 --> 01:40:35.220
and he's back to making
normal testosterone.

01:40:35.220 --> 01:40:38.460
- So he'll do this three
times a week, 50 milligrams,

01:40:38.460 --> 01:40:40.230
three times a week, for
a short course, and then?

01:40:40.230 --> 01:40:41.760
- Yeah, we would do that
for eight to 12 weeks

01:40:41.760 --> 01:40:43.110
and then we reevaluate.

01:40:43.110 --> 01:40:46.800
- And estrogen and testosterone
will increase in parallel.

01:40:46.800 --> 01:40:47.760
- Yes.

01:40:47.760 --> 01:40:49.050
And again, it depends, you know,

01:40:49.050 --> 01:40:50.760
aromatase activity is dependent

01:40:50.760 --> 01:40:53.253
on how much body fat
you have and genetics.

01:40:54.300 --> 01:40:56.100
And if estradiol gets too high,

01:40:56.100 --> 01:40:58.650
we think if it gets over about 55, 60,

01:40:58.650 --> 01:41:01.410
we will give micro doses of anastrozole.

01:41:01.410 --> 01:41:03.180
But it has to be real micro doses.

01:41:03.180 --> 01:41:05.640
I mean, you cannot pound
people with anastrozole.

01:41:05.640 --> 01:41:10.530
To give you perspective,
the sort of on-label use,

01:41:10.530 --> 01:41:13.020
like if you just go to a
pharmacy and order anastrozole,

01:41:13.020 --> 01:41:15.240
you're going to get one milligram tablets.

01:41:15.240 --> 01:41:16.950
Like we can't give anybody a milligram.

01:41:16.950 --> 01:41:18.480
- [Andrew] They'll feel like garbage.

01:41:18.480 --> 01:41:21.120
- We have to have it
compounded at 0.1 milligrams,

01:41:21.120 --> 01:41:24.150
and we might give a patient
0.1, two to three times a week.

01:41:24.150 --> 01:41:26.760
That would be a big dose of anastrozole.

01:41:26.760 --> 01:41:30.180
- Yeah, I think that the
typical TRT clinic out there

01:41:30.180 --> 01:41:34.230
is giving 200 milligrams per mil, one mil,

01:41:34.230 --> 01:41:36.330
200 milligrams of testosterone
once every two weeks,

01:41:36.330 --> 01:41:39.840
and then hitting people
with multiple milligrams

01:41:39.840 --> 01:41:42.330
of anastrozole, and
they're all over the place.

01:41:42.330 --> 01:41:43.740
- I've never really understood,

01:41:43.740 --> 01:41:45.390
I mean, I guess I shouldn't be surprised,

01:41:45.390 --> 01:41:47.880
but it kind of blows my
mind that these TRT clinics

01:41:47.880 --> 01:41:49.620
are up all over the place, given how bad.

01:41:49.620 --> 01:41:50.850
I mean, I see the results,

01:41:50.850 --> 01:41:53.490
'cause I have patients
that come from them,

01:41:53.490 --> 01:41:56.280
and I don't understand like
why they're so incompetent.

01:41:56.280 --> 01:41:57.840
- I actually think it's worse than that.

01:41:57.840 --> 01:42:01.260
I think that they simply don't
understand and don't care,

01:42:01.260 --> 01:42:04.890
because it's a pill mill
and it's a money mill.

01:42:04.890 --> 01:42:06.480
I think that nowadays it seems

01:42:06.480 --> 01:42:08.160
almost everybody who's doing TRT

01:42:08.160 --> 01:42:10.500
is taking lower doses more
frequently every other day,

01:42:10.500 --> 01:42:12.270
or twice a week, dividing the dose,

01:42:12.270 --> 01:42:14.670
and being very, very
careful with these estrogen

01:42:14.670 --> 01:42:16.143
or aromatase blockers.

01:42:17.370 --> 01:42:20.220
- Most of our patients do not
take aromatase inhibitors.

01:42:20.220 --> 01:42:21.053
It's not needed.

01:42:21.053 --> 01:42:23.793
It's really only the high
aromatasers that need it.

01:42:25.230 --> 01:42:27.300
And so yeah, when we'll
talk about testosterone,

01:42:27.300 --> 01:42:28.920
we'll talk about dosing
there, 'cause I agree.

01:42:28.920 --> 01:42:30.600
The more frequently you
can take it the better,

01:42:30.600 --> 01:42:32.880
and frankly, you don't
need to go more frequently

01:42:32.880 --> 01:42:34.470
than twice a week.

01:42:34.470 --> 01:42:35.313
- Because it's so slow-acting.
- The half-life, yeah,

01:42:35.313 --> 01:42:37.290
the half life of the drug is,

01:42:37.290 --> 01:42:38.580
I think it's about 3 1/2 days,

01:42:38.580 --> 01:42:39.413
is the plasma half-life
or something like that.

01:42:39.413 --> 01:42:44.010
I could be off a little
bit, but twice week dosing

01:42:44.010 --> 01:42:45.030
is really nice.

01:42:45.030 --> 01:42:49.380
So if you go to like a testosterone clinic

01:42:49.380 --> 01:42:51.540
that's giving you 200 every two weeks,

01:42:51.540 --> 01:42:54.990
50 twice a week is the same
total dose, which, by the way,

01:42:54.990 --> 01:42:56.250
is a physiologic dose.

01:42:56.250 --> 01:42:59.580
That's not going to give
somebody any of the side effects

01:42:59.580 --> 01:43:00.413
you would see.

01:43:00.413 --> 01:43:01.560
You're not going to get acne with that.

01:43:01.560 --> 01:43:03.600
You're not going to get gynecomastia.

01:43:03.600 --> 01:43:04.470
You're not going to get anything.

01:43:04.470 --> 01:43:06.210
The only real side
effect you get from that

01:43:06.210 --> 01:43:08.640
is you will get testicular atrophy.

01:43:08.640 --> 01:43:09.780
It is enough to suppress.

01:43:09.780 --> 01:43:12.120
- Yeah, to maintain fertility,

01:43:12.120 --> 01:43:13.650
what do you typically do for?

01:43:13.650 --> 01:43:14.790
- Well, so this is where,

01:43:14.790 --> 01:43:16.380
so I'll finish the story on Clomid,

01:43:16.380 --> 01:43:18.180
'cause we currently do not use Clomid,

01:43:18.180 --> 01:43:22.440
and that's due to a really
interesting observation

01:43:22.440 --> 01:43:25.890
that we made, that I don't
think has been reported

01:43:25.890 --> 01:43:27.300
in the literature yet,

01:43:27.300 --> 01:43:31.470
which is that Clomid was
increasing levels of a sterol

01:43:31.470 --> 01:43:35.123
that we also happen to
measure called desmosterol.

01:43:35.123 --> 01:43:36.030
- [Andrew] I'm not familiar with that.

01:43:36.030 --> 01:43:41.030
- So, and the way that
cholesterol is made, it's made by,

01:43:41.430 --> 01:43:43.380
there's two pathways
that make cholesterol.

01:43:43.380 --> 01:43:45.900
So it starts like with
two carbon sub units,

01:43:45.900 --> 01:43:48.300
like Acetyl-CoA, and it kind
of marches down a pathway,

01:43:48.300 --> 01:43:52.590
bifurcates, and cholesterol is
the finished product of both.

01:43:52.590 --> 01:43:54.962
But in one of those pathways,

01:43:54.962 --> 01:43:58.470
the molecule right before
cholesterol is called desmosterol,

01:43:58.470 --> 01:44:00.690
in the other pathway
it's called lathosterol.

01:44:00.690 --> 01:44:04.800
So we constantly measure
lathosterol and desmosterol,

01:44:04.800 --> 01:44:05.970
because we want to know

01:44:05.970 --> 01:44:08.610
how much cholesterol is being
synthesized in the body.

01:44:08.610 --> 01:44:10.770
Not just what your cholesterol is.

01:44:10.770 --> 01:44:13.770
We want to know how much
cholesterol you reabsorb.

01:44:13.770 --> 01:44:15.600
And those markers are
really important to us

01:44:15.600 --> 01:44:18.100
when we're looking at
cardiovascular disease risk.

01:44:19.050 --> 01:44:22.320
So when we gave patients Clomid,

01:44:22.320 --> 01:44:26.820
we were noticing a almost universal rise

01:44:26.820 --> 01:44:28.773
in their desmosterol levels.

01:44:30.030 --> 01:44:31.860
Now, the most obvious
explanation for that,

01:44:31.860 --> 01:44:33.750
though the last time I looked,

01:44:33.750 --> 01:44:36.000
I couldn't find clear explanation for this

01:44:36.000 --> 01:44:37.440
in any of the clinical,

01:44:37.440 --> 01:44:40.230
like the clinical trials that
led to the approval of Clomid.

01:44:40.230 --> 01:44:41.700
So I don't know if it was described.

01:44:41.700 --> 01:44:43.350
In fact, maybe it wasn't known.

01:44:43.350 --> 01:44:46.110
I suspect it is inhibiting the enzyme,

01:44:46.110 --> 01:44:48.510
which I think is called
delta-24 desaturates,

01:44:48.510 --> 01:44:51.480
that turns desmosterol into cholesterol.

01:44:51.480 --> 01:44:53.190
Makes sense if you inhibit that enzyme,

01:44:53.190 --> 01:44:55.563
you're going to see a rise in desmosterol.

01:44:56.460 --> 01:44:58.410
This wouldn't have been a concern to me,

01:44:58.410 --> 01:45:00.180
if not for the fact that Tom Dayspring,

01:45:00.180 --> 01:45:01.440
who's one of the physicians we work with,

01:45:01.440 --> 01:45:02.880
who's one of the world's
experts in lipids,

01:45:02.880 --> 01:45:06.240
pointed out a very obscure story,

01:45:06.240 --> 01:45:09.750
which was that the very
first drug ever approved

01:45:09.750 --> 01:45:12.720
to treat cardiovascular disease,

01:45:12.720 --> 01:45:14.850
at least to treat hypercholesterolemia,

01:45:14.850 --> 01:45:17.523
was a drug that attacked the same enzyme.

01:45:19.140 --> 01:45:21.750
So this is in the early 1960s I believe,

01:45:21.750 --> 01:45:22.583
maybe the mid '60s.

01:45:22.583 --> 01:45:26.520
This drug was approved and
it lowered cholesterol.

01:45:26.520 --> 01:45:28.800
And it was approved on the
basis of lowering cholesterol.

01:45:28.800 --> 01:45:32.130
Now today, no drug for ASCVD is approved

01:45:32.130 --> 01:45:34.140
on the basis of it lowering cholesterol.

01:45:34.140 --> 01:45:35.430
That's not a high enough bar.

01:45:35.430 --> 01:45:36.990
You have to reduce events.

01:45:36.990 --> 01:45:37.823
Actually have to show

01:45:37.823 --> 01:45:40.740
that you're preventing
heart attacks and death.

01:45:40.740 --> 01:45:42.510
But at the time it was like,
hey, it lowers cholesterol,

01:45:42.510 --> 01:45:43.680
it's got to be good.

01:45:43.680 --> 01:45:45.870
Well, in the late '60s it
was pulled from the market

01:45:45.870 --> 01:45:47.850
because events were going up.

01:45:47.850 --> 01:45:50.790
So cholesterol was coming
down, events were going up.

01:45:50.790 --> 01:45:52.142
How could that be?

01:45:52.142 --> 01:45:53.580
We don't know.

01:45:53.580 --> 01:45:58.200
What we are suspecting
is that desmosterol,

01:45:58.200 --> 01:46:02.910
which is still a sterol, was
potentially more damaging

01:46:02.910 --> 01:46:05.940
and created more oxidative
stress in the endothelium,

01:46:05.940 --> 01:46:07.770
in the sub-endothelial
space than cholesterol.

01:46:07.770 --> 01:46:08.603
- [Andrew] I see.

01:46:08.603 --> 01:46:11.160
- Which would at least
suggest to us, and again,

01:46:11.160 --> 01:46:13.020
we're taking a lot of leaps here,

01:46:13.020 --> 01:46:16.470
that maybe having high
desmosterol, very high desmosterol,

01:46:16.470 --> 01:46:17.470
is not a good thing.

01:46:18.548 --> 01:46:20.970
And so once we kind of
pieced all that together

01:46:20.970 --> 01:46:22.620
a few years ago, we were like, yeah,

01:46:22.620 --> 01:46:24.803
we're just not going to
prescribe Clomid anymore.

01:46:26.370 --> 01:46:30.750
And we then switched to HCG,

01:46:30.750 --> 01:46:33.330
which we used to use
sometimes instead of Clomid,

01:46:33.330 --> 01:46:35.100
but it's more cumbersome to work with.

01:46:35.100 --> 01:46:36.270
It needs to be refrigerated,

01:46:36.270 --> 01:46:38.100
it's a much more fragile molecule.

01:46:38.100 --> 01:46:39.330
- Yeah, I think we talked about this once.

01:46:39.330 --> 01:46:42.240
It's almost like if you
accidentally knock over

01:46:42.240 --> 01:46:45.213
the little bottle, it's
basically gone bad.

01:46:45.213 --> 01:46:46.464
- Yeah yeah.
- Travel with it

01:46:46.464 --> 01:46:48.840
is very challenging.
- Can't travel with it.

01:46:48.840 --> 01:46:51.300
It's a needle, you know,
it's an injection, Sub-Q.

01:46:51.300 --> 01:46:53.970
So easy to administer, it's
not IM or anything like that,

01:46:53.970 --> 01:46:55.870
but it's just more of a hassle factor.

01:46:56.880 --> 01:47:00.150
But that said, it has the
benefit that Clomid does,

01:47:00.150 --> 01:47:02.580
which is it preserves testicular function.

01:47:02.580 --> 01:47:05.190
It preserves testicular volume.

01:47:05.190 --> 01:47:07.680
So, you know, body builders
will often use this

01:47:07.680 --> 01:47:09.540
in their post-cycle therapy,

01:47:09.540 --> 01:47:11.820
as a way to kind of recover function.

01:47:11.820 --> 01:47:15.210
And we would just use it
now as ongoing therapy

01:47:15.210 --> 01:47:17.970
for a guy who still
has testicular reserve.

01:47:17.970 --> 01:47:19.740
- So on its own, no testosterone,

01:47:19.740 --> 01:47:21.570
no aromatase inhibitor, nothing.

01:47:21.570 --> 01:47:23.940
Just a way to crank out
a bit more testosterone

01:47:23.940 --> 01:47:24.773
from the testes.

01:47:24.773 --> 01:47:26.037
Maybe some additional estrogen also?

01:47:26.037 --> 01:47:27.810
- Yeah, and HCG is a different model.

01:47:27.810 --> 01:47:30.930
HCG is just an analog
of luteinizing hormone.

01:47:30.930 --> 01:47:33.300
So it's basically like giving
them luteinizing hormone.

01:47:33.300 --> 01:47:36.210
- So it's going to crush endogenous
luteinizing hormone levels,

01:47:36.210 --> 01:47:37.043
right, because it's-

01:47:37.043 --> 01:47:38.163
- Actually, yeah.

01:47:39.810 --> 01:47:42.000
You don't really see
much of an impact on LH,

01:47:42.000 --> 01:47:45.360
but you do see endogenous
testosterone production go down.

01:47:45.360 --> 01:47:46.260
Actually, no, I correct that.

01:47:46.260 --> 01:47:49.260
Both FSH and LH will go
down on a high enough dose.

01:47:49.260 --> 01:47:50.093
Yep.

01:47:50.093 --> 01:47:50.926
- Just as a mention,

01:47:50.926 --> 01:47:52.530
and here I'm not making recommendations,

01:47:52.530 --> 01:47:55.620
but one supplement I've
talked a lot about publicly

01:47:55.620 --> 01:47:56.850
is Fadogia agrestis,

01:47:56.850 --> 01:47:59.043
which is this weird Nigerian shrub that-

01:48:00.297 --> 01:48:01.260
- You talked about this on Tim's podcast.

01:48:01.260 --> 01:48:04.590
- On Tim's podcast and Joe's
podcast, and, you know,

01:48:04.590 --> 01:48:06.690
there was a bit of a backlash
because it does turn out

01:48:06.690 --> 01:48:09.000
that at high doses, in rodent studies,

01:48:09.000 --> 01:48:12.390
it can cause some toxicity of the testes.

01:48:12.390 --> 01:48:14.550
But at lower doses,

01:48:14.550 --> 01:48:16.650
it does seem to increase
luteinizing hormone.

01:48:16.650 --> 01:48:17.550
And after talking about this,

01:48:17.550 --> 01:48:18.450
a number of people went out there,

01:48:18.450 --> 01:48:19.650
did pre and post blood work,

01:48:19.650 --> 01:48:21.990
and the consistent effect
seems to be an increase

01:48:21.990 --> 01:48:23.100
in luteinizing hormone.

01:48:23.100 --> 01:48:26.700
There's a noticeable effect
on testicular size and volume.

01:48:26.700 --> 01:48:28.290
So a lot of people take this
and be like, oh, you know,

01:48:28.290 --> 01:48:30.840
their balls are getting bigger,
and so they get all excited

01:48:30.840 --> 01:48:32.760
that something good is happening.

01:48:32.760 --> 01:48:35.550
But we don't know the
long-term safety and efficacy

01:48:35.550 --> 01:48:36.690
of something like Fadogia,

01:48:36.690 --> 01:48:37.890
whether or not it needs to be cycled-

01:48:37.890 --> 01:48:40.830
- Yeah, this is why I'm also
very leery of the supplements

01:48:40.830 --> 01:48:44.520
in this space, because at
least when we're using HCG,

01:48:44.520 --> 01:48:48.600
or testosterone, like we
have so many years of data.

01:48:48.600 --> 01:48:51.060
You have to remember how many
women are using this stuff

01:48:51.060 --> 01:48:53.100
for reproductive medicine.

01:48:53.100 --> 01:48:57.273
So, you know, I think the
FDA has a lot of faults.

01:48:58.290 --> 01:49:00.930
I think I have an entire
podcast devoted to

01:49:00.930 --> 01:49:02.370
the corruption of the FDA

01:49:02.370 --> 01:49:03.930
and all of the mistakes
that have been made

01:49:03.930 --> 01:49:06.240
with respect to their oversight in,

01:49:06.240 --> 01:49:07.773
especially generic drugs.

01:49:08.640 --> 01:49:11.820
But it's way more regulated
than the wild wild west of

01:49:11.820 --> 01:49:13.140
nutty supplement land.

01:49:13.140 --> 01:49:14.160
- Absolutely.

01:49:14.160 --> 01:49:16.597
I think that the reason we're
talking about things like

01:49:16.597 --> 01:49:17.760
Tongkat and Fadogia was

01:49:17.760 --> 01:49:20.010
to provide some intermediate discussion

01:49:20.010 --> 01:49:22.200
between doing all the correct things,

01:49:22.200 --> 01:49:24.300
but no supplementation or hormone therapy,

01:49:24.300 --> 01:49:25.846
and then going straight
to hormone therapy.

01:49:25.846 --> 01:49:26.679
- Yeah, I-
- It's sort of like

01:49:26.679 --> 01:49:30.420
the leap from, I can't focus
very well, to Ritalin, right,

01:49:30.420 --> 01:49:34.290
without a real diagnosis
of ADHD to, oh well,

01:49:34.290 --> 01:49:35.580
maybe some things like

01:49:35.580 --> 01:49:38.040
Alpha-GPC low doses of nicotine, right?

01:49:38.040 --> 01:49:39.780
But I agree entirely.

01:49:39.780 --> 01:49:41.490
I mean, the sourcing is important.

01:49:41.490 --> 01:49:44.160
The dosages are worked out empirically

01:49:44.160 --> 01:49:45.720
on an individual basis.

01:49:45.720 --> 01:49:48.150
And there aren't
randomized control trials.

01:49:48.150 --> 01:49:49.290
There just aren't.

01:49:49.290 --> 01:49:54.290
- Yeah, and, you know,
have kind of like a seven,

01:49:54.510 --> 01:49:56.400
this is another Peter Principle, right.

01:49:56.400 --> 01:49:58.920
So I got a lot of patients that
come into the practice and,

01:49:58.920 --> 01:50:01.200
you know, during our intake, we go through

01:50:01.200 --> 01:50:03.390
what drugs and supplements
are you taking right now?

01:50:03.390 --> 01:50:05.117
And, you know, a lot of people come in,

01:50:05.117 --> 01:50:06.690
I'm not taking anything, Peter, I just,

01:50:06.690 --> 01:50:08.790
you're in charge now, like
tell me what you think.

01:50:08.790 --> 01:50:10.020
And then you get a lot
of people that come in

01:50:10.020 --> 01:50:12.690
and they're like, are going
to need an extra few pages

01:50:12.690 --> 01:50:14.070
for this part of the documentation.

01:50:14.070 --> 01:50:15.150
- Right, the people who
travel with a suitcase

01:50:15.150 --> 01:50:16.710
that you can hear as they
walk through the airport

01:50:16.710 --> 01:50:18.420
from all the pills rattling.

01:50:18.420 --> 01:50:20.550
- So I give these patients
a little homework exercise,

01:50:20.550 --> 01:50:22.710
which is you have to answer
these seven questions

01:50:22.710 --> 01:50:23.970
for every supplement you take,

01:50:23.970 --> 01:50:26.130
and here's the spreadsheet
and let's talk about it.

01:50:26.130 --> 01:50:28.980
And it basically just runs
through like, you know,

01:50:28.980 --> 01:50:32.070
it's basically walking
you through the logic

01:50:32.070 --> 01:50:34.380
of why do you take this molecule?

01:50:34.380 --> 01:50:38.790
And I think for many people,
it's when they do that,

01:50:38.790 --> 01:50:40.380
it's very sobering, right.

01:50:40.380 --> 01:50:42.270
They kind of, a lot of them
will come back and be like,

01:50:42.270 --> 01:50:45.840
you know what, I don't think
I can come up with any reason

01:50:45.840 --> 01:50:47.970
along this really
rigorous line of thinking

01:50:47.970 --> 01:50:50.490
as to why I'm taking 80% of this stuff.

01:50:50.490 --> 01:50:51.323
- Well, I know people,

01:50:51.323 --> 01:50:53.012
and actually we know
some of the same people,

01:50:53.012 --> 01:50:55.950
who are fanatic about like
red light on the testes,

01:50:55.950 --> 01:50:59.700
sunning their testes, putting
ice packs on their testes.

01:50:59.700 --> 01:51:00.990
It's kind of all over the place.

01:51:00.990 --> 01:51:03.510
The number of things that
people are trying and doing

01:51:03.510 --> 01:51:06.660
in order to increase testosterone
output from their testes

01:51:06.660 --> 01:51:07.493
is pretty remarkable.

01:51:07.493 --> 01:51:10.320
And that said, among
some of the women I know,

01:51:10.320 --> 01:51:11.340
the number of things that they're doing

01:51:11.340 --> 01:51:13.650
to try and promote
longevity and fertility,

01:51:13.650 --> 01:51:17.850
and in particular skin health,
hair health and nail health,

01:51:17.850 --> 01:51:19.650
is also kind of outrageous.

01:51:19.650 --> 01:51:21.840
Everything from collagen
to red light therapies,

01:51:21.840 --> 01:51:24.030
which may actually have some
efficacy in certain cases.

01:51:24.030 --> 01:51:25.200
But.

01:51:25.200 --> 01:51:26.790
- As an interesting aside.
- There's a hunger there,

01:51:26.790 --> 01:51:27.930
right?
- Oh for sure.

01:51:27.930 --> 01:51:29.850
One of the things that I hope
gets a lot more attention

01:51:29.850 --> 01:51:33.210
is the use of rapamycin for
preserving ovarian health.

01:51:33.210 --> 01:51:34.710
So the animal literature on this

01:51:34.710 --> 01:51:36.660
is pretty impressive, right?

01:51:36.660 --> 01:51:41.660
So in mouse models, rapamycin
will preserve ovarian life.

01:51:41.820 --> 01:51:42.653
And so-

01:51:42.653 --> 01:51:43.486
- Interesting.
- It makes sense, right.

01:51:43.486 --> 01:51:44.340
I mean, it totally makes sense

01:51:44.340 --> 01:51:48.840
why the most potent GRo
protective molecule we have

01:51:48.840 --> 01:51:51.840
would also preserve and
extend ovarian life,

01:51:51.840 --> 01:51:52.740
at least in mice.

01:51:52.740 --> 01:51:57.660
So I'd love to see the
clinical trials done in women

01:51:57.660 --> 01:51:59.280
to test this hypothesis.

01:51:59.280 --> 01:52:00.113
- I definitely want to come back to this,

01:52:00.113 --> 01:52:01.650
'cause that's a key thing.

01:52:01.650 --> 01:52:03.030
I know that a lot of
people are interested in

01:52:03.030 --> 01:52:06.390
female fertility out there,
including their male partners.

01:52:06.390 --> 01:52:09.990
So going back to, so now I understand

01:52:09.990 --> 01:52:11.190
why you don't prescribe clomiphene,

01:52:11.190 --> 01:52:16.020
because of this desmosterol,
potential desmosterol link.

01:52:17.700 --> 01:52:19.080
What about testosterone therapy?

01:52:19.080 --> 01:52:20.064
So less,

01:52:20.064 --> 01:52:22.024
- Yeah.
- less frequent, lower doses,

01:52:22.024 --> 01:52:25.080
less or no estrogen inhibition

01:52:25.080 --> 01:52:27.330
or aromatase inhibition.
- Yeah, only,

01:52:27.330 --> 01:52:29.550
we're only using an aromatase blocker,

01:52:29.550 --> 01:52:31.500
and we use Arimidex when we do.

01:52:31.500 --> 01:52:34.530
It's just to get that estradiol
into the range we want.

01:52:34.530 --> 01:52:36.750
I like to see it between 30 and 50.

01:52:36.750 --> 01:52:37.890
That's the sweet spot.

01:52:37.890 --> 01:52:42.720
And I don't know, I would
say like a third, maybe a,

01:52:42.720 --> 01:52:45.510
not even a third, I'd
say probably 20% of men

01:52:45.510 --> 01:52:49.770
require a micro dose of
anastrozole to get into that range.

01:52:49.770 --> 01:52:50.603
Most do not.

01:52:51.810 --> 01:52:53.760
And I'd rather err on the
side of being a little high

01:52:53.760 --> 01:52:54.900
than a little low.

01:52:54.900 --> 01:52:57.393
So I never really want to be below 25,

01:52:58.680 --> 01:53:00.930
unless sometimes it's
just below 25 and it is,

01:53:00.930 --> 01:53:02.430
it is what it is, that's fine.

01:53:02.430 --> 01:53:04.440
But if we're suppressing it to below 25,

01:53:04.440 --> 01:53:05.940
I never want to be in that zone.

01:53:06.840 --> 01:53:09.510
And then yes, so TRT is
ultimately, you know,

01:53:09.510 --> 01:53:12.660
giving testosterone cypionate
is usually what we use.

01:53:12.660 --> 01:53:15.030
- Injectable, so as
opposed to cream or pellet.

01:53:15.030 --> 01:53:16.470
- Correct.

01:53:16.470 --> 01:53:19.683
I used to use pellets with women,

01:53:21.420 --> 01:53:23.880
for some who were really adamant

01:53:23.880 --> 01:53:28.513
about the convenience of it,
but for a bunch of reasons

01:53:28.513 --> 01:53:31.260
I just, I'm mostly not doing that

01:53:31.260 --> 01:53:33.633
and I've never been a
fan of pellets in men.

01:53:34.903 --> 01:53:36.540
- You can't control the
dosages, once it's in,

01:53:36.540 --> 01:53:37.373
right?
- Well, even if you know

01:53:37.373 --> 01:53:38.760
the dose, yeah, that's
obviously a problem.

01:53:38.760 --> 01:53:40.860
But I don't think, there's
a big difference between

01:53:40.860 --> 01:53:42.120
putting a pellet into a man and a woman.

01:53:42.120 --> 01:53:44.220
So when you're putting a
estrogen pellet into a woman,

01:53:44.220 --> 01:53:47.400
it's like, it's that big.

01:53:47.400 --> 01:53:49.830
When you're putting
enough pellets into a man

01:53:49.830 --> 01:53:51.540
for six months of testosterone,

01:53:51.540 --> 01:53:54.810
it's two sums of pellets that
are longer than my fingers.

01:53:54.810 --> 01:53:56.330
So you're putting like a V-shape.

01:53:56.330 --> 01:53:57.166
- [Andrew] Where are you putting this?

01:53:57.166 --> 01:53:59.790
- You're putting it into the gluteal fat.

01:53:59.790 --> 01:54:01.650
So it's just a more morbid procedure,

01:54:01.650 --> 01:54:02.850
and I don't think it's necessary.

01:54:02.850 --> 01:54:05.580
I think if you know how
to manage it, you know,

01:54:05.580 --> 01:54:07.225
through sort of the injections, and now-

01:54:07.225 --> 01:54:08.058
- [Andrew] Injections are no big deal.

01:54:08.058 --> 01:54:09.540
- Yeah, well especially
now if you're doing,

01:54:09.540 --> 01:54:11.640
we're having them do
Sub-Q injections anyway.

01:54:11.640 --> 01:54:15.810
So it's not IM, they're using
five-eights inch to a one inch

01:54:15.810 --> 01:54:17.760
25 gauge needle, which is
about the smallest needle

01:54:17.760 --> 01:54:19.263
you can push the oil through,

01:54:20.169 --> 01:54:22.020
once to twice a week, depending on.

01:54:22.020 --> 01:54:23.490
And by the way, if
they're real needle-phobes

01:54:23.490 --> 01:54:26.280
we use Xyosted, which is a preloaded pen.

01:54:26.280 --> 01:54:29.820
- And are you having all men take HCG

01:54:29.820 --> 01:54:30.960
to maintain fertility

01:54:30.960 --> 01:54:32.241
and testicular size?
- Only if they want to.

01:54:32.241 --> 01:54:33.120
- Got it.

01:54:33.120 --> 01:54:36.240
- And by the way, we do not
like to use TRT in men who,

01:54:36.240 --> 01:54:38.190
we don't like to use
testosterone specifically in men

01:54:38.190 --> 01:54:39.930
who still want to maintain fertility.

01:54:39.930 --> 01:54:41.070
We just steer them away from that.

01:54:41.070 --> 01:54:42.570
- Because total sperm count goes down.

01:54:42.570 --> 01:54:43.530
- Yeah, we just say, why risk it.

01:54:43.530 --> 01:54:45.093
Like we'd rather use HCG.

01:54:46.041 --> 01:54:46.938
- [Andrew] Yeah, just on its own.

01:54:46.938 --> 01:54:48.570
- Yeah, just wait till
you're done reproducing.

01:54:48.570 --> 01:54:50.610
Bank sperm, wait till
you're done reproducing

01:54:50.610 --> 01:54:52.083
before we go to testosterone.

01:54:53.160 --> 01:54:54.455
- What are some of the benefits,

01:54:54.455 --> 01:54:57.690
and what are some of the cautionary notes

01:54:57.690 --> 01:55:00.150
with appropriate TRT,

01:55:00.150 --> 01:55:03.270
meaning the kind of contour
that we're talking about here.

01:55:03.270 --> 01:55:08.223
A lower dose with the yes
or no low estrogen control.

01:55:09.180 --> 01:55:13.440
People, what did generally
people report, how do they feel?

01:55:13.440 --> 01:55:14.710
What does it allow them to do

01:55:14.710 --> 01:55:16.740
that they couldn't do or feel before?

01:55:16.740 --> 01:55:19.630
And then in terms of what
are the markers to look for?

01:55:19.630 --> 01:55:24.060
Is it LDL, blood pressure,
water retention, acne,

01:55:24.060 --> 01:55:25.004
those kinds of things.

01:55:25.004 --> 01:55:26.045
Are there some other things as well?

01:55:26.045 --> 01:55:26.878
- Yeah, it depends on the doses, right?

01:55:26.878 --> 01:55:29.100
I mean, again, we're using
these in really low doses,

01:55:29.100 --> 01:55:31.200
so it's pretty rare
that we'd have a patient

01:55:31.200 --> 01:55:34.353
on more than a hundred milligrams
a week of testosterone.

01:55:35.640 --> 01:55:36.960
I think for comparison,

01:55:36.960 --> 01:55:40.350
like a bodybuilder could
easily take 500 to a thousand

01:55:40.350 --> 01:55:41.970
during a high growth phase.

01:55:41.970 --> 01:55:44.040
- I know some of these
guys, they go ballistic,

01:55:44.040 --> 01:55:47.460
or they're doing moderate levels
of testosterone cypionate,

01:55:47.460 --> 01:55:50.733
but they're also taking
Dianabols, Nandrolone,

01:55:51.930 --> 01:55:54.540
you know, SARMs, and a
bunch of other things.

01:55:54.540 --> 01:55:56.220
I mean, their stacks
are kind of ridiculous.

01:55:56.220 --> 01:55:58.410
I mean, no disrespect to that sport,

01:55:58.410 --> 01:56:00.960
but I mean, people are dying like crazy

01:56:00.960 --> 01:56:01.793
in that sport right now.

01:56:01.793 --> 01:56:02.850
- [Peter] It's outside of physiology.

01:56:02.850 --> 01:56:05.008
- Yeah, and I think for
99% of people listening,

01:56:05.008 --> 01:56:07.590
they look, they hear bodybuilder
and they just go, like,

01:56:07.590 --> 01:56:09.450
why would somebody do that anyway?

01:56:09.450 --> 01:56:10.283
- [Peter] Right.

01:56:10.283 --> 01:56:11.318
- I think that's the typical response.

01:56:11.318 --> 01:56:13.110
- So the point is, a lot of,

01:56:13.110 --> 01:56:15.450
but we owe those guys a
great deal of gratitude

01:56:15.450 --> 01:56:17.130
'cause they've shown us the boundaries.

01:56:17.130 --> 01:56:18.180
- Including the women.

01:56:18.180 --> 01:56:20.277
- That's right, yeah, yeah.

01:56:20.277 --> 01:56:24.630
And so, those bodybuilders
have taught us a lot about

01:56:24.630 --> 01:56:26.190
like what happens.

01:56:26.190 --> 01:56:31.190
And so yeah, the bloating,
the water retention, acne,

01:56:31.590 --> 01:56:35.070
hair loss, hair growth, all
of those things we understand.

01:56:35.070 --> 01:56:36.060
The truth of it is,

01:56:36.060 --> 01:56:38.997
we just don't see those
things in our patients.

01:56:38.997 --> 01:56:41.940
- But a hundred milligrams
per week is a very low output.

01:56:41.940 --> 01:56:43.826
My understanding-
- But it's a physiologic dose.

01:56:43.826 --> 01:56:44.970
I mean, the reality of it is
it's enough for most people.

01:56:44.970 --> 01:56:47.100
I mean there's, probably the
highest we've ever had to go

01:56:47.100 --> 01:56:49.530
is maybe 70 twice a week.

01:56:49.530 --> 01:56:52.830
- What's the youngest patient
you've ever had to put on TRT?

01:56:52.830 --> 01:56:54.480
- Actual testosterone?
- Mm hmm.

01:56:58.200 --> 01:56:59.820
- Probably, that's a good
question, I'm thinking about.

01:56:59.820 --> 01:57:01.173
Maybe 40.

01:57:02.190 --> 01:57:03.330
- I think that's great for people to hear,

01:57:03.330 --> 01:57:05.430
'cause I know that a lot of
guys in their 20s are thinking

01:57:05.430 --> 01:57:07.230
TRT is the way to go, and I would argue

01:57:07.230 --> 01:57:08.610
unless you're doing everything else right

01:57:08.610 --> 01:57:11.700
and you're still hypogonadal
and you're really struggling,

01:57:11.700 --> 01:57:12.690
put that time off.

01:57:12.690 --> 01:57:14.070
Because also the fertility issue

01:57:14.070 --> 01:57:14.940
you want to delay, delay, delay.

01:57:14.940 --> 01:57:17.160
- Well, again, it depends
if, when we say TRT,

01:57:17.160 --> 01:57:19.740
if you're in your 20s
and there's no other way,

01:57:19.740 --> 01:57:22.620
I would hope you would
be steered toward HCG

01:57:22.620 --> 01:57:24.360
to at least preserve testicular function.

01:57:24.360 --> 01:57:26.310
Now again, we don't actually know

01:57:26.310 --> 01:57:29.850
if after being on HCG for 10 years,

01:57:29.850 --> 01:57:31.530
your pituitary will still work.

01:57:31.530 --> 01:57:32.700
- Right, you won't be
able to make your own

01:57:32.700 --> 01:57:33.780
luteinizing hormone.
- Exactly.

01:57:33.780 --> 01:57:35.880
So, it might be the case that

01:57:35.880 --> 01:57:38.400
you're going to need something
upstream of that, like Clomid,

01:57:38.400 --> 01:57:41.250
to kick start it, and
then we're, but again,

01:57:41.250 --> 01:57:42.510
I don't want anybody
who's listening to this

01:57:42.510 --> 01:57:44.370
who's using Clomid for fertility,

01:57:44.370 --> 01:57:45.690
to think that there's
anything wrong with it.

01:57:45.690 --> 01:57:47.940
I was, my concern over this became like,

01:57:47.940 --> 01:57:50.670
if you're going to be on this
for 10 years, is it problematic?

01:57:50.670 --> 01:57:52.800
Not if you're using
this for a course of IVF

01:57:52.800 --> 01:57:54.120
or something like that.

01:57:54.120 --> 01:57:57.420
So, again, if we felt
that someone's pituitary

01:57:57.420 --> 01:57:59.370
was not working, I would be happy to put

01:57:59.370 --> 01:58:00.570
three months of Clomid on them

01:58:00.570 --> 01:58:02.850
to kind of try to see if
we could blast it back.

01:58:02.850 --> 01:58:05.640
- Do you have men cycle
on and off testosterone

01:58:05.640 --> 01:58:06.690
at these low dosages?

01:58:06.690 --> 01:58:08.520
Are they taking a month vacation from it

01:58:08.520 --> 01:58:09.540
every once in a while?
- Yeah, totally depends.

01:58:09.540 --> 01:58:11.760
You know, I was talking to
a patient yesterday where

01:58:11.760 --> 01:58:13.680
we're going to do, we just
decided to change his cycle,

01:58:13.680 --> 01:58:15.720
eight weeks on, then eight weeks on HCG,

01:58:15.720 --> 01:58:17.370
eight weeks on, then eight weeks on HCG.

01:58:17.370 --> 01:58:18.510
So that's going to be a cycle

01:58:18.510 --> 01:58:20.520
that maintains his testosterone level,

01:58:20.520 --> 01:58:21.660
but fluctuates between

01:58:21.660 --> 01:58:23.970
endogenous, exogenous,
endogenous, exogenous.

01:58:23.970 --> 01:58:27.660
Sometimes we'll just do
testosterone on, off, on, off.

01:58:27.660 --> 01:58:30.750
And there it's like, how much
can he replenish naturally,

01:58:30.750 --> 01:58:34.350
but understanding his T will
dip during those off cycles.

01:58:34.350 --> 01:58:37.230
- Seems to me there's
a tremendous incentive

01:58:37.230 --> 01:58:40.800
for somebody to develop a molecule

01:58:40.800 --> 01:58:43.470
that can directly target SHBG,

01:58:43.470 --> 01:58:46.410
besides Oxandrolone and Anavar, right?

01:58:46.410 --> 01:58:49.980
If one could just drop
SHBG just the tiniest bit,

01:58:49.980 --> 01:58:52.060
it seems like one could adjust the free T

01:58:53.310 --> 01:58:54.977
in a way that would be great.

01:58:54.977 --> 01:58:56.670
I don't know why that
molecule's so hard to target,

01:58:56.670 --> 01:58:57.750
but somebody ought to do it.

01:58:57.750 --> 01:58:58.972
The chemistry can't be
- Yeah, it's funny.

01:58:58.972 --> 01:59:00.690
- that hard.
- I talked with Patrick Arnold

01:59:00.690 --> 01:59:02.823
about this many, many years ago.

01:59:04.140 --> 01:59:06.870
I wish I could remember what his ID,

01:59:06.870 --> 01:59:09.999
he had a comment about this
that at the time made sense,

01:59:09.999 --> 01:59:10.832
and I don't remember what it was,

01:59:10.832 --> 01:59:13.203
'cause I had that thought too, like man.

01:59:14.400 --> 01:59:15.840
Especially for that subset of guys

01:59:15.840 --> 01:59:17.640
who have normal testosterone,

01:59:17.640 --> 01:59:19.347
but they're just over binding it.

01:59:19.347 --> 01:59:21.390
- I'm really glad that
you brought up this issue

01:59:21.390 --> 01:59:24.330
of total testosterone versus
free T, and the reason is,

01:59:24.330 --> 01:59:26.610
ever since going on podcasts
and talking about this stuff,

01:59:26.610 --> 01:59:28.560
and talking about it on this podcast,

01:59:28.560 --> 01:59:30.120
people will send me their numbers.

01:59:30.120 --> 01:59:31.410
They'll send me their charts

01:59:31.410 --> 01:59:33.060
and then they'll send
photos of themselves,

01:59:33.060 --> 01:59:34.890
and I can tell you,
while I'm not a clinician

01:59:34.890 --> 01:59:37.260
and I haven't done fancy statistics on it,

01:59:37.260 --> 01:59:39.000
there's very little correlation between

01:59:39.000 --> 01:59:42.030
someone's absolute testosterone
and how they appear.

01:59:42.030 --> 01:59:45.270
I mean, some of these guys
look, you know, really lean,

01:59:45.270 --> 01:59:46.680
really strong, and they'll say, oh,

01:59:46.680 --> 01:59:50.670
total testosterone is 550, 480, right.

01:59:50.670 --> 01:59:53.970
And then other people, you
know, testosterone is 860,

01:59:53.970 --> 01:59:56.132
but you look at them and you think, oh,

01:59:56.132 --> 01:59:57.627
they kind of have kind of
a dough-y look to them.

01:59:57.627 --> 02:00:00.330
And so it's got to be this
free testosterone thing,

02:00:00.330 --> 02:00:01.530
plus estrogen, et cetera.

02:00:01.530 --> 02:00:03.030
And so cosmetically-
- Well, but also training

02:00:03.030 --> 02:00:04.175
and nutrition too, right.
- Yeah, absolutely.

02:00:04.175 --> 02:00:05.541
- I mean, I just think,

02:00:05.541 --> 02:00:09.210
I think for all this
talk about testosterone,

02:00:09.210 --> 02:00:10.800
which I enjoy talking about and, you know,

02:00:10.800 --> 02:00:13.110
I enjoy talking about
the data on, you know,

02:00:13.110 --> 02:00:15.572
long-term health
consequences of testosterone,

02:00:15.572 --> 02:00:17.490
'cause it's another controversial topic,

02:00:17.490 --> 02:00:20.040
I also think people kind of
overstate its importance.

02:00:20.040 --> 02:00:20.873
- [Andrew] I agree.

02:00:20.873 --> 02:00:23.580
- And I think there's a
group of people who think

02:00:23.580 --> 02:00:24.960
if I could just fix my testosterone,

02:00:24.960 --> 02:00:25.890
everything will be better.

02:00:25.890 --> 02:00:29.670
And it's sort of like, no,
actually that's not true at all.

02:00:29.670 --> 02:00:32.640
Really, the only purpose in
my mind of fixing testosterone

02:00:32.640 --> 02:00:36.270
is to give you the
capacity to work harder.

02:00:36.270 --> 02:00:38.640
It's really going to help you recover more

02:00:38.640 --> 02:00:39.600
from your workouts.

02:00:39.600 --> 02:00:42.000
This should just give
you a greater ability

02:00:42.000 --> 02:00:45.030
to experience muscle protein synthesis.

02:00:45.030 --> 02:00:47.370
So, you know, if I just give
you a bunch of testosterone

02:00:47.370 --> 02:00:49.500
and you sit on the couch and
your nutrition doesn't change

02:00:49.500 --> 02:00:50.580
and you're not exercising anymore,

02:00:50.580 --> 02:00:53.580
you're not going to experience
any benefits of this thing.

02:00:53.580 --> 02:00:55.890
I mean, my testosterone level
has fluctuated quite a bit

02:00:55.890 --> 02:00:57.060
throughout my life.

02:00:57.060 --> 02:00:59.460
And when I think about as an adult,

02:00:59.460 --> 02:01:01.440
not sort of including when I was sort of

02:01:01.440 --> 02:01:03.990
a fanatical teenager, but as an adult,

02:01:03.990 --> 02:01:08.100
when was I at my absolute
most insane physique,

02:01:08.100 --> 02:01:12.153
like my best performance on
a DEXA scan, would've been,

02:01:14.220 --> 02:01:18.270
I was 38-years-old, by
DEXA I was 7% body fat.

02:01:18.270 --> 02:01:21.480
My fat free mass index was like 23.2,

02:01:21.480 --> 02:01:24.780
23.3 kilograms per meter squared.

02:01:24.780 --> 02:01:29.780
I mean, I was huge,
strong, and totally ripped.

02:01:30.360 --> 02:01:32.160
My testosterone was in the toilet.

02:01:32.160 --> 02:01:34.740
I was over-training like crazy.

02:01:34.740 --> 02:01:38.850
I was, you know, exercising
probably 26 hours a week,

02:01:38.850 --> 02:01:41.430
killing it in the gym,
swimming like a banshee,

02:01:41.430 --> 02:01:43.650
cycling like my life depended on it,

02:01:43.650 --> 02:01:47.580
grossly over-trained,
low T, but, you know,

02:01:47.580 --> 02:01:51.300
I mean physically looked like
twice the guy I am today.

02:01:51.300 --> 02:01:53.130
You know, today my T's
probably twice as high

02:01:53.130 --> 02:01:54.360
as it was then.

02:01:54.360 --> 02:01:57.150
So, you know, now you
could say, well, Peter,

02:01:57.150 --> 02:01:58.710
what if you took T back then?

02:01:58.710 --> 02:01:59.820
How much better could you have been?

02:01:59.820 --> 02:02:01.110
Sure.

02:02:01.110 --> 02:02:03.810
But, again, I think the take home is

02:02:03.810 --> 02:02:06.750
just giving somebody T
doesn't do much of anything.

02:02:06.750 --> 02:02:09.000
It probably helps on the
insulin resistance front,

02:02:09.000 --> 02:02:10.650
without any other thing.

02:02:10.650 --> 02:02:12.420
But to me that's a waste,

02:02:12.420 --> 02:02:15.300
like that's squandering the
gift that it is giving you,

02:02:15.300 --> 02:02:19.020
which is the ability to do
more work and, you know,

02:02:19.020 --> 02:02:22.140
capture the benefit of it
via muscle protein synthesis.

02:02:22.140 --> 02:02:23.820
- I agree, and I think that the

02:02:23.820 --> 02:02:25.830
psychological effect of testosterone,

02:02:25.830 --> 02:02:27.840
whether or not it's
exogenous or endogenous,

02:02:27.840 --> 02:02:30.630
is it makes effort feel good.

02:02:30.630 --> 02:02:31.920
- Yeah.
- At some level

02:02:31.920 --> 02:02:32.753
it really seems to do that.

02:02:32.753 --> 02:02:34.920
And Sapolsky tells me the main reason,

02:02:34.920 --> 02:02:37.560
or mechanistically the main
reason that it can do that

02:02:37.560 --> 02:02:41.070
is by adjusting levels of
activity in the amygdala.

02:02:41.070 --> 02:02:42.030
- Interesting.
- And so there's

02:02:42.030 --> 02:02:44.010
some interesting imaging there.

02:02:44.010 --> 02:02:47.490
I'd love to chat more about
the cholesterol pathway,

02:02:47.490 --> 02:02:49.530
and I know this is a
huge landscape as well,

02:02:49.530 --> 02:02:53.040
but I think we're doing a
good job of diving in deep,

02:02:53.040 --> 02:02:57.033
but not getting stuck in the
underlying currents at all.

02:02:58.680 --> 02:03:02.100
There's tremendous debate
about whether or not

02:03:02.100 --> 02:03:05.430
dietary cholesterol directly relates to,

02:03:05.430 --> 02:03:09.900
or does not relate to, serum
cholesterol, LDL and HDL.

02:03:09.900 --> 02:03:11.040
Here's my-
- Is there?

02:03:11.040 --> 02:03:13.383
- I think, well, let me put it this way.

02:03:14.610 --> 02:03:17.280
There are people that argue,
I'm certainly not arguing.

02:03:17.280 --> 02:03:19.290
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- There are people that argue

02:03:19.290 --> 02:03:24.290
that if one eats a ton of saturated fat,

02:03:24.660 --> 02:03:27.600
that LDL goes up and HDL goes down.

02:03:27.600 --> 02:03:29.700
- Oh, okay, but that's not
dietary cholesterol per se.

02:03:29.700 --> 02:03:31.380
- No, not dietary cholesterol per se, but,

02:03:31.380 --> 02:03:35.550
and then there are people
that argue that, you know,

02:03:35.550 --> 02:03:39.030
any increase in saturated fat
intake is going to be bad,

02:03:39.030 --> 02:03:40.830
that you already synthesize
enough cholesterol

02:03:40.830 --> 02:03:42.660
for hormone production, et cetera.

02:03:42.660 --> 02:03:44.250
I'd like to talk about this in terms of

02:03:44.250 --> 02:03:45.933
how one should read their charts.

02:03:46.860 --> 02:03:49.740
My LDL is in what I'm
told is healthy range.

02:03:49.740 --> 02:03:52.380
My HDL is in what I'm
told is healthy range.

02:03:52.380 --> 02:03:55.740
I do try and not overeat
things like butter, cheese,

02:03:55.740 --> 02:03:58.080
and red meat, but I do
eat some of those things

02:03:58.080 --> 02:03:59.460
and I feel pretty good.

02:03:59.460 --> 02:04:04.460
But most people are operating
under the assumption that

02:04:05.940 --> 02:04:07.500
eating saturated fat is bad,

02:04:07.500 --> 02:04:10.980
and you only do it insofar
as you want to taste it.

02:04:10.980 --> 02:04:13.170
And then of course there's
a small group of people

02:04:13.170 --> 02:04:15.600
that love to eat organs and meats,

02:04:15.600 --> 02:04:17.970
and really pack cholesterol,

02:04:17.970 --> 02:04:21.963
and would argue that it doesn't
matter if your LDL is 870,

02:04:22.803 --> 02:04:24.450
it's not going to impact your health.

02:04:24.450 --> 02:04:29.100
What's the reality around
LDL, HDL, dietary cholesterol,

02:04:29.100 --> 02:04:31.863
saturated fat, at least in your view?

02:04:33.420 --> 02:04:35.730
- So first let's differentiate
between cholesterol and fat,

02:04:35.730 --> 02:04:38.983
just for the listener,
'cause we use them, you know,

02:04:38.983 --> 02:04:40.110
I want to make sure people understand.

02:04:40.110 --> 02:04:43.650
So, cholesterol is a really
complicated molecule.

02:04:43.650 --> 02:04:45.633
So it's a ringed molecule.

02:04:46.860 --> 02:04:49.230
God, I used to know exactly
what its structure was,

02:04:49.230 --> 02:04:53.070
but like it could have 36
carbons, for all I remember.

02:04:53.070 --> 02:04:56.730
It is a lipid, so it is
a hydrophobic molecule

02:04:56.730 --> 02:05:00.393
that is synthesized by every
cell in the human body.

02:05:01.442 --> 02:05:04.050
It is so important that without it,

02:05:04.050 --> 02:05:07.770
if you look at sort of genetic conditions

02:05:07.770 --> 02:05:10.590
that impair cholesterol synthesis,

02:05:10.590 --> 02:05:14.310
depending on their severity,
they can be fatal in utero.

02:05:14.310 --> 02:05:16.950
So in other words, anything
that really interferes with

02:05:16.950 --> 02:05:19.503
our ability to produce cholesterol,

02:05:20.400 --> 02:05:23.010
is a threat to us as a species.

02:05:23.010 --> 02:05:26.430
And the reason for that
is cholesterol makes up

02:05:26.430 --> 02:05:29.460
the cell membrane of
every cell in our body.

02:05:29.460 --> 02:05:33.030
So, you know, as you know,
but maybe the listeners don't,

02:05:33.030 --> 02:05:35.220
even though a cell is a spherical thing,

02:05:35.220 --> 02:05:36.960
it has to be fluid, right?

02:05:36.960 --> 02:05:40.183
It's not just a rigid like
sphere, like a, you know,

02:05:40.183 --> 02:05:41.370
a blow up ball, right?

02:05:41.370 --> 02:05:44.970
It's got to be able to
kind of move in this way

02:05:44.970 --> 02:05:47.430
to mesh with other cells.

02:05:47.430 --> 02:05:50.670
It also has to accommodate
having porous structures

02:05:50.670 --> 02:05:52.680
that traverse its membrane

02:05:52.680 --> 02:05:56.760
to allow ions and things
like that to go across.

02:05:56.760 --> 02:05:59.430
And it's cholesterol
that gives the fluidity

02:05:59.430 --> 02:06:01.170
to that membrane.

02:06:01.170 --> 02:06:02.940
It's also, as you're alluding to,

02:06:02.940 --> 02:06:06.240
the backbone of some of
the most important hormones

02:06:06.240 --> 02:06:07.110
in our body.

02:06:07.110 --> 02:06:11.463
Estrogen, progesterone,
testosterone, cortisol.

02:06:12.510 --> 02:06:14.580
So we have this thing, super important.

02:06:14.580 --> 02:06:15.960
Okay.

02:06:15.960 --> 02:06:20.220
Then let's talk about, can you
get cholesterol in your diet?

02:06:20.220 --> 02:06:23.373
Yes, you can eat foods that
are rich in cholesterol.

02:06:24.810 --> 02:06:28.500
What was known in 1960,

02:06:28.500 --> 02:06:31.830
but somehow escaped
everybody's imagination

02:06:31.830 --> 02:06:33.780
until finally the
American Heart Association

02:06:33.780 --> 02:06:35.850
acknowledged this a few years ago,

02:06:35.850 --> 02:06:37.950
is that the cholesterol you eat

02:06:37.950 --> 02:06:40.230
does not really make it into your body.

02:06:40.230 --> 02:06:42.660
And the reason for that
is it's esterified.

02:06:42.660 --> 02:06:46.590
So we have, and not to get
too nerdy, but I think people,

02:06:46.590 --> 02:06:48.000
I really think it's
important people understand

02:06:48.000 --> 02:06:48.833
how this thing works.

02:06:48.833 --> 02:06:51.780
So we have cells in our
gut, and enterocytes,

02:06:51.780 --> 02:06:54.390
they're the endothelial cells of our gut.

02:06:54.390 --> 02:06:57.510
They have, each one of them has basically

02:06:57.510 --> 02:06:58.710
two transporters on them.

02:06:58.710 --> 02:06:59.880
So the first is called

02:06:59.880 --> 02:07:03.060
the Niemann-pick C1-like 1 transporter.

02:07:03.060 --> 02:07:08.060
The second is called the
ATP-binding cassette G5/G8.

02:07:08.128 --> 02:07:09.000
Okay.

02:07:09.000 --> 02:07:11.460
The Niemann-pick C1-like 1 transporter

02:07:11.460 --> 02:07:13.830
will bring in any sterol,

02:07:13.830 --> 02:07:16.770
cholesterol, zoosterol, phytosterol.

02:07:16.770 --> 02:07:19.473
Any sterol that fits through
the door will come in.

02:07:20.850 --> 02:07:25.260
Virtually all of that is
the cholesterol we produce

02:07:25.260 --> 02:07:27.480
that gets taken back to the liver,

02:07:27.480 --> 02:07:30.930
that the liver packages
in bile and secretes.

02:07:30.930 --> 02:07:32.790
So that's what aids in our digestion,

02:07:32.790 --> 02:07:34.440
which is another thing I
should've mentioned earlier.

02:07:34.440 --> 02:07:36.780
In addition to using cholesterol

02:07:36.780 --> 02:07:38.790
for cell membranes and hormones,

02:07:38.790 --> 02:07:41.190
we wouldn't be able to digest
our food without cholesterol,

02:07:41.190 --> 02:07:44.100
because it's what makes up the bile salts.

02:07:44.100 --> 02:07:47.760
So our own cholesterol is
basically recirculated in a pool

02:07:47.760 --> 02:07:48.870
throughout our body,

02:07:48.870 --> 02:07:51.570
and this is the way it
gets back into the body.

02:07:51.570 --> 02:07:54.720
It's through this Niemann-pick
C1-like 1 transporter.

02:07:54.720 --> 02:07:56.640
When it gets in there, the body,

02:07:56.640 --> 02:07:58.590
this is the checkpoint of regulation.

02:07:58.590 --> 02:07:59.520
This is where the body says,

02:07:59.520 --> 02:08:02.340
do you have enough cholesterol
in the body, yes or no?

02:08:02.340 --> 02:08:05.940
If yes, I will let that
cholesterol make its way

02:08:05.940 --> 02:08:07.380
into the circulation.

02:08:07.380 --> 02:08:10.080
So it'll go off the
basolateral side of the cell,

02:08:10.080 --> 02:08:12.090
not the luminal side, into the body.

02:08:12.090 --> 02:08:14.130
Alternatively, the body
says, you know what?

02:08:14.130 --> 02:08:16.110
We have enough cholesterol.

02:08:16.110 --> 02:08:17.830
I'm going to let you poop this out.

02:08:17.830 --> 02:08:20.040
And now the ATP-binding
cassette will shoot it out,

02:08:20.040 --> 02:08:22.740
it'll go back into the luminal
side, and away it goes.

02:08:25.050 --> 02:08:26.520
So all of the cholesterol in our body

02:08:26.520 --> 02:08:27.567
is not esterified.

02:08:27.567 --> 02:08:30.300
And it doesn't have that
big, bulky side chain

02:08:30.300 --> 02:08:31.830
attached to it.

02:08:31.830 --> 02:08:34.470
The cholesterol you eat is esterified,

02:08:34.470 --> 02:08:37.200
and an esterified cholesterol molecule

02:08:37.200 --> 02:08:39.090
simply can't physically pass through

02:08:39.090 --> 02:08:41.220
that Niemann-pick C1-like 1 transporter.

02:08:41.220 --> 02:08:46.220
Now, we probably manage to de-esterify

02:08:46.740 --> 02:08:50.250
10 to 15% of our dietary cholesterol.

02:08:50.250 --> 02:08:51.083
So in other words,

02:08:51.083 --> 02:08:53.490
there are small amounts
of dietary cholesterol

02:08:53.490 --> 02:08:57.150
that do make their way
into our circulation,

02:08:57.150 --> 02:08:59.760
but it represents a small fraction

02:08:59.760 --> 02:09:02.190
of our total body's pool of cholesterol.

02:09:02.190 --> 02:09:05.430
Again, this was known, even by Ancel Keys,

02:09:05.430 --> 02:09:08.580
the guy who turned fat
into the biggest boogeyman

02:09:08.580 --> 02:09:09.750
of all time.

02:09:09.750 --> 02:09:12.780
Ancel Keys acknowledged this in the 1960s.

02:09:12.780 --> 02:09:16.083
Dietary cholesterol plays no
role in serum cholesterol.

02:09:17.190 --> 02:09:19.110
Again, it took the
American Heart Association

02:09:19.110 --> 02:09:21.390
another 60 years to figure that out.

02:09:21.390 --> 02:09:23.550
But even now they acknowledge that.

02:09:23.550 --> 02:09:25.530
Dietary cholesterol has no bearing.

02:09:25.530 --> 02:09:30.030
- So why is it that it's
pretty easy to find studies,

02:09:30.030 --> 02:09:33.000
or at least people who
are highly credentialed

02:09:33.000 --> 02:09:36.930
from good institutions, claiming
that eating saturated fat-

02:09:36.930 --> 02:09:38.430
- [Peter] Saturated fat's different.

02:09:38.430 --> 02:09:39.660
- Saturated fat and red meat,

02:09:39.660 --> 02:09:44.660
things that are rich in
cholesterol, to be more specific,

02:09:44.790 --> 02:09:48.060
is bad for us in terms
of our eventual LDL.

02:09:48.060 --> 02:09:49.200
- So this is two different things.

02:09:49.200 --> 02:09:52.710
So saturated fat
consumption in many people

02:09:52.710 --> 02:09:54.933
will erase LDL cholesterol.

02:09:55.800 --> 02:09:57.990
So it's important to
differentiate between the,

02:09:57.990 --> 02:09:58.950
what is saturated fat?

02:09:58.950 --> 02:10:00.900
So saturated fat of
course is a fatty acid,

02:10:00.900 --> 02:10:01.980
just so people understand.

02:10:01.980 --> 02:10:03.630
Totally different
molecule from cholesterol.

02:10:03.630 --> 02:10:06.030
Cholesterol is this very
complicated ring structure,

02:10:06.030 --> 02:10:08.130
multiple rings stuck together.

02:10:08.130 --> 02:10:11.370
SFA, saturated fat, is just
a long chain fatty acid

02:10:11.370 --> 02:10:14.190
that is fully saturated,
meaning it has no double bonds,

02:10:14.190 --> 02:10:16.140
and it can exist in isolation.

02:10:16.140 --> 02:10:19.320
It can exist in a
triglyceride, triglycerides,

02:10:19.320 --> 02:10:21.420
or, you know, a phospho lipid,

02:10:21.420 --> 02:10:23.190
or all sorts of things like that.

02:10:23.190 --> 02:10:27.780
So when we eat foods that contain fat,

02:10:27.780 --> 02:10:30.360
basically there are three
distinctions for that fat.

02:10:30.360 --> 02:10:33.240
Is it saturated, is it
monounsaturated, one double bond,

02:10:33.240 --> 02:10:35.790
or is it polyunsaturated,
two or more double bonds?

02:10:37.260 --> 02:10:40.680
The observation that eating saturated fat

02:10:40.680 --> 02:10:43.983
raises cholesterol is generally correct.

02:10:45.000 --> 02:10:46.080
But again, now it makes,

02:10:46.080 --> 02:10:47.700
because if we're going to
start talking about LDL,

02:10:47.700 --> 02:10:49.170
we have to explain what LDL is.

02:10:49.170 --> 02:10:50.190
This is another one of those things

02:10:50.190 --> 02:10:53.073
that's just so grossly
misunderstood, that it's,

02:10:54.120 --> 02:10:57.693
it makes having discussions
about this very complicated.

02:10:58.650 --> 02:11:00.390
Let's go back to the
cholesterol problem, right?

02:11:00.390 --> 02:11:04.470
So every cell in our
body makes cholesterol,

02:11:04.470 --> 02:11:08.490
and almost without
exception, they make enough.

02:11:08.490 --> 02:11:11.340
There are a handful of times, however,

02:11:11.340 --> 02:11:15.003
when a cell needs to borrow
cholesterol from another cell.

02:11:16.440 --> 02:11:18.000
Okay, so how would you do this, right?

02:11:18.000 --> 02:11:20.880
So if you're sort of, if
you're playing God for a minute

02:11:20.880 --> 02:11:22.740
and you want to design a system,

02:11:22.740 --> 02:11:24.960
you have to be able to
transport cholesterol

02:11:24.960 --> 02:11:26.970
from one cell to another.

02:11:26.970 --> 02:11:30.120
The most logical place
you would transport this

02:11:30.120 --> 02:11:31.563
is through the circulation.

02:11:32.670 --> 02:11:35.190
And the problem with
circulation is it's water.

02:11:35.190 --> 02:11:36.840
Plasma is water.

02:11:36.840 --> 02:11:38.430
So now you have this problem, which is,

02:11:38.430 --> 02:11:43.290
I want to transport cargo
that is hydrophobic,

02:11:43.290 --> 02:11:45.150
in a hydrophilic medium.

02:11:45.150 --> 02:11:46.770
Can't do it.

02:11:46.770 --> 02:11:49.350
So if you think about all
the things that we transport

02:11:49.350 --> 02:11:53.790
in our blood, sodium,
electrolytes, glucose,

02:11:53.790 --> 02:11:56.490
things like that, they're water soluble.

02:11:56.490 --> 02:11:57.323
It's easy.

02:11:57.323 --> 02:11:58.710
They just move back and forth in our blood

02:11:58.710 --> 02:11:59.850
with no chaperone.

02:11:59.850 --> 02:12:01.950
But when you want to move cholesterol,

02:12:01.950 --> 02:12:05.048
you have to package it in
something that's hydrophilic.

02:12:05.048 --> 02:12:07.920
That something is called a lipoprotein.

02:12:07.920 --> 02:12:10.470
So we have these spherical molecules

02:12:10.470 --> 02:12:11.940
that are lipid on the inside,

02:12:11.940 --> 02:12:14.760
protein on the outside, lipoprotein,

02:12:14.760 --> 02:12:18.183
and inside they contain
cholesterol and triglycerides.

02:12:19.140 --> 02:12:20.550
So now you've got this spherical thing,

02:12:20.550 --> 02:12:23.130
triglyceride, cholesterol on the inside,

02:12:23.130 --> 02:12:26.520
and it's chaperoned by
a hydrophilic molecule

02:12:26.520 --> 02:12:30.360
that allows it to move
through our circulation.

02:12:30.360 --> 02:12:34.320
And those lipoproteins exist
in different densities.

02:12:34.320 --> 02:12:37.380
So if you run these out on
a gel electrophoresis plate,

02:12:37.380 --> 02:12:39.150
you'll identify different densities.

02:12:39.150 --> 02:12:40.980
The density is a function
of how much protein

02:12:40.980 --> 02:12:42.750
and how much lipid is in it.

02:12:42.750 --> 02:12:44.550
So the highest density of this

02:12:44.550 --> 02:12:47.010
is called a high density lipoprotein,

02:12:47.010 --> 02:12:49.020
and the lowest density of this is called

02:12:49.020 --> 02:12:52.590
a very low density lipoprotein, a VLDL.

02:12:52.590 --> 02:12:54.703
And then next to that you have an LDL,

02:12:54.703 --> 02:12:56.220
a low density lipoprotein,

02:12:56.220 --> 02:12:57.870
and then next to that you have an IDL,

02:12:57.870 --> 02:12:59.973
an intermediate density lipoprotein.

02:13:01.260 --> 02:13:06.260
So, you know, it actually goes
VLDL, IDL, LDL, but anyway.

02:13:07.380 --> 02:13:12.380
So when people say my LDL
is high, or my LDL is 100,

02:13:13.830 --> 02:13:15.420
what are they saying?

02:13:15.420 --> 02:13:17.790
They're saying the
cholesterol concentration

02:13:17.790 --> 02:13:21.510
of my LDL particles is a hundred
milligrams per deciliter.

02:13:21.510 --> 02:13:24.060
So the total cholesterol concentration

02:13:24.060 --> 02:13:26.490
you have in your circulation

02:13:26.490 --> 02:13:27.990
is that number that
says total cholesterol.

02:13:27.990 --> 02:13:30.570
So if someone's blood panel
says my total cholesterol's 200,

02:13:30.570 --> 02:13:31.920
it means that if you take

02:13:31.920 --> 02:13:34.890
all the lipoproteins in their
circulation, bust them open

02:13:34.890 --> 02:13:36.390
and measure the cholesterol content,

02:13:36.390 --> 02:13:38.640
it's 200 milligrams per deciliter.

02:13:38.640 --> 02:13:40.560
And for all intents and purposes,

02:13:40.560 --> 02:13:42.960
because the IDLs are so short-lived,

02:13:42.960 --> 02:13:45.360
that's basically the sum
of your LDL cholesterol,

02:13:45.360 --> 02:13:47.940
your VLDL cholesterol,
and your HDL cholesterol.

02:13:47.940 --> 02:13:51.600
Those three things sum to
your total cholesterol.

02:13:51.600 --> 02:13:53.730
- What about LDL little a
that you mentioned earlier?

02:13:53.730 --> 02:13:54.690
- [Peter] LP little a is another-

02:13:54.690 --> 02:13:55.523
- LP, excuse me.
- Yeah, yeah,

02:13:55.523 --> 02:13:56.490
he's another actor.

02:13:56.490 --> 02:14:00.390
He is a special type of LDL that, again,

02:14:00.390 --> 02:14:02.940
in sort of 10 to 20% of the population,

02:14:02.940 --> 02:14:04.590
is a really bad actor.

02:14:04.590 --> 02:14:09.180
So that's an LDL that has
another apolipoprotein on it

02:14:09.180 --> 02:14:11.580
called apolipoprotein little a.

02:14:11.580 --> 02:14:12.480
- [Andrew] Got it.

02:14:13.410 --> 02:14:14.550
- The other thing I'll just say on this,

02:14:14.550 --> 02:14:16.950
because earlier I mentioned ApoB.

02:14:16.950 --> 02:14:19.890
There are two broad
families of lipoproteins.

02:14:19.890 --> 02:14:21.927
There are those that are wrapped in ApoBs,

02:14:21.927 --> 02:14:24.207
and those that are wrapped in ApoAs.

02:14:24.207 --> 02:14:27.270
The ApoA family is the HDL family.

02:14:27.270 --> 02:14:31.860
The ApoB family is the
VLDL, IDL, LDL family.

02:14:31.860 --> 02:14:32.693
- I see.

02:14:32.693 --> 02:14:35.250
So for somebody who let's say
their total cholesterol is,

02:14:35.250 --> 02:14:37.860
let's just stay with 200 for simplicity.

02:14:37.860 --> 02:14:41.730
What do you like to see in
terms of the HDL LDL ratio?

02:14:41.730 --> 02:14:43.140
- Couldn't care less.

02:14:43.140 --> 02:14:45.420
I only care about ApoB.

02:14:45.420 --> 02:14:47.730
I only care about ApoB.

02:14:47.730 --> 02:14:50.610
I care about the causative
agent of atherosclerosis.

02:14:50.610 --> 02:14:54.600
ApoB is the thing that
drives atherosclerosis.

02:14:54.600 --> 02:14:58.170
- And what levels are
attractive or repulsive for you

02:14:58.170 --> 02:15:02.160
when you see levels of
ApoB that are blank,

02:15:02.160 --> 02:15:03.783
you get really concerned?

02:15:04.830 --> 02:15:06.990
- It depends on the person's objectives.

02:15:06.990 --> 02:15:10.560
So again, we take a very different view.

02:15:10.560 --> 02:15:11.463
I mean, we have.

02:15:12.300 --> 02:15:15.420
- Vitality now, and I want
to live to be a hundred.

02:15:15.420 --> 02:15:16.860
- Yeah, so if you see-
- And I'm assuming some taper.

02:15:16.860 --> 02:15:18.660
- If you tell me you want
to live to be a hundred,

02:15:18.660 --> 02:15:20.250
you're going to need to keep your ApoB

02:15:20.250 --> 02:15:22.410
below 30 milligrams per deciliter.

02:15:22.410 --> 02:15:26.820
- Let's say I want to live to
be a hundred, but I also, well,

02:15:26.820 --> 02:15:29.700
how about, I don't care how long I live,

02:15:29.700 --> 02:15:32.580
but I want to feel great while I live.

02:15:32.580 --> 02:15:33.870
- Again, it depends, right?

02:15:33.870 --> 02:15:35.400
Like anybody who's had a heart attack

02:15:35.400 --> 02:15:37.230
is going to be compromised
in their ability

02:15:37.230 --> 02:15:39.150
to feel well after.

02:15:39.150 --> 02:15:40.110
Right, so.

02:15:40.110 --> 02:15:42.300
- I guess, I say it that way,

02:15:42.300 --> 02:15:44.040
because if you're going to tell me that

02:15:44.040 --> 02:15:46.950
in order to achieve that
live to a hundred level,

02:15:46.950 --> 02:15:50.130
I'm going to have to
give up my personal life

02:15:50.130 --> 02:15:51.330
and my-

02:15:51.330 --> 02:15:53.070
- No no, to get-
- and my brain functioning,

02:15:53.070 --> 02:15:54.290
then I'm not really interested.

02:15:54.290 --> 02:15:57.900
- Sure, but to get LDL
levels, and really, again,

02:15:57.900 --> 02:16:00.240
people think of it as LDL,
it's really ApoB, right?

02:16:00.240 --> 02:16:04.017
ApoB is this total
concentration of LDL and VLDL.

02:16:04.017 --> 02:16:05.040
And that's what matters.

02:16:05.040 --> 02:16:06.900
Those are the big atherogenic particles.

02:16:06.900 --> 02:16:10.140
LDL also includes the Lp[a],

02:16:10.140 --> 02:16:11.610
although the concentration of Lp[a]

02:16:11.610 --> 02:16:13.680
is relatively speaking so small

02:16:13.680 --> 02:16:16.800
that it doesn't generally
show up as much in the ApoB.

02:16:16.800 --> 02:16:20.610
So we treat ApoB, and basically
what it comes down to is

02:16:20.610 --> 02:16:23.790
you want ApoB to be as
close to the level as it was

02:16:23.790 --> 02:16:24.900
when you were born.

02:16:24.900 --> 02:16:27.840
So we start developing heart
disease when we're born.

02:16:27.840 --> 02:16:29.910
That's just the way it is.

02:16:29.910 --> 02:16:32.880
The autopsy studies make
this abundantly clear.

02:16:32.880 --> 02:16:36.270
When you look at autopsies of young people

02:16:36.270 --> 02:16:37.560
who are dying in their 20s,

02:16:37.560 --> 02:16:40.740
and this was first done in the
1970s, it was again repeated.

02:16:40.740 --> 02:16:42.540
Again, it's always done
after we have a war, right?

02:16:42.540 --> 02:16:44.850
So in the 1970s, it was
done on people, you know,

02:16:44.850 --> 02:16:46.170
who died in Vietnam.

02:16:46.170 --> 02:16:48.990
In the early 2000s it was
done on mostly young men,

02:16:48.990 --> 02:16:50.460
but some young women, who were, you know,

02:16:50.460 --> 02:16:52.470
dying in Iraq and Afghanistan.

02:16:52.470 --> 02:16:54.600
And we saw without any ambiguity

02:16:54.600 --> 02:16:57.330
that cardiovascular disease is
already taking hold in people

02:16:57.330 --> 02:16:59.160
who are 18, 19, 20-years-old.

02:16:59.160 --> 02:17:00.120
- [Andrew] Wow!

02:17:00.120 --> 02:17:03.210
- And to be clear, they aren't
going to die of atherosclerosis

02:17:03.210 --> 02:17:04.043
at that age.

02:17:04.043 --> 02:17:06.120
They're still 40, 50 years away from it.

02:17:06.120 --> 02:17:09.300
But this is a lifelong disease.

02:17:09.300 --> 02:17:12.960
And we also know that the
disease can't really develop

02:17:12.960 --> 02:17:16.050
until ApoB reaches a certain threshold.

02:17:16.050 --> 02:17:17.580
And that's the threshold
that most of us get to

02:17:17.580 --> 02:17:20.010
by the time we're sort of in our teens.

02:17:20.010 --> 02:17:23.310
So it's this really young ApoB level

02:17:23.310 --> 02:17:26.490
of kind of 20 to 30
milligrams per deciliter,

02:17:26.490 --> 02:17:29.000
that makes it impossible
to get atherosclerosis.

02:17:29.000 --> 02:17:34.000
So ApoB is necessary, but not
sufficient to develop ASCVD.

02:17:34.260 --> 02:17:35.569
Now that, go ahead.

02:17:35.569 --> 02:17:36.776
- Oh, I'm sorry, I was just going to ask,

02:17:36.776 --> 02:17:37.650
what are some of the top

02:17:37.650 --> 02:17:42.270
behavioral nutritional
supplementation, if any, based,

02:17:42.270 --> 02:17:46.650
and prescription drug-based
ways to target ApoB?

02:17:46.650 --> 02:17:48.930
- Well, nutritionally, you basically have

02:17:48.930 --> 02:17:50.490
two big tools, right?

02:17:50.490 --> 02:17:52.800
And it depends on what's driving up ApoB.

02:17:52.800 --> 02:17:54.270
So ApoB, remember,

02:17:54.270 --> 02:17:58.260
is the concentration of
LDL and VLDL particles.

02:17:58.260 --> 02:18:00.510
And what do they carry?

02:18:00.510 --> 02:18:02.160
Cholesterol and triglycerides.

02:18:02.160 --> 02:18:05.340
So anything that reduces cholesterol

02:18:05.340 --> 02:18:09.840
and reduces triglycerides,
is going to reduce ApoB.

02:18:09.840 --> 02:18:13.710
Triglycerides are generally
driven by carbohydrate intake.

02:18:13.710 --> 02:18:15.544
So more insulin-resistance,

02:18:15.544 --> 02:18:19.020
more carbohydrate intake,
more triglycerides.

02:18:19.020 --> 02:18:21.300
So we, I mean clinically,

02:18:21.300 --> 02:18:23.880
this is readily apparent to
anyone who treats patients.

02:18:23.880 --> 02:18:25.380
If you restrict carbohydrates,

02:18:25.380 --> 02:18:27.090
you will reduce triglycerides.

02:18:27.090 --> 02:18:29.010
That just happens all day long.

02:18:29.010 --> 02:18:30.540
But if you reduce triglycerides

02:18:30.540 --> 02:18:35.154
by raising fat intake so
much, it can still raise ApoB.

02:18:35.154 --> 02:18:37.200
So you have to be able to think about it.

02:18:37.200 --> 02:18:41.700
So in an ideal world, it's
can you lower saturated fat,

02:18:41.700 --> 02:18:44.910
which tends to be the one
that is most driving ApoB,

02:18:44.910 --> 02:18:48.060
while lowering carbohydrate,
and then see what you can get.

02:18:48.060 --> 02:18:49.590
But here's the reality of it is,

02:18:49.590 --> 02:18:51.870
there's nobody with dietary intervention

02:18:51.870 --> 02:18:53.070
that's going to get to a level of

02:18:53.070 --> 02:18:54.630
30 milligrams per deciliter.

02:18:54.630 --> 02:18:55.800
I mean, I've never seen anything.

02:18:55.800 --> 02:18:57.420
- Pure dietary intervention.
- Yeah.

02:18:57.420 --> 02:18:58.253
- So what are the other things that-

02:18:58.253 --> 02:18:59.820
- [Peter] It's got to be
pharmacologic at this point.

02:18:59.820 --> 02:19:01.518
- Statin type interventions?

02:19:01.518 --> 02:19:03.060
- Well, now you have
multiple classes of drugs.

02:19:03.060 --> 02:19:05.160
So the tried and true is the statin.

02:19:05.160 --> 02:19:08.280
So statins work by inhibiting
cholesterol synthesis,

02:19:08.280 --> 02:19:11.010
and the net effect of that is that the,

02:19:11.010 --> 02:19:13.620
so the liver is really
sensitive to cholesterol levels.

02:19:13.620 --> 02:19:15.870
It doesn't want too much,
it doesn't want too little.

02:19:15.870 --> 02:19:18.090
When you inhibit cholesterol synthesis,

02:19:18.090 --> 02:19:20.790
the liver says, I want more cholesterol.

02:19:20.790 --> 02:19:23.670
So it puts more LDL
receptors on its surface,

02:19:23.670 --> 02:19:26.700
and it pulls the LDL out of circulation.

02:19:26.700 --> 02:19:30.783
That's what lowers the
LDL in the circulation.

02:19:32.280 --> 02:19:35.850
So, you know, again, nine
statins in use today,

02:19:35.850 --> 02:19:38.444
we typically use four of them.

02:19:38.444 --> 02:19:40.080
The side effect profile,

02:19:40.080 --> 02:19:42.750
contrary to kind of all the sort of

02:19:42.750 --> 02:19:45.823
statin-hating propaganda out
there, very benign, right?

02:19:45.823 --> 02:19:48.270
5% of people experience muscle soreness,

02:19:48.270 --> 02:19:50.193
which reverses upon cessation.

02:19:52.680 --> 02:19:54.630
- Cognitive effects?

02:19:54.630 --> 02:19:58.440
- Again, I think it's, in
terms of actual comparing it

02:19:58.440 --> 02:20:00.420
in a placebo, no effect whatsoever, right.

02:20:00.420 --> 02:20:02.340
So does that mean that
you put a patient on it,

02:20:02.340 --> 02:20:03.720
they won't complain of something?

02:20:03.720 --> 02:20:05.730
No, but if you look at clinical trials,

02:20:05.730 --> 02:20:07.410
there's no evidence whatsoever

02:20:07.410 --> 02:20:09.390
that statins impair cognition.

02:20:09.390 --> 02:20:11.250
There's also no evidence
in clinical trials

02:20:11.250 --> 02:20:13.230
that they accelerate the risk
of neurodegenerative disease.

02:20:13.230 --> 02:20:14.580
In fact, it's the opposite.

02:20:14.580 --> 02:20:18.540
Now we will, there's a very
nuanced case we make, Andrew,

02:20:18.540 --> 02:20:20.550
which is we'll look at patients with

02:20:20.550 --> 02:20:23.340
highly suppressed desmosterol levels.

02:20:23.340 --> 02:20:24.720
We will back off.

02:20:24.720 --> 02:20:28.380
We do want to maintain
desmosterol above a certain level,

02:20:28.380 --> 02:20:31.980
because of some evidence
that is still, I think,

02:20:31.980 --> 02:20:34.200
very preliminary, but
enough for us that we say,

02:20:34.200 --> 02:20:35.160
why take the chance?

02:20:35.160 --> 02:20:37.200
We have so many other
tools to lower cholesterol,

02:20:37.200 --> 02:20:39.330
why would we over-suppress synthesis

02:20:39.330 --> 02:20:40.863
in a susceptible individual?

02:20:42.300 --> 02:20:44.820
So the next tool you look at is

02:20:44.820 --> 02:20:47.160
a drug that blocks the absorption,

02:20:47.160 --> 02:20:49.080
or the reabsorption of cholesterol.

02:20:49.080 --> 02:20:51.600
Remember that Niemann-pick
C1-like 1 transporter?

02:20:51.600 --> 02:20:55.500
So that guy has a drug called ezetimibe

02:20:55.500 --> 02:20:57.303
that just mechanically blocks it.

02:20:58.170 --> 02:21:01.518
So in people, and that's
why I mentioned earlier,

02:21:01.518 --> 02:21:03.570
we measure all those sterols in people,

02:21:03.570 --> 02:21:06.106
so we also measure things
called phytosterols.

02:21:06.106 --> 02:21:07.440
And the phytosterols give us an indication

02:21:07.440 --> 02:21:09.330
of how active that transporter is.

02:21:09.330 --> 02:21:10.407
So the higher your phytosterols,

02:21:10.407 --> 02:21:13.590
the more likely you are
to respond to ezetimibe.

02:21:13.590 --> 02:21:15.120
Next class of drugs

02:21:15.120 --> 02:21:17.310
is a drug that blocks
cholesterol synthesis,

02:21:17.310 --> 02:21:18.300
but only in the liver.

02:21:18.300 --> 02:21:19.923
So the statin does it globally.

02:21:21.870 --> 02:21:23.430
This other drug called bempedoic acid

02:21:23.430 --> 02:21:24.600
does it only in the liver.

02:21:24.600 --> 02:21:26.730
So it has a very similar
mechanism to statins.

02:21:26.730 --> 02:21:27.663
Different enzyme.

02:21:28.500 --> 02:21:31.200
Not quite as potent, but
way fewer side effects.

02:21:31.200 --> 02:21:33.570
So any patient that's
having a response to statins

02:21:33.570 --> 02:21:35.940
that's adverse, we'll
try this other thing.

02:21:35.940 --> 02:21:37.359
- What's it called, one more time?

02:21:37.359 --> 02:21:38.247
- [Peter] Bempedoic acid.

02:21:38.247 --> 02:21:39.150
- Bempedoic acid.

02:21:39.150 --> 02:21:42.990
- The most potent drug of the
lot is the PCSK9 inhibitor.

02:21:42.990 --> 02:21:46.110
So PCSK9, it's a protein
that was discovered

02:21:46.110 --> 02:21:48.990
in the late '90s, I believe,

02:21:48.990 --> 02:21:52.593
is responsible for the
degradation of LDL receptors.

02:21:53.430 --> 02:21:56.760
This was first discovered in
people who had a condition

02:21:56.760 --> 02:21:59.430
called familial
hypercholesterolemia, or FH.

02:21:59.430 --> 02:22:01.680
So these are people that have
incredibly high cholesterol.

02:22:01.680 --> 02:22:05.580
Typically their total
cholesterol level is 300.

02:22:05.580 --> 02:22:08.220
Their LDL cholesterol
is typically, you know,

02:22:08.220 --> 02:22:10.143
north of 200 milligrams per deciliter.

02:22:11.070 --> 02:22:14.310
This is a disease that is
defined by the phenotype,

02:22:14.310 --> 02:22:15.143
not the genotype.

02:22:15.143 --> 02:22:17.100
So the phenotype has a
very clear definition,

02:22:17.100 --> 02:22:18.690
which I basically just gave you.

02:22:18.690 --> 02:22:21.630
The genotype is, there's a
million paths to get there.

02:22:21.630 --> 02:22:23.250
There's over 3000 mutations

02:22:23.250 --> 02:22:25.050
that are known to produce that phenotype.

02:22:25.050 --> 02:22:26.850
This was discovered to be one of them.

02:22:26.850 --> 02:22:29.843
In people who had hyper functioning PCSK9,

02:22:33.090 --> 02:22:35.460
this protein was just constantly hammering

02:22:35.460 --> 02:22:37.560
and destroying the LDL receptors.

02:22:37.560 --> 02:22:39.990
And so their LDL would be huge.

02:22:39.990 --> 02:22:41.790
And by extension, their
total cholesterol would be.

02:22:41.790 --> 02:22:45.330
So in 19, no sorry, in 2006,

02:22:45.330 --> 02:22:47.640
Helen Hobbs and colleagues discovered

02:22:47.640 --> 02:22:49.830
an opposite group of population.

02:22:49.830 --> 02:22:52.470
People who had LDL cholesterol naturally

02:22:52.470 --> 02:22:55.050
of 10 to 20 milligrams per deciliter,

02:22:55.050 --> 02:22:58.050
which would be an ApoB of about
20 milligrams per deciliter,

02:22:59.820 --> 02:23:00.870
and who never got heart disease.

02:23:00.870 --> 02:23:01.920
They were immune to heart disease,

02:23:01.920 --> 02:23:03.180
no matter how long they live.

02:23:03.180 --> 02:23:07.080
And they had the opposite,
they had hypofunctioning PCSK9.

02:23:07.080 --> 02:23:08.322
And so that was 2006

02:23:08.322 --> 02:23:09.210
in the "New England Journal of Medicine."

02:23:09.210 --> 02:23:11.220
That basically got a whole
bunch of drug companies

02:23:11.220 --> 02:23:13.627
hot on the trail of
producing a drug to mimic it.

02:23:13.627 --> 02:23:15.780
So now we have these antibodies,

02:23:15.780 --> 02:23:17.790
and they're wildly effective.

02:23:17.790 --> 02:23:22.110
- What percentage of your patients over 45

02:23:22.110 --> 02:23:24.090
do you have on either a statin

02:23:24.090 --> 02:23:25.560
or on one of these
other classic compounds?

02:23:25.560 --> 02:23:29.461
- Well, often it's in
combinations, and I would say 80%.

02:23:29.461 --> 02:23:30.348
- [Andrew] Eight zero?

02:23:30.348 --> 02:23:31.740
- Well you have to remember
what our objective is.

02:23:31.740 --> 02:23:34.620
Like, we're in the business
of trying to make sure

02:23:34.620 --> 02:23:36.750
people live as long as possible.

02:23:36.750 --> 02:23:39.387
And you have to take a sort
of world view of this, right?

02:23:39.387 --> 02:23:41.370
If you, like what's the most prevalent

02:23:41.370 --> 02:23:42.750
cause of death globally?

02:23:42.750 --> 02:23:43.890
- [Andrew] Say cardiovascular disease.

02:23:43.890 --> 02:23:45.660
- Yeah, and like, how close is it?

02:23:45.660 --> 02:23:48.030
So the last year before COVID,

02:23:48.030 --> 02:23:49.800
COVID kind of messes up
these numbers a little bit,

02:23:49.800 --> 02:23:51.633
but if you go to 2019,

02:23:52.470 --> 02:23:56.760
18.6 million people died of heart disease.

02:23:56.760 --> 02:23:59.283
Number two, cancer, 10 million.

02:24:00.120 --> 02:24:05.040
Like nothing's in the zip
code of atherosclerosis.

02:24:05.040 --> 02:24:06.901
And if you remember what I just said,

02:24:06.901 --> 02:24:09.720
if you took everybody in their 20s

02:24:09.720 --> 02:24:14.100
and reduced them to a
level of that of a child,

02:24:14.100 --> 02:24:16.533
you'd make ASCVD an orphan disease.

02:24:17.970 --> 02:24:19.290
- So.
- So the question is

02:24:19.290 --> 02:24:20.123
can you do that?
- Why don't we hear more,

02:24:20.123 --> 02:24:21.300
why don't we hear more about this?

02:24:21.300 --> 02:24:22.500
I realize there's some nuance.

02:24:22.500 --> 02:24:25.410
It's not straightforward,
it's not as simple as saying,

02:24:25.410 --> 02:24:28.800
eat less cheese, red
meat and watch your LDL,

02:24:28.800 --> 02:24:29.633
get on a statin.

02:24:29.633 --> 02:24:33.630
But why do we hear so little about ApoB,

02:24:33.630 --> 02:24:35.100
in the general discussion?

02:24:35.100 --> 02:24:38.643
Social media is such a
skewed landscape, as we know.

02:24:39.630 --> 02:24:44.110
People shouting into
tunnels of varying clarity.

02:24:45.240 --> 02:24:48.660
Some are beautiful bronze tunnels

02:24:48.660 --> 02:24:51.243
with clean walls and others
are sewer lines, right.

02:24:52.350 --> 02:24:53.500
And they all converge in the same place.

02:24:53.500 --> 02:24:55.800
- Yeah.
- Right, as we know.

02:24:55.800 --> 02:24:57.480
But why do we hear so little about this?

02:24:57.480 --> 02:24:59.490
I mean, I'm not on a statin,

02:24:59.490 --> 02:25:00.660
but now I'm beginning to think that

02:25:00.660 --> 02:25:02.370
maybe that might be a
good idea to consider

02:25:02.370 --> 02:25:03.990
one of these other compounds.

02:25:03.990 --> 02:25:06.630
I don't know the last time I
looked at my ApoB specifically,

02:25:06.630 --> 02:25:07.830
I'm guessing my physician did.

02:25:07.830 --> 02:25:10.683
But why don't we hear more about this?

02:25:10.683 --> 02:25:12.960
This sounds so important.

02:25:12.960 --> 02:25:14.760
It sounds like the most
important conversation,

02:25:14.760 --> 02:25:16.260
'cause all the hormone stuff

02:25:16.260 --> 02:25:20.370
and all the stuff about
smoking, and head injuries,

02:25:20.370 --> 02:25:21.713
and ADHD and all the rest,

02:25:21.713 --> 02:25:24.450
I mean, is irrelevant
if you're dead, right?

02:25:24.450 --> 02:25:25.500
- Yeah.

02:25:25.500 --> 02:25:27.570
It's a great question, I don't
think I have a great insight

02:25:27.570 --> 02:25:30.543
as to why this isn't
more front and center.

02:25:32.220 --> 02:25:35.640
I think the bigger problem is
why don't we even understand

02:25:35.640 --> 02:25:36.480
how to think about it?

02:25:36.480 --> 02:25:39.300
I mean the, and there's a
whole chapter in my book

02:25:39.300 --> 02:25:42.780
I'm working on that really
gets to this problem of,

02:25:42.780 --> 02:25:46.860
why aren't we looking at atherosclerosis

02:25:46.860 --> 02:25:49.410
in terms of treating the causative agent?

02:25:49.410 --> 02:25:53.040
Instead, we look at
modifying 10-year risk.

02:25:53.040 --> 02:25:54.900
So that's the fundamental
difference between what I call

02:25:54.900 --> 02:25:56.970
medicine 2.0 and medicine 3.0.

02:25:56.970 --> 02:25:58.380
Medicine 2.0, which is what we're

02:25:58.380 --> 02:26:00.120
generally practicing today,

02:26:00.120 --> 02:26:02.040
when it comes to ASCVD says, look,

02:26:02.040 --> 02:26:05.280
we will treat you, we will
lower that LDL cholesterol,

02:26:05.280 --> 02:26:06.570
they still don't talk about ApoB,

02:26:06.570 --> 02:26:08.310
but that's a very American thing.

02:26:08.310 --> 02:26:09.750
If you go outside of the United States,

02:26:09.750 --> 02:26:11.010
everybody's talking about ApoB,

02:26:11.010 --> 02:26:12.780
it's in the guidelines
in Europe and Canada,

02:26:12.780 --> 02:26:13.613
and everywhere else.

02:26:13.613 --> 02:26:15.660
The United States is
very stubborn on this,

02:26:15.660 --> 02:26:18.030
and it's due to a couple of
really weird personalities

02:26:18.030 --> 02:26:19.113
in the lipid world.

02:26:23.321 --> 02:26:28.321
But the paradigm is, when
your 10-year risk reaches 5%,

02:26:29.520 --> 02:26:31.200
when there's a 5% chance
that you're going to have

02:26:31.200 --> 02:26:33.780
a heart attack, stroke, or
die in the next 10 years,

02:26:33.780 --> 02:26:35.130
now it's time to treat you.

02:26:36.270 --> 02:26:39.420
Medicine 3.0 says that's not
the way to think about it.

02:26:39.420 --> 02:26:41.730
You treat the causative agent.

02:26:41.730 --> 02:26:44.550
If there's a causative
agent, you treat it.

02:26:44.550 --> 02:26:46.980
If blood pressure raises
the risk of heart disease,

02:26:46.980 --> 02:26:48.390
you lower blood pressure.

02:26:48.390 --> 02:26:51.340
If smoking raises the risk of
something, you treat smoking.

02:26:53.550 --> 02:26:57.180
And the reason that the
risk model is so bad

02:26:57.180 --> 02:26:58.530
when you're looking at 10-year risk,

02:26:58.530 --> 02:27:01.410
is age is the biggest driver of risk.

02:27:01.410 --> 02:27:02.570
I mean bar none, right?

02:27:02.570 --> 02:27:07.140
So if you take a 70-year-old
with perfect lipids

02:27:07.140 --> 02:27:10.050
and perfect blood pressure
and perfect everything,

02:27:10.050 --> 02:27:12.840
their 10-year risk of ASCVD

02:27:12.840 --> 02:27:16.500
is probably four to five times higher

02:27:16.500 --> 02:27:19.110
than the most unhealthy 30-year-old.

02:27:19.110 --> 02:27:19.980
It's not even close.

02:27:19.980 --> 02:27:21.157
- It's a lot like eye disease.

02:27:21.157 --> 02:27:22.797
You know, there are exceptions, of course,

02:27:22.797 --> 02:27:24.660
but we always say that
the biggest risk factor

02:27:24.660 --> 02:27:26.790
for going blind from glaucoma

02:27:26.790 --> 02:27:30.630
is being an older person, frankly.

02:27:30.630 --> 02:27:33.000
- So if you could identify
what the risk factors are

02:27:33.000 --> 02:27:36.570
for glaucoma, imagine if the paradigm was,

02:27:36.570 --> 02:27:37.740
we're only going to treat it

02:27:37.740 --> 02:27:40.350
when your risk of blindness reaches 5%,

02:27:40.350 --> 02:27:42.930
which isn't triggered until
you're old enough anyway.

02:27:42.930 --> 02:27:44.150
Wouldn't you rather know
that when you're 30?

02:27:44.150 --> 02:27:45.870
- Absolutely.
- And say, wait,

02:27:45.870 --> 02:27:49.080
if maybe being in the sun
without sunglasses, or, you know,

02:27:49.080 --> 02:27:51.570
using this type of eye drop
or something like that,

02:27:51.570 --> 02:27:54.000
has a negative impact, I
would rather know that sooner.

02:27:54.000 --> 02:27:56.610
So that's the fundamental difference.

02:27:56.610 --> 02:28:01.610
It's a philosophical difference,
with respect to prevention.

02:28:01.830 --> 02:28:06.150
And I will acknowledge that
in one element of prevention,

02:28:06.150 --> 02:28:08.190
I make no consideration.

02:28:08.190 --> 02:28:11.040
I am only coming at this through
the lens of the individual.

02:28:11.040 --> 02:28:13.980
I am never coming at this
through the lens of society.

02:28:13.980 --> 02:28:16.200
That makes my life easier,

02:28:16.200 --> 02:28:17.670
and it makes the problem
I'm solving easier.

02:28:17.670 --> 02:28:19.020
I don't have to answer

02:28:19.020 --> 02:28:21.240
the quality adjusted life year problem.

02:28:21.240 --> 02:28:23.280
I don't have to ask the question,

02:28:23.280 --> 02:28:26.700
is it economical to treat people at 30?

02:28:26.700 --> 02:28:27.900
I don't know the answer to that question.

02:28:27.900 --> 02:28:29.520
But I also know that when
you're trying to solve

02:28:29.520 --> 02:28:30.510
really complicated problems,

02:28:30.510 --> 02:28:31.950
the more you can simplify the better.

02:28:31.950 --> 02:28:34.860
So I've just acknowledged
openly, not solving that,

02:28:34.860 --> 02:28:36.780
if you want to criticize
me for it, that's fine.

02:28:36.780 --> 02:28:38.250
Let's be transparent.

02:28:38.250 --> 02:28:41.040
But all I care about is the
person I'm sitting across from,

02:28:41.040 --> 02:28:43.800
and in that situation,
it's really their decision

02:28:43.800 --> 02:28:46.650
if they can justify the cost of treatment.

02:28:46.650 --> 02:28:50.310
- An esoteric question, and
then a less esoteric question.

02:28:50.310 --> 02:28:52.530
The esoteric question relates to something

02:28:52.530 --> 02:28:56.640
that I think is a little bit
niche, but not necessarily so,

02:28:56.640 --> 02:29:00.330
which is, peptides and stem cells and PRP.

02:29:00.330 --> 02:29:02.790
I don't want to go off on too
much of a tangent on rehab,

02:29:02.790 --> 02:29:04.290
but I know you've done a number of posts

02:29:04.290 --> 02:29:06.060
on social media recently that were,

02:29:06.060 --> 02:29:07.740
I have to just tell you
were really thoughtful,

02:29:07.740 --> 02:29:09.300
and I really appreciate
that you're willing to share

02:29:09.300 --> 02:29:12.300
your own tissue rehabilitation experience,

02:29:12.300 --> 02:29:13.133
and point people to that,

02:29:13.133 --> 02:29:16.050
because this is a landscape
that a lot of people are in

02:29:16.050 --> 02:29:17.760
and they don't know how to navigate it.

02:29:17.760 --> 02:29:20.310
And a mutual friend of
ours, not to be named,

02:29:20.310 --> 02:29:23.640
sent me a text and said, I'm
going to be talking to Attia,

02:29:23.640 --> 02:29:27.360
and what do you know about
studies on things like BPC-157,

02:29:27.360 --> 02:29:32.190
this gastric peptide that
anecdotally, again, anecdotally,

02:29:32.190 --> 02:29:35.100
people report getting injections
of this into the shoulder,

02:29:35.100 --> 02:29:38.130
knee, et cetera, and feeling
so much better, so much faster,

02:29:38.130 --> 02:29:42.420
but there really aren't good
studies, controlled studies.

02:29:42.420 --> 02:29:46.020
And you hear all the same
sorts of things about

02:29:46.020 --> 02:29:49.140
platelet-rich plasma, PRP,
which, if someone tells you

02:29:49.140 --> 02:29:51.330
there are a lot of stem
cells in them, they're lying.

02:29:51.330 --> 02:29:52.433
There are not a lot of stem cells in them.

02:29:52.433 --> 02:29:54.120
And you also hear about stem cells,

02:29:54.120 --> 02:29:56.460
which are not FDA
approved, at least in this,

02:29:56.460 --> 02:29:58.590
for most uses in this
country, but are certainly,

02:29:58.590 --> 02:30:00.960
people are flying down to
Colombia and getting injections.

02:30:00.960 --> 02:30:04.140
And what is your
understanding or experience

02:30:04.140 --> 02:30:06.960
with things like BPC-157, specifically,

02:30:06.960 --> 02:30:08.400
'cause peptides is a huge landscape.

02:30:08.400 --> 02:30:10.620
We should probably do a
whole episode on peptides.

02:30:10.620 --> 02:30:11.647
Things like PRP.

02:30:12.600 --> 02:30:14.550
PRP is now approved for, I mean,

02:30:14.550 --> 02:30:16.530
women are getting injections
of this into their ovaries

02:30:16.530 --> 02:30:19.410
to improve follicle count.

02:30:19.410 --> 02:30:20.280
We know this.

02:30:20.280 --> 02:30:21.540
People are getting injections of PRP

02:30:21.540 --> 02:30:24.300
into every tissue and organ and, hell,

02:30:24.300 --> 02:30:27.019
men are getting injected
into their penis, so I hear,

02:30:27.019 --> 02:30:30.483
for all sorts of reasons
that are unclear to me.

02:30:32.130 --> 02:30:35.820
What's the deal with PRP,
BPC-157, and stem cells?

02:30:35.820 --> 02:30:38.070
Do you ever see interesting effects?

02:30:38.070 --> 02:30:40.890
Are you curious about these compounds?

02:30:40.890 --> 02:30:43.500
Do you prescribe or direct
people towards these?

02:30:43.500 --> 02:30:44.590
The FDA approved ones of course.

02:30:44.590 --> 02:30:46.110
- Yeah, so short answer is,

02:30:46.110 --> 02:30:47.550
I'm definitely curious about them

02:30:47.550 --> 02:30:48.900
and I'd love to see the work done,

02:30:48.900 --> 02:30:51.930
but I also think this is
about as wild wild west

02:30:51.930 --> 02:30:53.040
as it gets.

02:30:53.040 --> 02:30:57.930
PRP less so, but certainly
stem cells and peptides.

02:30:57.930 --> 02:31:00.063
And, you know, I just think,

02:31:02.790 --> 02:31:04.830
if you're going to do something
without a clinical trial,

02:31:04.830 --> 02:31:06.540
you got to show up with
a lot more data, right?

02:31:06.540 --> 02:31:08.580
So let's use rapamycin
as an example, right?

02:31:08.580 --> 02:31:10.260
I'm a huge proponent of rapamycin.

02:31:10.260 --> 02:31:11.280
And you can say, well, Peter,

02:31:11.280 --> 02:31:14.220
how can you take or prescribe rapamycin

02:31:14.220 --> 02:31:15.810
for GRo protective effects,

02:31:15.810 --> 02:31:17.580
when we do not have a human clinical trial

02:31:17.580 --> 02:31:19.740
demonstrating that it lengthens life?

02:31:19.740 --> 02:31:24.740
And the answer is, because I
have 84 other pieces of data

02:31:24.750 --> 02:31:27.750
that all point in the same direction

02:31:27.750 --> 02:31:29.850
across every model organism

02:31:29.850 --> 02:31:31.983
going back more than a billion years.

02:31:32.970 --> 02:31:35.163
And that's really different from,

02:31:36.060 --> 02:31:39.034
Joey, Sammy, and Sally did this thing,

02:31:39.034 --> 02:31:40.680
and I think it works.

02:31:40.680 --> 02:31:43.080
And they just can't be compared.

02:31:43.080 --> 02:31:46.230
Now I have no idea if stem cells work.

02:31:46.230 --> 02:31:49.470
I have no idea if BPC-157 works.

02:31:49.470 --> 02:31:52.620
I have no idea, frankly,
if PRP even works,

02:31:52.620 --> 02:31:54.477
though it might seem to have some efficacy

02:31:54.477 --> 02:31:55.920
and some indications.

02:31:55.920 --> 02:31:59.340
For example, maybe when it
comes to early hair loss.

02:31:59.340 --> 02:32:03.663
Maybe when it comes to, you
know, certain joint issues.

02:32:05.130 --> 02:32:06.360
But the reality of it is like,

02:32:06.360 --> 02:32:09.000
I think we just have
to accept the fact that

02:32:09.000 --> 02:32:11.460
everything we do has an opportunity cost,

02:32:11.460 --> 02:32:15.030
and that opportunity cost
is sometimes financial.

02:32:15.030 --> 02:32:17.850
But I actually find a lot of times it's in

02:32:17.850 --> 02:32:20.880
time and effort and energy
that goes into something.

02:32:20.880 --> 02:32:23.520
Now, when I was, you know,

02:32:23.520 --> 02:32:24.990
waiting to get my shoulder surgery,

02:32:24.990 --> 02:32:27.349
this is an injury that
I've had forever, right?

02:32:27.349 --> 02:32:28.749
This is an injury, you know,

02:32:30.745 --> 02:32:32.310
this injury was actually probably

02:32:32.310 --> 02:32:34.170
the greatest source of discomfort I had

02:32:34.170 --> 02:32:36.833
swimming the Catalina Channel
the last time in 2009.

02:32:36.833 --> 02:32:39.243
So that tells you how
long I've had this injury.

02:32:40.440 --> 02:32:41.970
But, you know, I sort
of knew at some point,

02:32:41.970 --> 02:32:44.070
like, I'm going to have to have it fixed.

02:32:44.070 --> 02:32:46.320
And I sort of went down
this rabbit hole like, hey,

02:32:46.320 --> 02:32:48.540
is there anything I can do
to avoid having surgery?

02:32:48.540 --> 02:32:52.113
You know, would infusing a
million stem cells into it work?

02:32:53.040 --> 02:32:56.400
And in speaking with as many
orthopedic surgeons as I could,

02:32:56.400 --> 02:33:00.060
the answer was kind of unambiguously no.

02:33:00.060 --> 02:33:01.740
And by the way, it doesn't
mean you wouldn't feel better

02:33:01.740 --> 02:33:03.930
if I injected a bunch of stem
cells into your shoulder.

02:33:03.930 --> 02:33:05.850
There are a lot of reasons that
might make you feel better.

02:33:05.850 --> 02:33:07.890
Just like there are a bunch
of reasons you can feel better

02:33:07.890 --> 02:33:10.983
if somebody injects saline
directly into your joint.

02:33:12.030 --> 02:33:14.937
So the question is, is it going
to fix the underlying problem,

02:33:14.937 --> 02:33:18.510
and if so, will it do
so by what mechanism?

02:33:18.510 --> 02:33:21.240
So I'm pretty sure that if
you took a thousand people

02:33:21.240 --> 02:33:23.490
with my particular injury,

02:33:23.490 --> 02:33:25.050
and injected them with stem cells,

02:33:25.050 --> 02:33:27.780
it wouldn't do a thing, because
of the nature of my injury.

02:33:27.780 --> 02:33:29.793
I had a complete labral tear.

02:33:31.200 --> 02:33:33.360
Are there some injuries
that might benefit from it?

02:33:33.360 --> 02:33:34.740
Yeah, possible.

02:33:34.740 --> 02:33:37.380
So the question is, how
would you design the trial

02:33:37.380 --> 02:33:40.980
to narrow down your patient
population correctly,

02:33:40.980 --> 02:33:42.630
so that you might see a signal?

02:33:42.630 --> 02:33:45.360
'Cause the other risk of doing a trial is

02:33:45.360 --> 02:33:48.810
you have too much of a
heterogeneous patient population.

02:33:48.810 --> 02:33:51.000
You don't know what the
heck you're really doing,

02:33:51.000 --> 02:33:53.100
and you get meaningless results.

02:33:53.100 --> 02:33:55.620
You get a null result, when in
fact there's a small signal,

02:33:55.620 --> 02:33:58.350
but you were underpowered to
pick it up because, you know,

02:33:58.350 --> 02:34:00.690
you only had 10% of
your patient population

02:34:00.690 --> 02:34:03.150
that was the right patient
population to get that.

02:34:03.150 --> 02:34:05.670
So, you know, will we ever get there?

02:34:05.670 --> 02:34:06.503
I don't know,

02:34:06.503 --> 02:34:08.520
because I don't see what
the incentive is, right.

02:34:08.520 --> 02:34:11.310
You have people who are
making money hand over fist,

02:34:11.310 --> 02:34:16.230
doing procedures on the
basis of I'm not sure what,

02:34:16.230 --> 02:34:18.510
what would their
motivation or incentive be

02:34:18.510 --> 02:34:21.150
to sort of see this legitimized?

02:34:21.150 --> 02:34:22.560
You'd really have to be able to say,

02:34:22.560 --> 02:34:24.060
well, there really needs to be sort of

02:34:24.060 --> 02:34:25.833
a pharma angle to this.

02:34:27.420 --> 02:34:29.190
It's sort of one of the
wishes I had, right?

02:34:29.190 --> 02:34:31.920
Like if I was a billionaire,

02:34:31.920 --> 02:34:35.040
I feel like the way I would
probably waste all of my money

02:34:35.040 --> 02:34:37.230
would be running clinical trials on stuff

02:34:37.230 --> 02:34:38.310
nobody cared about.

02:34:38.310 --> 02:34:39.143
- Yeah.
- It would just be-

02:34:39.143 --> 02:34:41.670
- Likewise, I will join
you, because that would be,

02:34:42.660 --> 02:34:46.050
yesterday we recorded a sit
down with somebody from Caltech

02:34:46.050 --> 02:34:49.770
who works on aggression and rage,

02:34:49.770 --> 02:34:50.727
and other things related to that,

02:34:50.727 --> 02:34:54.630
and has identified peptides
that are approved the FDA

02:34:54.630 --> 02:34:57.300
for other reasons that
seem to adjust anxiety,

02:34:57.300 --> 02:35:00.300
might even adjust aggression
and pathologic aggression,

02:35:00.300 --> 02:35:02.730
and went off on to a
long description of why

02:35:02.730 --> 02:35:04.260
none of these drugs exist on the market

02:35:04.260 --> 02:35:05.700
for the treatment of psychiatric illness,

02:35:05.700 --> 02:35:07.953
and yet probably would work.

02:35:09.180 --> 02:35:12.420
And what's missing is a billionaire,

02:35:12.420 --> 02:35:14.430
or a billion dollar company,

02:35:14.430 --> 02:35:16.140
that is willing to invest in something

02:35:16.140 --> 02:35:17.760
that very likely will work,

02:35:17.760 --> 02:35:20.066
but the market value isn't quite there.

02:35:20.066 --> 02:35:22.317
Or it failed in a previous trial,

02:35:22.317 --> 02:35:25.230
and so no one wants to touch
it with a 10-foot pole.

02:35:25.230 --> 02:35:27.600
Hopefully someone listening
to this will be incentivized

02:35:27.600 --> 02:35:30.401
to provide this sort of a venue for that.

02:35:30.401 --> 02:35:32.501
The kind of work that we're talking about.

02:35:33.420 --> 02:35:34.470
I have to ask.

02:35:34.470 --> 02:35:37.860
- But I want to make one other
point, Andrew, which is, to me,

02:35:37.860 --> 02:35:41.550
the problem with a lot of
these things is it gets,

02:35:41.550 --> 02:35:43.350
it's a crutch.

02:35:43.350 --> 02:35:45.420
You know, it's sort of like
what we talked about with like,

02:35:45.420 --> 02:35:47.790
hey, just fix my T man, and
everything's going to be fine.

02:35:47.790 --> 02:35:50.370
And it's like, no, that's
just the beginning.

02:35:50.370 --> 02:35:52.380
You know, what I worry
about when I see people

02:35:52.380 --> 02:35:54.630
who are clamoring for this stuff is

02:35:54.630 --> 02:35:57.660
a lot of times they don't realize that,

02:35:57.660 --> 02:35:59.100
whether it's psychologically or otherwise,

02:35:59.100 --> 02:36:00.930
they sort of say, well, now
that I've had this thing done,

02:36:00.930 --> 02:36:03.120
I don't have to do the hard
work of the real rehab.

02:36:03.120 --> 02:36:04.080
I mean, if I've learned anything

02:36:04.080 --> 02:36:06.960
through my shoulder surgery,
and I'm now 3 1/2 months out.

02:36:06.960 --> 02:36:07.793
- How does it feel?

02:36:07.793 --> 02:36:08.626
- Amazing.

02:36:08.626 --> 02:36:10.560
I mean, look, I still
can't do a lot of stuff.

02:36:10.560 --> 02:36:12.060
It's going to be, you know, a while.

02:36:12.060 --> 02:36:13.860
I haven't even been
able to shoot a bow yet.

02:36:13.860 --> 02:36:17.820
And it'll probably be a
year before I'll go back to,

02:36:17.820 --> 02:36:19.613
you know, long dead hangs
and heavy deadlifts.

02:36:19.613 --> 02:36:21.240
I mean, I dunno, maybe nine months.

02:36:21.240 --> 02:36:23.310
But it's, you know, I'm not there yet.

02:36:23.310 --> 02:36:25.290
But what I learned through

02:36:25.290 --> 02:36:29.883
a really amazing prehab
and rehab process is,

02:36:30.810 --> 02:36:35.100
like you just got to do the
work, and it's freaking hard.

02:36:35.100 --> 02:36:38.783
Shoulders are the most tedious,
boring thing in the world.

02:36:38.783 --> 02:36:41.550
I mean, three days a week I am doing,

02:36:41.550 --> 02:36:46.550
four days a week I am doing one
hour of just dedicated stuff

02:36:46.620 --> 02:36:51.620
for this shoulder, that
is super uncomfortable,

02:36:51.870 --> 02:36:54.993
super boring, super frustrating.

02:36:56.430 --> 02:36:59.520
But, I mean, I have faith
in the methodology, right.

02:36:59.520 --> 02:37:01.080
And I think a lot of people are saying,

02:37:01.080 --> 02:37:02.157
just shoot the stem cells into me

02:37:02.157 --> 02:37:03.750
and I don't have to do any of that stuff.

02:37:03.750 --> 02:37:05.130
And the reality of it is,

02:37:05.130 --> 02:37:08.073
I think that's a very
dangerous place to be.

02:37:08.910 --> 02:37:11.850
- Have you ever tried BPC-157?

02:37:11.850 --> 02:37:14.160
- Yeah, we tried it.

02:37:14.160 --> 02:37:16.920
We had, you know, again,
maybe seven, eight years ago,

02:37:16.920 --> 02:37:18.660
we had a bunch of patients ask about it,

02:37:18.660 --> 02:37:21.210
so, you know, my view is, okay,

02:37:21.210 --> 02:37:23.610
I was pretty convinced that
there was no safety downside

02:37:23.610 --> 02:37:24.480
to it so I was like, well,

02:37:24.480 --> 02:37:25.710
I wouldn't prescribe it to a patient

02:37:25.710 --> 02:37:26.910
unless I tried it myself.

02:37:26.910 --> 02:37:29.910
So me and another doc
in the practice, Ralph,

02:37:29.910 --> 02:37:32.310
we did it for, I don't
know, a couple months.

02:37:32.310 --> 02:37:34.320
I didn't notice a single thing.

02:37:34.320 --> 02:37:35.490
- Interesting.

02:37:35.490 --> 02:37:36.990
Well, thank you for that.

02:37:36.990 --> 02:37:39.123
Shifting to a less esoteric, but,

02:37:40.140 --> 02:37:43.530
and I think probably more
important topic overall.

02:37:43.530 --> 02:37:45.081
Metabolomics.

02:37:45.081 --> 02:37:48.249
We're talking about this
before we sat down to record.

02:37:48.249 --> 02:37:50.013
What is, what are metabolomics?

02:37:50.910 --> 02:37:52.920
Why should we be thinking about them?

02:37:52.920 --> 02:37:55.980
I have some idea of
what it might be about,

02:37:55.980 --> 02:37:58.628
but most people I think
are not thinking about

02:37:58.628 --> 02:38:01.110
metabolomics at all.

02:38:01.110 --> 02:38:03.660
And for those that are, I'm
sure they could learn more.

02:38:03.660 --> 02:38:04.493
So tell us about metabolomics

02:38:04.493 --> 02:38:06.617
and what you'd like to see more of

02:38:06.617 --> 02:38:08.550
in the world of metabolomics.

02:38:08.550 --> 02:38:10.470
- Yeah, so omics is just
the term that we use

02:38:10.470 --> 02:38:12.510
to describe the study of something.

02:38:12.510 --> 02:38:15.270
So genomics right, is like
the broad study of genes,

02:38:15.270 --> 02:38:17.250
and, you know, proteomics,

02:38:17.250 --> 02:38:19.530
the broad study of proteins
and things like that.

02:38:19.530 --> 02:38:22.380
So metabolomics is just
study of metabolites,

02:38:22.380 --> 02:38:25.440
and metabolites, unlike a
lot of these other things,

02:38:25.440 --> 02:38:28.470
they're a relatively finite
number of these things,

02:38:28.470 --> 02:38:30.720
many of which are known, but
some of which are not known.

02:38:30.720 --> 02:38:32.580
So glucose is a metabolite.

02:38:32.580 --> 02:38:34.050
Acetyl-CoA is a metabolite.

02:38:34.050 --> 02:38:35.760
Lactate is a metabolite.

02:38:35.760 --> 02:38:40.760
And so the question is, what
do we know about these things

02:38:41.610 --> 02:38:42.443
and how they work?

02:38:42.443 --> 02:38:43.380
And more importantly,

02:38:43.380 --> 02:38:46.440
what do we know about
certain physiologic states,

02:38:46.440 --> 02:38:49.350
and the metabolomic profile
that results from them?

02:38:49.350 --> 02:38:54.350
So let's use two extreme
examples, like exercise.

02:38:54.540 --> 02:38:58.020
Everybody understands, the
data are unambiguously clear,

02:38:58.020 --> 02:39:00.090
exercise produces about

02:39:00.090 --> 02:39:03.720
the most favorable phenotype imaginable.

02:39:03.720 --> 02:39:06.480
So if you wanted to
take a genomics approach

02:39:06.480 --> 02:39:08.880
to understanding that, you might look at,

02:39:08.880 --> 02:39:11.938
is there a change in the
genome when you exercise?

02:39:11.938 --> 02:39:13.410
And the answer is probably not,

02:39:13.410 --> 02:39:17.070
but maybe if you looked at
the methylation patterns

02:39:17.070 --> 02:39:19.953
and epigenome, you could
look at epigenomic studies.

02:39:20.910 --> 02:39:22.710
But you might instead look at kind of

02:39:22.710 --> 02:39:24.593
the proteomic side of that.

02:39:24.593 --> 02:39:26.610
Like, what is gene expression doing?

02:39:26.610 --> 02:39:28.620
And there you would see a lot of changes.

02:39:28.620 --> 02:39:30.330
Well, what I don't think people
are really understanding,

02:39:30.330 --> 02:39:31.860
although there was a
very interesting paper

02:39:31.860 --> 02:39:35.130
that just came out two weeks ago

02:39:35.130 --> 02:39:39.990
that looks for novel
metabolites that are changing,

02:39:39.990 --> 02:39:44.520
is they're a huge signal
in a metabolomic profile

02:39:44.520 --> 02:39:47.070
that looks different in
the state of exercise

02:39:47.070 --> 02:39:48.360
versus non-exercise.

02:39:48.360 --> 02:39:51.390
And could that represent part of

02:39:51.390 --> 02:39:54.423
how exercise is transmitting
its benefit through the body?

02:39:55.950 --> 02:39:57.180
You know, people always talk about

02:39:57.180 --> 02:39:59.010
the holy grail of metabolomics would be

02:39:59.010 --> 02:40:02.100
can you find a pill to mimic exercise?

02:40:02.100 --> 02:40:03.120
And I think the answer to that question

02:40:03.120 --> 02:40:04.833
is going to be undoubtedly no.

02:40:06.120 --> 02:40:07.050
For a couple of reasons.

02:40:07.050 --> 02:40:09.390
One, even if you could mimic the

02:40:09.390 --> 02:40:12.090
longevity sort of lifespan parts of it,

02:40:12.090 --> 02:40:14.970
you could never mimic the
health span parts of it.

02:40:14.970 --> 02:40:16.530
But what if you could do both, right?

02:40:16.530 --> 02:40:20.100
What if there were small
molecules that can replicate

02:40:20.100 --> 02:40:22.080
some of the protective
benefits of exercise,

02:40:22.080 --> 02:40:23.970
and you could combine those with exercise?

02:40:23.970 --> 02:40:26.850
What if those could be treatments
for other disease states,

02:40:26.850 --> 02:40:28.650
like diabetes, things like that?

02:40:28.650 --> 02:40:30.570
So that's why I think
this field of metabolomics

02:40:30.570 --> 02:40:33.363
is relatively untapped,

02:40:34.290 --> 02:40:38.250
and I think potentially
the next sort of frontier.

02:40:38.250 --> 02:40:39.330
- Speaking of frontiers,

02:40:39.330 --> 02:40:44.280
I hear a lot nowadays about
GLP-1 and pharmacology,

02:40:44.280 --> 02:40:47.160
that prescription drugs
that mimic or increase

02:40:47.160 --> 02:40:52.160
GLP-1 directly, glucagon-like peptide.

02:40:52.710 --> 02:40:53.910
People are talking about this as

02:40:53.910 --> 02:40:55.740
the blockbuster obesity drug.

02:40:55.740 --> 02:40:57.600
I haven't heard this
much talk about a drug

02:40:57.600 --> 02:41:01.260
to adjust human body weight favorably,

02:41:01.260 --> 02:41:04.050
since the discussions of
fen-phen when I was in college,

02:41:04.050 --> 02:41:06.450
and then of course fen-phen
was pulled from the market

02:41:06.450 --> 02:41:08.370
because people were dying.

02:41:08.370 --> 02:41:10.050
Not left and right, but enough people died

02:41:10.050 --> 02:41:11.460
that they pulled it from the market.

02:41:11.460 --> 02:41:13.830
- Which, by the way, is
an interesting story.

02:41:13.830 --> 02:41:17.070
You know, it was the enantiomer
that they chose to use

02:41:17.070 --> 02:41:18.330
that was the wrong enantiomer.

02:41:18.330 --> 02:41:23.100
And what it resulted in was,
God, I think it was like.

02:41:23.100 --> 02:41:24.520
- Was it mitral valve prolapse?

02:41:24.520 --> 02:41:27.520
- It was an MVP, yeah, it was
something in the mitral valve.

02:41:28.650 --> 02:41:30.690
Yeah, I think the chordae
tendineae were rupturing

02:41:30.690 --> 02:41:32.430
in the mitral valve,

02:41:32.430 --> 02:41:34.950
and it was mostly young
women I think were getting,

02:41:34.950 --> 02:41:37.440
you know, horrible pulmonary
disease as a result of it.

02:41:37.440 --> 02:41:40.190
Probably pulmonary hypertension
or something like that.

02:41:41.430 --> 02:41:42.505
But there were, you know,

02:41:42.505 --> 02:41:43.950
there were two enantiomers of the drug,

02:41:43.950 --> 02:41:45.870
and had they just used the other one,

02:41:45.870 --> 02:41:47.250
this issue wouldn't have happened.

02:41:47.250 --> 02:41:49.620
And there was a stupid reason
why they made the choice

02:41:49.620 --> 02:41:51.270
to use the one they did.

02:41:51.270 --> 02:41:52.110
And it's one of those things where

02:41:52.110 --> 02:41:53.790
once you make the mistake,
you're never going back.

02:41:53.790 --> 02:41:55.560
It's not like that company
could say, okay, wait,

02:41:55.560 --> 02:41:56.393
we want to do over,

02:41:56.393 --> 02:41:57.930
but we're going to do it
with the right version.

02:41:57.930 --> 02:42:00.750
So it's a tragic outcome.

02:42:00.750 --> 02:42:02.040
But you're absolutely right.

02:42:02.040 --> 02:42:06.030
I think the GLP-1 agonists
have more efficacy, and,

02:42:07.110 --> 02:42:10.410
you know, for all intensity
and for everything we can see,

02:42:10.410 --> 02:42:11.990
certainly seem safer.

02:42:11.990 --> 02:42:13.204
- Are you excited about them?

02:42:13.204 --> 02:42:14.037
- Yeah, I am, yeah.

02:42:14.037 --> 02:42:15.660
I mean, I think we're
just seeing the kind of

02:42:15.660 --> 02:42:17.220
tip of the iceberg.

02:42:17.220 --> 02:42:18.510
They're not miracle drugs, right.

02:42:18.510 --> 02:42:21.386
They come with problems,
right, which is, you know,

02:42:21.386 --> 02:42:23.640
they're catabolic across the board.

02:42:23.640 --> 02:42:25.680
So patients are losing fat,

02:42:25.680 --> 02:42:27.570
but they're losing muscle as well.

02:42:27.570 --> 02:42:28.403
So, you know-

02:42:28.403 --> 02:42:32.331
- You just sent all the
gym jockeys running from

02:42:32.331 --> 02:42:34.770
[indistinct], that's all you have to say.

02:42:34.770 --> 02:42:37.140
All you have to say nowadays
about something is that

02:42:37.140 --> 02:42:40.050
it's going to drop
testosterone, lower fertility,

02:42:40.050 --> 02:42:43.710
change someone's skin, hair or
nails, and it's like people,

02:42:43.710 --> 02:42:46.140
it could extend life
to being 250-years-old

02:42:46.140 --> 02:42:47.850
and people are gone.

02:42:47.850 --> 02:42:48.747
Humans are humans.

02:42:48.747 --> 02:42:51.301
That's a neuroscience
and psychology issue,

02:42:51.301 --> 02:42:54.030
not a biology medicine issue.

02:42:54.030 --> 02:42:55.967
But I'm pleased to hear
that you're excited by them,

02:42:55.967 --> 02:42:58.080
'cause I hear a lot of excitement.

02:42:58.080 --> 02:43:01.350
I haven't heard anything
disastrous about them.

02:43:01.350 --> 02:43:03.863
- It takes a while to
get people up to dose.

02:43:03.863 --> 02:43:05.250
So if you're looking at semaglutide,

02:43:05.250 --> 02:43:09.660
the dose that was studied,
so did a one-year trial,

02:43:09.660 --> 02:43:11.790
or maybe it was a little
over that, maybe 60 weeks.

02:43:11.790 --> 02:43:15.060
But it took about 16
weeks to get the patients

02:43:15.060 --> 02:43:17.490
comfortably up to 2.4 milligrams weekly,

02:43:17.490 --> 02:43:20.160
which was the dose that
they ultimately stayed on.

02:43:20.160 --> 02:43:21.870
In our experience, when we use it,

02:43:21.870 --> 02:43:24.180
we don't even usually
go up to 2.4 milligrams.

02:43:24.180 --> 02:43:25.260
We can usually get enough benefit

02:43:25.260 --> 02:43:26.880
between one and two milligrams.

02:43:26.880 --> 02:43:29.010
And we usually move people
along a little bit quicker.

02:43:29.010 --> 02:43:30.990
But we've definitely had
our share of patients

02:43:30.990 --> 02:43:32.820
who can't tolerate it due to the nausea.

02:43:32.820 --> 02:43:33.690
- Interesting.

02:43:33.690 --> 02:43:35.820
- Which might be part of
how it's working, right,

02:43:35.820 --> 02:43:38.010
is the sort of suppression of appetite,

02:43:38.010 --> 02:43:41.280
which, if taken to an
extreme can produce nausea.

02:43:41.280 --> 02:43:42.113
- Interesting.

02:43:42.113 --> 02:43:44.130
- Yeah, I think most of
the effect of semaglutide

02:43:44.130 --> 02:43:45.780
is central, not peripheral.

02:43:45.780 --> 02:43:46.613
- Huh.

02:43:46.613 --> 02:43:50.520
So I don't know, I saw
one paper that GLP-1

02:43:52.230 --> 02:43:55.650
is acting both on cells in the periphery,

02:43:55.650 --> 02:43:57.750
to cause gut distention in some ways,

02:43:57.750 --> 02:43:59.670
or sort of make people feel full,

02:43:59.670 --> 02:44:03.390
through promotion of
literally mechanical receptors

02:44:03.390 --> 02:44:05.550
that make people feel as if
their stomach is distended,

02:44:05.550 --> 02:44:06.780
even though their stomach is empty.

02:44:06.780 --> 02:44:09.150
And then perhaps some
central hypothalamic effects.

02:44:09.150 --> 02:44:09.983
Is that what-

02:44:09.983 --> 02:44:11.400
- Yeah, I think it's doing,

02:44:11.400 --> 02:44:14.133
I would bet 80% of it's
in the hypothalamus.

02:44:15.000 --> 02:44:18.180
It is also improving insulin
sensitivity in the periphery,

02:44:18.180 --> 02:44:20.340
but I don't think that
that's accounting for

02:44:20.340 --> 02:44:22.080
much of its benefit.

02:44:22.080 --> 02:44:22.950
- Super interesting.

02:44:22.950 --> 02:44:26.010
- And there's next gen versions of these

02:44:26.010 --> 02:44:27.510
that seem to be more long-lasting.

02:44:27.510 --> 02:44:31.110
So right now, if you look
at coming off semaglutide,

02:44:31.110 --> 02:44:32.910
you're going to see a weight regain.

02:44:32.910 --> 02:44:34.590
So there's newer versions

02:44:34.590 --> 02:44:38.210
that seem to preserve the
weight loss, even off the drug.

02:44:38.210 --> 02:44:40.157
So it begs the ultimate
question, which is like,

02:44:40.157 --> 02:44:42.240
what's the total use case
for this going to be?

02:44:42.240 --> 02:44:44.580
Is this going to be a
drug you cycle on and off?

02:44:44.580 --> 02:44:45.413
Or is it going to be a drug

02:44:45.413 --> 02:44:47.580
that a person has to stay
on indefinitely, and if so,

02:44:47.580 --> 02:44:48.870
will they become tachyphylactic?

02:44:48.870 --> 02:44:51.530
Will they gain resistance to it?

02:44:51.530 --> 02:44:54.240
So it's still super early
days on these things.

02:44:54.240 --> 02:44:55.950
- My hope is that it
would be a little bit like

02:44:55.950 --> 02:44:56.783
the way that you describe

02:44:56.783 --> 02:44:58.290
testosterone and estrogen therapies,

02:44:58.290 --> 02:45:01.020
that it would allow people to
do more of the behavioral work

02:45:01.020 --> 02:45:04.410
that's absolutely required
for health span and lifespan.

02:45:04.410 --> 02:45:07.290
- Yep, and we've also seen,
on the flip side of that,

02:45:07.290 --> 02:45:11.730
you can cheat through semaglutide, right?

02:45:11.730 --> 02:45:13.950
You can drink a lot of calories,

02:45:13.950 --> 02:45:17.310
and sort of get around the drug.

02:45:17.310 --> 02:45:19.740
So, you know, for example, like, you know,

02:45:19.740 --> 02:45:21.780
we always encourage patients
who want to lose weight

02:45:21.780 --> 02:45:23.820
to really just eliminate alcohol.

02:45:23.820 --> 02:45:26.225
That's like, that's the
cheapest, easiest trick

02:45:26.225 --> 02:45:27.750
to lose weight.

02:45:27.750 --> 02:45:30.240
And so if you're still
drinking a lot of alcohol,

02:45:30.240 --> 02:45:32.070
which is incredibly caloric,

02:45:32.070 --> 02:45:34.533
and just drinking a lot of caloric stuff,

02:45:35.430 --> 02:45:37.620
we've seen that that's less,

02:45:37.620 --> 02:45:39.720
this is just anecdotal with our patients,

02:45:39.720 --> 02:45:41.400
but we've seen that that's,

02:45:41.400 --> 02:45:45.750
it's easier to get around the
benefits of the drug that way.

